/* Machine translations of Logik Pölitz (uncorrected OCR), in the manner of Kemp Smith: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['poelitz/kemp'] = {"poelitz-s01-001":["Logic.",[]],"poelitz-s01-002":["Everything happens according to rules, in the corporeal world as well as in the world of mankind. The exercise of our powers [Kräfte] likewise takes place according to certain rules, and we proceed in accordance with them although we are not conscious of them. We come to knowledge of them, however, through trials and through use. In this way we can learn much from ourselves by experiment. Gradually, through longer use of our powers, we find these rules becoming more familiar, and finally so much so that it costs effort to abstract them. For example, grammar is the form of a language in general. Yet men speak without having learnt grammar; that is, they speak according to rules of which they are not conscious. The understanding also acts according to rules, and these we can investigate. It is the faculty of thinking, and we can therefore think only according to certain rules. The rules themselves we can again think without investigating them, that is, *in abstracto* [reading doubtful]. To think is, properly speaking, nothing but to bring the representations of the senses under rules. Hence the understanding is also defined as the faculty of rules. A man often gapes at objects without thinking; that is, he does not bring his representations under rules. The understanding is therefore entitled also the faculty of bringing the representations of the senses under rules. Since, then, our understanding always acts according to rules, it is well worth our while to concern ourselves with knowledge of them, even though we have already made use of our understanding beforehand. The understanding is the faculty of rules (just as the senses are the faculty of intuitions), and it is eager to seek rules and is satisfied when it has found them. Since it is the source of rules, the question arises according to what rules the understanding itself proceeds. On this the following.",["Restated 'Der Verstand handelt ... wir können also nicht denken als nach gewissen Regeln' as separate sentences; the dashes of the transcript are replaced by sentence breaks.","'die Regeln selbst können wir wieder selbst denken ohne ihre Untersuchung d. i. in abstracto' is probably corrupt or a slip in the transcript (one expects 'in concreto' or a different contrast); translated as it stands.","'gaft' (= gafft) rendered 'gapes'; 'Kräfte' rendered 'powers' to keep it distinct from Vermögen ('faculty').","OCR 'Vorstelhingen' silently corrected to Vorstellungen; 'heißt' rendered 'is entitled'.","The passage breaks off ('Von diesem folgendes'); not completed."]],"poelitz-s01-003":["All rules are either necessary, that is, rules without which no correct use of the understanding is possible, or contingent, that is, rules without which no determinate use of the understanding takes place. For example, the rules for the use of the understanding in mathematics, metaphysics, morals and the like are contingent rules, whereas those without which we could not think at all are necessary. The contingent rules, which depend upon a determinate object of explanation, are as manifold as the objects. But since in all thinking there is an understanding which acts according to rules, there must be rules of thinking common to all thinking without distinction of objects, which underlie every use of the understanding and without which it is not possible at all. These are the necessary rules, and they must therefore contain merely the form of thinking.",["OCR 'not h wendig' and 'bestirnter' silently corrected to nothwendig and bestimmter ('determinate'); 'Object' rendered 'object'.","Divided the long final sentence into three; 'Sie müßen also' is taken to refer to the necessary rules."]],"poelitz-s01-004":["In all our thinking there are two elements [Stücke]: matter and form.",[]],"poelitz-s01-005":["Some modes of knowledge differ in respect of matter but are one in respect of form. For example, physics and psychology are both empirical. A science that contains the universal rules is accordingly merely a science of the form of knowing by our understanding, or of thinking, in which we abstract from all objects; and such a science we entitle logic. The word λόγος, from which the word [logic] is derived, has various meanings, but here it means understanding and reason. Logic will therefore be a science of the form of understanding and reason in general. General logic must abstract from all objects. For example, concepts are present in all knowledge; logic shows only their form, without concerning itself with the matter. We have thus the outline of a science which contains the rules of the form of the understanding without distinction of objects. Such a science extends to all thinking and is a foundation for other sciences. A science which prepares the way for others is entitled a propaedeutic; logic is therefore the propaedeutic to the use of our understanding. The sum of the rules *in concreto*, which we do not know *in abstracto*, is supposed to be natural logic; artificial logic sets out these rules in detail. This division of logic into natural and artificial is not a good one. Logic ought to be a science, and a science is a sum of rules *in abstracto*, and so presupposes artificial knowledge. We must distinguish the logic of the common understanding from the logic of the speculative understanding. Understanding in general is the faculty of rules, and the rules we can take either *in concreto* or *in abstracto*. The common understanding, *intellectus communis*, is the faculty of rules *in concreto*. The common understanding, in so far as it is correct, is entitled sound understanding. It is to be distinguished from the *vulgaris*: the *vulgaris* is the understanding which every man has, but the *communis* is that which can be demanded of everyone, namely that he judge correctly. The speculative understanding is the faculty of rules *in abstracto*. Only a scholar has this, and it cannot be demanded of anyone who does not apply himself to the sciences. The rules of logic are twofold:",["The OCR 'Aoyos' is read as λόγος; 'Propädevtik' as Propädeutik ('propaedeutic'). 'komt' = kommt.","'Der Inbegrif der Regeln in concreto, die wir in abstracto nicht kennen' is rendered literally; Kant's relative clause is ambiguous but the sense is the rules as exercised though not known in abstraction.","'[logic]' is supplied: 'das Wort, von dem das Wort herkommt' is elliptical; Erkenntniß (singular) rendered 'knowledge', the plural 'Erkenntniße' as 'modes of knowledge'.","The long passage has been divided into shorter sentences; 'heißt/nennen' rendered 'is entitled/entitle'.","The passage breaks off at 'Die Regeln der Logik sind 2fach'; continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s01-006":["1.) how the understanding thinks, and",["Breaks off; the second member follows in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s01-007":["2.) how it ought to think; and this second is what logic teaches us. Logic therefore teaches the correct use of the understanding, that is, the use of the understanding which agrees with itself. — A correct understanding is one that in its use agrees throughout with itself; the rules which it then follows are correct. If the understanding is often in conflict with itself in its use, the rule is false; that is the sole criterion *a priori*. The subjective rules would be the laws in accordance with which we [actually] make use of our understanding. The objective rules are those in accordance with which we ought to make use of our understanding. In logic, when we abstract from content, objective and subjective rules are one and the same. Everything empirical must be left out of logic, and nothing must enter it save what can be known *a priori*. It may nevertheless be retained by way of explanation. Were we to leave out everything empirical, logic would become too short. It must, however, be set apart, and not placed among the proper members of logic. For example, the whole chapter on prejudices would have to drop out, since it is borrowed from psychology; psychology, however, considers the phenomena of the human soul, and these are taken from experience. All knowledge is either *a priori*, independent of all experience, e.g. 2 × 2 = 4, or *a posteriori*, empirical, having its source in experience. The latter is contingent, and its rules are not necessary. From this follows the preceding proposition, that logic must have no empirical principles whatever. For since in logic we abstract from all content, we abstract at the same time from every way in which objects are given to us through experience; and such a science is entitled *rationalis*, in distinction from the empirical. A logic will therefore have to be *rationalis* as regards its source. Logic is derived from reason, and has reason as its object. Logic is defined thus: *rationalis* through *scientia*; this involves two things, that it is not taken from experience, and that it has reason for its object. Now the question is what the purpose of this science is. — The purpose of a science is twofold: 1.) to extend our knowledge; 2.) to elucidate and rectify it. General logic can be no extension, but it can certainly be a rectification, of our knowledge. — A science which supplies principles *a priori* in accordance with which the powers of our understanding can be correctly used is a canon. A canon is a precept resting on pure reason, without which no correct use of the understanding is possible. Logic is therefore also a canon of understanding and reason. Besides the rectification of our knowledge we also want an extension of it; and if we demand this of logic, we require something that is contrary to its nature. A science that is the ground of an extension of our knowledge is entitled an organon, e.g. mathematics.",["Several OCR slips silently corrected (Logüi, weü, nnsers, 2erle}^ and the like); the passage opens mid-enumeration, so '2.)' answers a '1.)' not given here.","The long run from 'Alle Erkenntniß ist…' to 'Object hat' was divided into shorter sentences; 'Erkenntniß' is rendered 'knowledge'.","'[actually]' is supplied to bring out the contrast between subjective rules (how we do use the understanding) and objective rules (how we ought to).","'als Erklärung beybehalten' is rendered 'by way of explanation'; the sense (retaining empirical material as a mere gloss, not as a member of logic) is a judgment from the context.","'rationalis' and 'scientia' are Kant's own Latin and are kept in italics; 'Kräfte' is rendered 'powers'."]],"poelitz-s01-008":["Logic as canon is a science, but logic as organon is an art. Logic, however, is no organon, for otherwise there would have to be also a practical logic; it is only a canon of judging [*Beurteilung*]. That logic cannot be divided into the theoretical and the practical will be shown later.",["'Beurteilung' is given in German because the contrast with organon (an art of production) depends on it."]],"poelitz-s01-009":["We can now give a proper definition of logic, namely *Logica est regularum vniversalium vsus intellectus et rationis*. Aesthetic contains the principles of taste. Logic and aesthetic would thus already be distinguished by the difference of the powers of knowledge that are treated in these disciplines. The object of logic is the higher faculty of knowledge. The definition of logic as *scientia rationalis* is ambiguous: it is rational as regards form, but in that respect it is not yet distinguished from the other sciences at all; the real ground of the definition lies in this, that reason is its object, and indeed according to the general laws of its use. This expression is therefore not suitable. From the definition given above we now wish to derive the remaining properties of logic, namely 1.) if it is to be a science of general laws:",["The Latin is kept exactly as printed (with 'vniversalium', 'vsus'); it reads as a compressed formula and is not completed or emended.","'Erkenntniß Kräfte' / 'Erkenntnißvermögen' rendered 'powers of knowledge' and 'faculty of knowledge'.","The passage breaks off in mid-sentence (the colon) and is left so."]],"poelitz-s01-010":["it must also be a science of the necessary [laws]; for if they",["'[laws]' is supplied to resolve the elliptical 'von den nothwendigen'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is continued in the next."]],"poelitz-s01-011":["are general, they must hold for every understanding, without distinction of objects. 2.) A science which rests on principles *a priori* is a demonstrative science. Logic is therefore a demonstrative science. Hence everything it sets forth is capable of strict proof.",["Continues the previous fragment: 'sind' takes 'they' (the laws) as subject."]],"poelitz-s01-012":["To see the truth of a thing from experience is not yet",["Breaks off mid-sentence; completed in the next fragment."]],"poelitz-s01-013":["to demonstrate; for in that case I do not see the necessity.",["'einsehen' rendered 'see' to match the preceding fragment."]],"poelitz-s01-014":["Rules that are necessary must be derived *a priori*;",[]],"poelitz-s01-015":["those that are taken from experience are always contingent, because experience teaches how things are, and not how they",["Breaks off; 'müßen' follows in the next fragment."]],"poelitz-s01-016":["must [be].",["'[be]' supplied to complete the elliptical 'müßen'."]],"poelitz-s01-017":["… is a doctrine. — Doctrine is a science which can be proved from principles *a priori*. This name cannot be given to empirical sciences. Every doctrine",["The fragment begins mid-sentence; the subject of 'ist eine Doktrin' is lost (the preceding words are missing in the OCR), so it is marked by an ellipsis.","'Doktrin' rendered 'doctrine'; breaks off mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s01-018":["[is] dogmatic, that is, it can be proved *a priori*. Aesthetic cannot be a doctrine, for it can never be a science. Home [Kames] therefore does better to call it criticism [*Kritik*]. But since it contains the guiding line of our understanding, or the rules for judging the right use of understanding and reason, it is also entitled a canon. By any canon I can judge whether the procedure is correct. Logic is therefore the *Principium* of the *Dijudication* of the use of the understanding, but only as regards correctness of form. Logic is, however, no organon; through logic we only become conscious of the rules in accordance with which we have long since been thinking. It is a canon which afterwards serves for",["'[is]' supplied; the verb is lost at the line break. 'Home' is Henry Home, Lord Kames; the bracket is mine.","'Aesthetik' stands in the text, although the sentence seems to run on from logic; the transcript may be disordered here, and I translate what is given.","'Principium' and 'Dijudication' are Kant's Latin terms (dijudicatio, 'judging between') and are kept; 'Kritik' is given in German beside 'criticism'.","Breaks off before 'Kritik' in the next fragment."]],"poelitz-s01-019":["criticism, [to judge] whether what has been thought is correct. — Logic is divided into Analytic, or logic of truth, and Dialectic, or logic of illusion. (Our author divides it thus",["'[to judge]' is supplied to complete 'dient ob…'.","OCR 'Wahrund' is read as 'Wahrheit und' (the word is damaged; the sense is certain from 'Logik des Scheins' and the later 'Logik der Wahrheit').","'Schein' rendered 'illusion'; elsewhere in the passage 'dem Schein nach' is 'according to appearance'. The parenthesis is left open, as in the source."]],"poelitz-s01-020":["we find that Aristotle had already divided it so.) The",["The first words of the parenthesis are lost between this fragment and the preceding one; I have not filled them. The noun after 'Der' is carried on in the next fragment."]],"poelitz-s01-021":["[first] part discovers, by analysing them, all the actions of reason which we perform in thinking; it contains the necessary rules of all truth. It is entitled the logic of truth because without it our knowledge, irrespective of objects, is untrue even in itself. It is therefore only a canon of judging. Should anyone wish to use it as an organon, this would already be dialectic. It is an abuse of Analytic in so far as we wish to use it as an organon. The canon seems to be an organon, but is not. — Dialectic was studied with great diligence in former times; it sought to confirm things according to appearance [*dem Schein nach*], and so set forth false principles under the semblance of truth. — (The dialecticians were also otherwise called sophists, *Sophistae*, from which also comes the word *Sophisma*, a fallacy.) Among the Greeks the dialecticians were the advocates and orators who could prove a matter with equally good grounds",["'[first]' is supplied: the word is missing ('Der / Theil'), and the Analytic, named first, is evidently meant; flagged as a conjecture of sense, not a reading.","'Der Theil … zergliederung' restated as shorter sentences where needed; 'ohnangesehn der Objecte' is rendered 'irrespective of objects'.","'Mißbrauch' and 'Schein' rendered 'abuse' and 'semblance'; 'Sophistae' and 'Sophisma' are kept in Latin form, in italics.","Breaks off mid-sentence ('beweisen' continues with 'und stürzen' in the next fragment)."]],"poelitz-s01-022":["and overthrow, and thereby deceived the people. Nothing is",["'Volle' is read as 'Volk' (OCR slip).","Continues 'could prove … and overthrow' from the previous fragment; breaks off after 'Nichts ist'."]],"poelitz-s01-023":["[...] more unbecoming to a philosopher than this science. We shall treat dialectic as the art of distinguishing false appearance [*Schein*] from truth. — We have, then, two parts in logic, namely analytic, which sets forth the formal criteria of truth, and dialectic, which will contain the marks by which we recognise whether something fails to agree with the formal criteria of truth, even though it actually seems to agree with them. — How can we contrive an illusion [*Schein*], and why must logic have antidotes? This arises because in our judgments and inferences we are very much subject to illusion. Logical illusion in the form of knowledge springs from certain means to truth, by which, however, one often produces only a semblance of truth. For example, we are often deceived when we believe that the agreement of a mode of knowledge with the logical form of thought is already a sufficient mark of truth. But it is not. Agreement with the logical rules is a *conditio sine qua non*; it cannot be lacking, but by itself it is not yet sufficient, because it abstracts from all content. Just as there is a rhetorical dialectic, namely, uttering a multitude of words in the form of a speech in which, however, there is neither sense nor understanding, so there is also a logical dialectic, [which consists in] speaking the language of the understanding even when the understanding is empty of all content. The author and others hold that dialectic is the logic of probability; but there is a difference between this and illusion. — Probability is partial truth. Illusion is opposed to truth, but probability is not. A probable judgment is a judgment about truth from certain but insufficient grounds, and the probable belongs to truth just as the insufficient belongs to the sufficient. For if further grounds are added to the probable, it becomes true. — Logic can be divided into pure and applied. Pure logic considers how the understanding ought to act. Applied logic ought properly not to be entitled logic, for it is a psychology, in which we consider the phenomena of the understanding, that is, how it is accustomed to act, not how it ought to act. It is said that technic, or the way of constructing a science, ought to be set forth in applied logic. But this is vain, indeed even harmful, for one cannot build without materials. Technic must be set forth in every science. — Logic is also divided into theoretical and practical. But logic as canon cannot be practical, because logic abstracts from all content. A practical logic presupposes a certain kind of objects to which it is applied. — We say that every science is an applied logic; for in every science we must have a form of thought. — We shall divide logic into a dogmatic and a technical part. The former contains all the rules of the use of the understanding in general, that is, the theory of [...]; the latter is the prescription of the rules of the school, according to which we name all logical distinctions. It accordingly indicates all the technical terms, which afterwards serve for criticism. This part is therefore not superfluous, because without terminology we cannot criticise. The technical part of logic might be logic in so far as it contains the form of a system. — The division of logic into the logic of the common understanding and that of the speculative understanding is more important. The common understanding is the capacity to have insight into the rules of thought *in concreto*. (The logic of the common understanding is acquaintance with the rules *in concreto*.) If the common understanding makes right use of these rules *in concreto*, it is entitled the sound understanding. (The logic of the speculative understanding is acquaintance with the rules *in abstracto*.) The speculative understanding is the capacity to have insight into the rules *in abstracto*. For example, if someone promises another something and does not keep his word, this is unfair *in concreto*. Everyone readily sees this; but *in abstracto* it is difficult even for the jurist. Yet it is absolutely necessary to have insight into the rules *in abstracto*, since without this we cannot see their limits. If, then, we wish to extend our knowledge, we must pass over to speculative reason. If the common understanding always confined itself to judgments which it can see *in concreto*, it would be quite sufficient. The common understanding must therefore never go beyond the guiding thread of experience. A logic as a product of the common understanding, containing the rules in so far as they are known *in concreto*, is not possible. If, therefore, logic is to be a science of the rules *in abstracto*, it cannot be a product of the common understanding.",["The passage begins mid-sentence; the opening is rendered as a fragment, marked [...].","*Schein* is rendered 'illusion' or 'semblance' according to context, with the German added at the first occurrences. 'The author' is the textbook author (presumably Meier); I have not added the name.","'die Theorie der 2te ist...' is broken in the text (something is evidently missing after 'Theorie der'). I mark the gap [...] and read 'der 2te' as 'the latter' (the technical part); 'Er' in the next sentence refers to it.","'gewißen aber unzureichenden Gründen': 'gewiß' may mean 'certain' (sure) or 'particular'. I keep 'certain'.","I restated the long Schein/probability and common/speculative sections as shorter sentences. The OCR slips (istesaber, dieKenntniß, giebt s, woUen) were silently corrected, and a stray ■ was dropped.","*Kenntniß* is rendered 'acquaintance' to distinguish it from *Erkenntniß* ('knowledge'). *Vermögen* is rendered 'capacity'; *heißen* as 'is entitled'."]],"poelitz-s01-024":["Logic can be treated or expounded in two ways. Exposition [*Vortrag*] is the manner in which a doctrine can be made comprehensible. Method is the manner in which the object is to be completely known. — The exposition is either scholastic, when it is suited to the desire for knowledge, the ability and the culture of those who wish to treat the knowledge as a science; or popular, for those who do not wish to treat logic as a science but only need it to enlighten their understanding. The popular exposition avoids all technical terms and scientific arrangement. It is very beneficial to the community, and every scholar earns great credit if he can condescend to popularity. In the scholastic sense the rules must be universal; in the latter, however, [they must be] as few as possible, and everything *in concreto*. No one can learn to write popularly; it must come through knowledge of the world, or through practice in presenting one's school instruction to the public. The French have the advantage in this. — The scholastic exposition is the foundation of the popular.",["*Vortrag* is rendered 'exposition' (the transcript contrasts it with *Methode*). *das Erkenntniß* is 'the knowledge'; *Object* is rendered 'object'.","'Im scholastischen Verstände' is read as 'in the scholastic sense'; 'im lezteren' is read as the popular exposition, and I supply 'they must be' in brackets.","*gemeine Wesen* is rendered 'community' (commonwealth); OCR slip 'woUen' silently corrected."]],"poelitz-s02-001":["Designation and History of Logic.",[]],"poelitz-s02-002":["Epicurus called logic *canonica*; it was very compendious, and the name was a good one, namely, canon of reason. Aristotle may be regarded as the father of logic, at least so far as we are concerned. His manner of teaching is very scholastic, and goes to the most general concepts that underlie logic. In fact, however, no one derives any benefit from it, because almost everything ends in subtleties. The designation of the various actions of the understanding has been drawn from it, which is almost its only use. — The Aristotelian philosophy held the upper hand for a very long time, until the reformer Ramus came, who, however, lost his life in the Paris massacre of St. Bartholomew. Next [came] Malebranche and Locke, who, however, did not treat of logic proper, because they also speak of the content of knowledge. Among the moderns there are two who consider general logic as a whole, namely Leibniz and Wolff, and the latter's general logic is the best we have. — Others have combined Wolff's logic with the Aristotelian. — Wolff's logic was condensed by Baumgarten, a man who has great merit in this, and Meier has written a commentary upon him. [Let us] note briefly: Aristotle presented it as an organon and divided it into analytic and dialectic. He showed almost the greatest acuteness in his writings of all the ancients, yet he had many pettifogging minutiae [*Mikrologien*]. — The Scholastics, who were always vying with one another as to who could best expound Aristotle, lost their standing after the Reformation. Then came the sceptics, among whom Lullus, David Hume and Bayle, who may also be entitled antilogicians (that is, those who placed the understanding in uncertainty). To this place belongs, further, the book whose author is unknown, *Recherches de la vérité*. Lambert has laid down [?] something with regard to logic in his *Organon*.",["'bemerken wir kürzlich' follows a full stop without a capital and apparently lacks an introductory word (perhaps 'hier'). I translate it as it stands, with 'Let us' supplied in brackets.","'bestirnt' is an OCR misreading, probably 'bestimmt' ('determined, laid down'). I render it so, with a query.","'Lullus' stands in the text among the sceptics beside Hume and Bayle, which is chronologically odd; I leave it as printed. The *Recherches de la vérité* is Malebranche's title, but the transcript calls its author unknown. 'Wolf' is given in the usual English spelling 'Wolff'.","I split the long sentence on Wolff, Baumgarten and Meier, and read 'Pariser Bluthochzeit' as the St. Bartholomew's Day massacre. *Mikrologien* is retained in brackets as a technical term of the scholastic period. *Antilogiker* is kept as 'antilogicians'."]],"poelitz-s03-001":["Treatment of Logic. Of Knowledge.",["*Abhandelung* is rendered 'treatment'. *Erkenntniß* is rendered 'knowledge'."]],"poelitz-s03-002":["All our modes of knowledge have a twofold relation:",["*Erkenntniße* (plural) rendered 'modes of knowledge', since 'knowledge' cannot be pluralised."]],"poelitz-s03-003":["1) a relation to the object, that is, representation;",["'that is, representation' (*d. i. Vorstellung*) is kept as it stands; the transcript is elliptical, and the sense is presumably that the relation to the object is the representation."]],"poelitz-s03-004":["2) a relation to the subject, that is, the consciousness of this representation. Consciousness is, strictly speaking, a representation that another representation is in me. The author endeavours to explain representation, but in vain, for a representation would always have to be explained through another representation.",["*Bewustseyn* = consciousness. 'The author' is Meier, whose text the lectures follow; the transcript does not name him here."]],"poelitz-s03-005":["In every mode of knowledge we must distinguish matter, that is, the object, and form, that is, the manner in which we know the object. For example, a savage sees a house in the distance, but does not know its use. He then has in his representation precisely the same object as another man has, but the form is different. For him it is mere intuition; for the other it is intuition and concept, namely that it is a dwelling for human beings. —",["*Erkenntniß* in the singular rendered 'mode of knowledge'. *Begrife* corrected silently to *Begriff* ('concept')."]],"poelitz-s03-006":["A representation which contains what is common to several things is a concept; a single representation, on the other hand, which contains what [belongs to] only one [thing], is an intuition. Knowledge through concepts is discursive knowledge; knowledge through intuition is intuitive knowledge.",["The German reads 'was nur einem gemein ist' ('what is common only to one'), which is odd; I have rendered the evident sense and supplied the bracketed words. The transcript may be faulty."]],"poelitz-s03-007":["The difference of form rests upon consciousness. If I am conscious of the representation, it is clear; if I am not conscious of it, it is obscure. Consciousness is the essential condition of all logical form of modes of knowledge. Logic does not treat of the manner in which we occupy ourselves with obscure representations. It assumes that they are clear, and only in so far as they are clear can it concern itself with them; for if I do not know that I have a representation, I cannot apply the logical rules to it. Obscure representations are therefore not an object of logic, because here we do not consider how representations arise, but only how they accord with logical form. —\n\nAll clear representations can be distinguished in respect of distinctness and indistinctness. If I am conscious of the whole representation, but not of the manifold contained in it, the representation is indistinct. For example, I see a country house in the distance and am conscious that it is a house. Yet I must also necessarily have a representation of the parts of the house, for example of the window, etc.; for if I did not see the parts, I should not see the whole house either. Since, however, I am not conscious of this representation of the manifold, I have an indistinct representation. Another example: in the concept of beauty various things occur; namely, there must be something that falls within the senses, and something that pleases universally. Everyone has a clear concept of the beautiful; but as soon as he cannot set out its manifold [in analysis], the concept is nevertheless indistinct.\n\nThe followers of Wolff entitle an indistinct representation 'confused' (*verworren*). This expression is not appropriate, because the opposite of confusion is not distinctness but order. Distinctness is indeed an effect of order, and indistinctness an effect of disorder; but they are not the same thing. Every confused mode of knowledge is indeed indistinct, but not every indistinct one is confused. Indistinctness often springs not from confusion but from want of consciousness. Where there is no manifold in a mode of knowledge, there is neither order nor confusion. Hence all simple representations must be entitled not confused but indistinct. Something can be distinct in respect of form, that is, I can be conscious of the manifold in the representation; but in respect of matter distinctness can diminish, when the degree of consciousness becomes smaller, although all order is present. —",["*Undeutlichkeit* = 'indistinctness'; *Deutlichkeit* = 'distinctness'; *klar/dunkel* = 'clear/obscure'. Rendering of *Deutlichkeit* is a choice.","'in [analysis]' supplied for *auseinander sezen* to bring out the sense of setting out the parts; literally 'set apart'.","*nennen die Schüler des Wolfs* rendered 'entitled' per the method; 'followers of Wolff' for *Schüler*.","The last sentence is obscure in the German (form versus matter of distinctness); rendered closely, and the sense is uncertain.","Spacing before colons and the 'denTheilen' slip silently corrected; the long paragraph is divided into three for readability."]],"poelitz-s03-008":["A representation is indistinct if I am conscious of the whole of it but not of its parts; it is distinct if I am conscious also of the manifold, or of the parts in the whole. Yet consciousness of the manifold in the representation does not directly constitute distinctness, for order is also required for it.",["*wie wohl* taken as 'yet/although'; the sentence has been divided in two."]],"poelitz-s03-009":["Distinctness can be of two kinds: 1) sensible distinctness, which consists in consciousness of the manifold in intuition. For example, I regard the Milky Way as",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage. 'l.)' read as '1.)'."]],"poelitz-s03-010":["a whitish streak in the sky; the stars of it",["Fragment of a sentence continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-011":["must necessarily have come into my eye, but the representation was only clear; through the telescope it becomes distinct, because",["*Aug* = eye; sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-012":["I now see all the small stars contained in it. 2) Distinctness in concepts, or distinctness of the understanding, consists in this, that I analyse the concept according to the manifold that lies in it. For example, virtue: here there is contained first of all",["*Verstandesdeutlichkeit* = 'distinctness of the understanding'. Breaks off; continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-013":["the concept of freedom, then adherence to rules, and",["Fragment continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-014":["the power to overcome the inclinations when they are contrary to these rules,",["Fragment continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-015":["to overcome them.",["Likely a stray repetition: the German *zu überwältigen* completes the infinitive begun in the preceding passage ('the power … to overcome'); the English duplicates 'overcome' because the passages are split mid-clause."]],"poelitz-s03-016":["By such a making distinct [Deutlichmachung] I have added nothing to my concept, here for example of virtue, but have merely explained it. — In distinctness, then, representations are improved not in respect of matter but in respect of form. — The author speaks of rational modes of knowledge in order to distinguish them from historical. All knowledge by which it is represented that something *is*, is historical; knowledge that something *must* be, is rational knowledge. They are thus modes of knowledge of the necessity of judgments. Otherwise modes of knowledge are also commonly entitled rational if they accord with reason. I know something historically in so far as it is communicated to me by others; such knowledge comprehends in itself experience of the judgment of others. Reason infers necessarily, because it derives everything from the necessary conditions of truth. —\n\nAll our modes of knowledge are either intuitions or concepts. The faculty of intuition is sensibility; the faculty of concepts is understanding. Sensibility can also be defined in another way, namely metaphysically, where it is considered from another side: it is the faculty of receptivity, whereas the understanding is the faculty of spontaneity, and represents things only as they are, not as they affect us. — Sensibility is entitled *facultas inferior*, because it gives us the material for thinking; understanding is entitled *facultas superior*, because it disposes of this material and brings it under a representation or a concept. A mode of knowledge may be perfect in accordance with the laws of sensibility, and is then aesthetically perfect; or in accordance with the laws of the understanding, and is then logically perfect. — Sensibility is therefore a perfection in so far as it serves to supply material for thinking and to make the rules of the understanding distinct *in concreto*. In this respect it is indispensable. But sensibility is an imperfection when it confuses concepts. — The understanding [is] elevated when it is accompanied by sensibility; but if the latter takes the place of the former, confusion arises. Without sensibility concepts would have no object, and they have significance only in intuition, that is, in sensibility. In respect of sensibility a mode of knowledge may have much perfection that is far removed from the logical; for example, when it is new, easy and lively, that is, has subjective clarity. A mathematical proof, however, cannot be rejected because it is difficult. — The perfection of knowledge, in so far as it can be judged only by norms *a posteriori*, is aesthetic; in so far as it can be judged by norms *a priori*, it is logical.\n\nThe question arises: What is perfection? Strictly this belongs to metaphysics. In place of 'perfection' we shall put 'it pleases'; for we call perfection that which contributes to something's pleasing. If something pleases according to laws of reason, it *must* please; if according to laws of sensibility, this is not necessary. The constitution of objects by which they agree with the universal laws of sensibility is aesthetic perfection, and we learn it solely from experience. — The faculty of judging whether something agrees with the laws of sensibility is taste; whether it agrees with the laws of the understanding, is the power of judgment [Urtheilskraft]. What pleases, pleases either mediately or immediately. — In general, whatever agrees with the laws of the power of representation pleases, whether the representations be sensations, concepts or intuitions; hence something pleases either intellectually or sensitively. What pleases mediately is in itself indifferent, and may even in itself displease; but it pleases as a means to something that pleases in a higher degree. That something should please as a means is a matter for the understanding, and is not an object of our present enquiry into the universal laws of what pleases and displeases.\n\nThat which pleases is divided into the agreeable, the beautiful and the good. The concepts are correct, but the division is not properly logical. Better is this division: everything that pleases is either an object of private or of universal satisfaction [Wohlgefallen]. If the latter, I also know the necessity. Nothing else holds here than that I must be able to say: It must please everyone; and then the ground lies in the object. — That which is a ground of private satisfaction is agreeable. Hence we do not say the object is agreeable, but that it is agreeable *to me*. The agreeable is either charm [Reiz] or emotion [Rührung]. What pleases universally pleases either according to laws of sensibility, and is then entitled beautiful, or according to laws of the understanding, and is then entitled good. True beauty must therefore be wholly separated from charm and emotion. The agreeable pleases the senses in sensation and is the cause of private satisfaction. The beautiful pleases the senses in intuition and is the cause of universal satisfaction. Hence it is also said that the faculty of distinguishing the agreeable from the disagreeable is feeling, which everyone has; the faculty of distinguishing the beautiful from the not beautiful (not from the ugly, for what is not beautiful is not always ugly) is taste. Both belong to the sensible faculty of judging. He has taste who chooses what pleases universally; and in so far as it pleases the understanding of every rational man, it is good. The good and the beautiful agree in that both relate to the object. In both there is a universally valid satisfaction, but they differ in this: in the former the universality is strict, in the latter comparative. The highest objective satisfaction is in the good, the highest subjective in the agreeable. All strict universality must admit of proof; that is, it must be possible to show why something pleases and that it must please. — The good too can be proved from universal principles; but the rules of taste cannot be proved, that is, cannot be known *a priori*, for they are rules of sensibility. — Accordingly experience alone can decide on the correctness of taste.\n\nAll logical perfection rests on the agreement of knowledge with the object; the sensible, on the agreement of knowledge with the subject. Logical perfection therefore contains rules which must hold for every understanding and every mode of knowledge; for the knowledge of all thinking beings must coincide with ours, if we contemplate one and the same object. The perfection of the understanding thus rests on universally valid laws, the aesthetic on the particular sensibility of each man. A logically perfect mode of knowledge can also be aesthetic; for example, when a piece of knowledge that is true is at the same time easy and widely extended. — Of all aesthetic perfections none can be better combined with logic, and none seems more essential to it, than intuition, that is, form, in which there are also universal laws. Sensations, that is, matter, cannot be communicated, and here too no universal laws obtain. —\n\nOur modes of knowledge, even when they already have logical perfection, which consists in distinctness, truth and universality, can still acquire aesthetic perfection. In respect of the points just named, aesthetic perfection is not only different from the logical but in some respects opposed to it. First, in respect of distinctness through concepts. Aesthetic perfection is distinctness in intuition, hence mere liveliness. The liveliness of knowledge is promoted by the object's being presented to the senses; distinctness through concepts is promoted when the object is abstracted from the senses. Next, as to truth: logical truth need not obtain here, or [rather] the knowledge need not agree with the object but with the subject. Aesthetically and poetically true is that which accords with sensible appearance [Schein]. — Truth requires investigation, comparison and testing, which is hard; if I merely let the imagination play, it is easier, that is, something enlivening, something that brings my powers of mind into a harmonious play. — Universality: this concept is the opposite of what is required for aesthetic perfection. In logical perfection universality is required *in abstracto*. In aesthetic perfection this universality is illustrated *in concreto* by particular cases, and here aesthetic perfection is detrimental to the logical. Aesthetic perfection can therefore be considered from two points of view, namely as advantageous and as harmful to the logical; the first is the essential beautiful, the last the non-essential beautiful. — Of what belongs to sensation and not to intuition we have no concept. Sensations admit of no logical perfection whatever. Charm belongs to the agreeable, emotion to the sublime; both belong to aesthetic perfection, but to the non-essential beautiful. Only what lies in intuition belongs to the essential beautiful. Some objects occupy our feeling more, others our intuition more. Our sensible power of knowledge can therefore have more intuition, or, from another object, more sensation. Sensation belongs to beauty and to what accords with taste, but it is most opposed to logical perfection, as are charm and emotion; for by them my knowledge becomes neither more certain nor more true, and charm and emotion even corrupt the logical perfection of our modes of knowledge and judgments.\n\nFrom this it follows that logical perfection is the basis without which no perfection can obtain. Aesthetic perfection cannot subsist by itself alone; the logical can indeed yield, but cannot be wholly cancelled. I must not, for example, give a concept all possible distinctness in order to concede something to the aesthetic; and it may be said generally that aesthetic perfection always takes something from the logical, though the need of human nature requires it. Both, sensibility and understanding, must be paired together, and the scholastically correct (logical) perfection must become popular, that is, yield. The rule for uniting logical and aesthetic perfection therefore follows from this: (1) logical perfection is the foundation; (2) sensibility may be added, in order to explain the concepts *in concreto* and make them comprehensible; (3) but one must attend more to intuitions than to sensations, because the former are the essential beautiful; (4) finally charm and emotion may be added, though one must be very cautious with them, since through them attention can be drawn from the object to the subject, which is very detrimental. That logical perfection is given the preference arises from this, that the understanding has the advantage that what it says is universal; since the judgment of the understanding has universal validity, sensation has little worth in comparison with it, because sensation bears only on particulars, not on the universal.\n\nBut because logical and aesthetic perfection must be together, there always arises from this a mixture of praise and blame, since one can never do full justice to both powers of knowledge; for example, a discourse is beautiful but shallow, or thorough but dry. Here one must necessarily look to the purpose. Certain modes of knowledge are meant to instruct, and these must be thorough; or to entertain, and these must be beautiful. In the union of these two requirements in modes of knowledge that are meant both to instruct and to entertain rests what is called genius. The understanding wishes to be instructed, sensibility to be enlivened; the first demands insight, the second comprehensibility. Between the two there is therefore a kind of conflict that cannot be removed. One must accordingly seek to satisfy the greater purpose. For example, in an oration logical perfection is more needed than aesthetic, though the latter is not to be neglected; in a poem the aesthetic is more needed. Many modes of knowledge can become very tasteful if they have first been presented in a dry and thorough manner, for example morals.",["Silently corrected OCR slips (e.g. Deutlichniachung, metaphj^sisch, SinnHchkeit, Beiz for Reiz, kerne for keine, Kraft Kraft duplicated); stray marks (■) and line numbers omitted.","'Ein Erkenntniß kann vorkommen seyn' is read as 'vollkommen seyn'; translated 'can be perfect'.","'Verstand von Sinnlichkeit begleitet wird erhoben' is garbled or elliptical in the transcript; rendered 'The understanding [is] elevated when it is accompanied by sensibility', with the verb supplied in brackets. Doubtful.","'derlogischen vortheilhaft,und schädlich' is read as 'der logischen vortheilhaft und schädlich'.","Long sentences were divided and some run-on passages restated, as the method allows. In the sentence on truth, 'oder' is read as 'or [rather]', and 'Next, as to truth' is supplied to mark the second head after 'Zuerst', which has no explicit follow-up.","Wohlgefallen = 'satisfaction'; gefallen = 'please'; Reiz = 'charm'; Rührung = 'emotion'; Urtheilskraft = 'power of judgment'; Beurtheilungs Vermögen = 'faculty of judging'; Faßlichkeit = 'comprehensibility'. These are choices.","Vollkommenheit is rendered 'perfection' throughout. 'Schein' is rendered 'appearance [Schein]', not Erscheinung.","In 'Es gefält' the quotation marks around 'perfection' and 'it pleases' are supplied for clarity; the German has none."]],"poelitz-s04-001":["On the perfection of knowledge in general.",[]],"poelitz-s04-002":["The perfection of knowledge is 1) logical, 2) aesthetic, 3) practical.",[]],"poelitz-s04-003":["Remark. Practical perfection does not belong in logic, for logic deals with the faculty of knowledge, not with the will. Strictly speaking, aesthetic perfection does not belong there either; but we take it up only in order to elucidate logical perfection better by contrast. — These perfections rest on the three faculties: understanding, feeling, desire. A perfection can accordingly be considered in a threefold respect: 1) in relation to the faculty of knowledge itself; 2) in so far as it fills us with satisfaction [*Wohlgefallen*]; 3) in so far as it is not an idle piece of knowledge but has an influence upon our action. ■ — ■ *Perfectio logica consistit*",["OCR 'Erkenntniß vermögen' read as 'Erkenntnißvermögen'; 'x4bsicht' silently corrected to 'Absicht' (rendered 'respect'); '■ — ■' is an OCR/apparatus artefact, kept as it stands.","The passage breaks off after *Perfectio logica consistit*; the Latin is left uncompleted, the continuation being in the next passages.","'Vermögen' rendered 'faculty' as the sense requires; 'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'."]],"poelitz-s04-004":["1) in respect of quantity, in universality [*in vniversalitate*].",["Latin 'vniversalitate' is the OCR/transcript spelling of *universalitate*."]],"poelitz-s04-005":["2) of quality, in clarity [*in perspicuitate*].",["The governing 'respectu' is carried over from the preceding line."]],"poelitz-s04-006":["3) of relation, in truth [*in veritate*].",[]],"poelitz-s04-007":["4) of modality, in necessity [*in necessitate*].",["'neceßitate' silently normalised in rendering."]],"poelitz-s04-008":["The logical perfection of a mode of knowledge is considered under four heads.",["'Avird' is OCR for 'wird'. 'Erkenntnißes' is singular, but 'a mode of knowledge' is used since English 'knowledge' takes no article."]],"poelitz-s04-009":["1) In respect of quantity, when it is universal.",["'algemem' is OCR for 'algemein'."]],"poelitz-s04-010":["2) In respect of quality, when it is distinct [*deutlich*].",["'Deutlich' is rendered 'distinct'; the Latin parallel in s04-005 is *perspicuitas*, so 'clear' is the alternative."]],"poelitz-s04-011":["3) In respect of relation, when it is true.",[]],"poelitz-s04-012":["4) In respect of modality, when it is certain. On (1): A mode of knowledge that serves as a rule and has other modes of knowledge under it",["The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage; translated as it stands.","'Gewiß' rendered 'certain' (cf. 'Gewißheit' below), though the Latin has *necessitas*."]],"poelitz-s04-013":["is more perfect than one that has only a particular use. On (2): One asks here, is a mode of knowledge distinct or indistinct?",["'volkommener' rendered 'more perfect'."]],"poelitz-s04-014":["...use. As to the second point: here we ask, Is a mode of knowledge [Erkenntniß] distinct or indistinct?",["The passage opens mid-sentence (\"Gebrauch hat\"); the preceding clause is not given, so the fragment is translated as it stands, with an ellipsis.","\"Zu 2.\" refers to the second item of an enumeration that begins before this passage; rendered \"As to the second point\" to make the reference plain.","Erkenntniß rendered \"mode of knowledge\" because it is used here of a particular cognition, not of knowledge in general; deutlich/undeutlich = distinct/indistinct (the Latin parallel being *distinctus/indistinctus*)."]],"poelitz-s04-015":["If it is distinct, it is more perfect in respect of quality. On (3): The relation of knowledge to the object is truth. If the",["The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s04-016":["knowledge of the object is not true, it is no knowledge at all, or at least not knowledge of the object which I believed myself to know,",["'oder nicht des Objects' is elliptical; 'at least' and 'knowledge' are supplied by judgment."]],"poelitz-s04-017":["and it [truth] is the chief matter. — If one looks merely to sensation [*Empfindung*],",["'sie' is taken to refer to truth (*Wahrheit*), not to 'Erkenntniß'; the antecedent is uncertain, since the clause 'und sie ist die Hauptsache' may be a garbled transcription."]],"poelitz-s04-018":["one does not trouble much about truth. On (4): Certainty is that which banishes all doubt; it is the consciousness of the necessity of truth.",[]],"poelitz-s04-019":["We shall now take the aesthetic perfections which correspond to these. — I can think to myself",["The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s04-020":["1) subjective truth, which is not the truth of a thing in itself, but of the thing as it appears to us and affects our senses; 2) subjective distinctness, or distinctness in intuition [*Anschauung*], which is obtained through examples; 3) aesthetic universality, that is, popularity: namely, that it pleases everyone and is suited to every understanding; 4) aesthetic necessity and certainty rests on this, that a mode of knowledge is necessary according to our senses, that is, that experience confirms it. Here two factors always occur which constitute perfection, manifoldness and unity. Our faculty of knowledge is eager for the former; but if the latter is lacking to it, it cannot satisfy us. In the understanding the unity is a concept [*Begriff*], in the senses it is intuition. Truth, however, is the chief perfection, since it is the ground of unity through the relation to the object. In aesthetic perfection also truth is the foremost negative condition; for this [the aesthetic] is agreeableness, but untruth cannot subsist with aesthetic perfection either. No one therefore makes progress in the fine sciences unless he has laid logical perfection at the foundation of his modes of knowledge. No science, it may be said, is in itself beautiful, since rules must always precede practice, whereas the beautiful must be judged from the effect, which belongs to sensibility [*Sinnlichkeit*]. — Taste, however, cannot be brought under rules. One can indeed give some, but they serve for judging, and do not hold as laws which carry an inner certainty in themselves. The rules of the understanding, on the other hand, are all laws. It is a peculiarity of everything beautiful that it cannot be treated as a science; hence there can be no fine science. — There is therefore no other way of coming to possess the beautiful and taste than through beautiful models, such as are found among the ancients. They are of such a kind that they have withstood the criticism [*Kritik*] of so many centuries and have always been found good. Something permanent, then, seems to be present here. The mark of taste is this: that what pleases one pleases others also; it has therefore something social about it, and is subject to fashion and imitation. — Taste, however, is what comes last, for it is a power of judgment [*Urtheilskraft*], which comes later than all cultivation of memory and of understanding; for one must already have much to choose from if one is to judge. — We can occupy ourselves in two ways: through leisure, and through proper occupation or labour. All our occupation with the beautiful is play, and as such is to be approved; but if this play becomes a business, it is ridiculous. All this gives us guidance as to how taste is to be formed: not by rules, but by models; these, however, are not to be imitated, but are only to leave an impression upon us.",["Passage continues s04-019; 'Algernemheit' → 'Allgemeinheit', 'm der Anschauung' → 'in der', 'Begrif ' → 'Begriff', 'Kerne' → 'Keine', 'findt' → 'findet' silently corrected.","'Hier kommen immer 2 Stücke vor' etc.: sentence 4 ('beruht darauf…') is kept as in the German, with its singular verb.","'denn dieses ist die Annehmlichkeit' is obscure; 'this' taken as the aesthetic perfection, and the bracket is mine. Doubtful reading.","'Verstand' in 'für jedes Verstand' is rendered 'understanding'; 'Kritik' rendered 'criticism' as the context suits; 'Erkenntniß Kraft' rendered 'faculty of knowledge'.","Long sentences kept largely as they stand, since the transcript's looseness is part of the source; lecture-note style is not smoothed beyond punctuation."]],"poelitz-s05-001":["On the concept of science.",["'Begrif' → 'Begriff'; 'Wißenschaft' rendered 'science'."]],"poelitz-s05-002":["A science is a system of modes of knowledge. Modes of knowledge are either common or systematic. — One may have many common modes of knowledge which are yet not connected in a systematic way, and so do not have the utility which they might have. Sciences are either sciences of reason or erudition [*Gelehrsamkeit*]. The former can be known only from universal principles of reason; the latter can be taught, or may be learned historically. Acquaintance with and criticism of languages and books are instruments of erudition, and may also be reckoned as belonging to it. The philosopher is therefore not a scholar; he must look only to the ultimate ends of erudition. Nor is he an artist of reason [*Vernunftkünstler*], but studies the legislation of reason. Philosophy and mathematics likewise do not belong to erudition, though to know the philosophy of others is erudition. The philosopher may adorn his philosophy with erudition.",["'sein1' read as 'seyn' (to be); the '1' is an OCR mark. 'Vernunftwißenschafft' read as 'Vernunftwissenschaft'.","'Systehm' → 'System'. 'Gelehrsamkeit' rendered 'erudition'.","'können auch zu ihr gerechnet werden': 'ihr' taken as Gelehrsamkeit."]],"poelitz-s05-003":["Erudition is the sum total of historical sciences; modes of knowledge of reason, however, can be attained without being taught by anyone, as, for example, a lad in England who as yet understood nothing of mathematics gradually hit of himself upon many Euclidean propositions. The philosopher and the mathematician are indeed called scholars [*Gelehrte*] in contrast to the ordinary citizen, but in Latin not *eruditi* but *litterati* (these being properly only such as can read and write). The sum total of all historical sciences is entitled polyhistory; the sum total of all sciences of reason taken together, polymathy. — The sum total of these two, pansophy. The historical knowledge of reason combined with philosophy might be entitled encyclopaedia.",["OCR slips corrected: 'Phüosophen' → 'Philosophen', 'gelahrt' → 'gelehrt', 'Vernunfter kenntnißen' → 'Vernunfterkenntnißen', 'beyder' → 'beider'.","Latin 'eruditos'/'litteratos' are accusative in the source; given in nominative plural in italics for English use.","'Lehrsatze' rendered 'propositions'; 'heißet' rendered 'is entitled'.","'Das historische Erkenntniß der Vernunft' is awkward; rendered literally as 'historical knowledge of reason', doubtful as to sense."]],"poelitz-s05-004":["In vastness [*Vastität*], learning [*Gelehrsamkeit*] takes precedence over rational knowledge; for it is itself a science of rational knowledge, although it is not rational knowledge. Both, however, are distinguished from art. The man of fine wit [*der schöne Geist*], the orator and the poet are not philosophers, not mathematicians, not scholars, but artists; for the beautiful cannot be taught. All imperfection of knowledge concerns either quantity or quality. In respect of both, it is either material or formal. Logical or formal magnitude is universality; aesthetic or material magnitude is extensiveness. The more things to which the knowledge can be applied, the greater it is logically; the more I know, the greater it is aesthetically. Material imperfection as regards quality consists in error or falsity, that is, in the knowledge's not agreeing with the object. A piece of knowledge may be true but indistinct, or true but not universal. Imperfection is either imperfection of privation or imperfection of opposition: *imperfectio contradictorie sic dicta* and *imperfectio contrarie sic dicta*. The first might also be entitled *imperfectio negative sic dicta*, in which there is a mere absence of perfection. Imperfection of lack is entitled in logic, in general, ignorance; that of privation is entitled error. Where the latter occurs, the former is always present, and the latter is worse than the former. The author divides imperfection into *defectus* and *vitium*. *Imperfectio privative dicta est defectus*; *imperfectio contrarie dicta est vitium*. Imperfection of lack is not to be blamed, [whereas this is not so] in respect of imperfection *in sensu contrario*. The faults are either logical or aesthetic; [they are] essential, when they concern the end itself, or *extra* essential. Aesthetic imperfection is dryness; logical imperfection is shallowness. Aesthetic imperfection is not ugliness, for dryness is a lack of what pleases the senses. True ugliness, moreover, cannot be reconciled with logical perfection. One must not flatter the senses too much, but neither must one withhold too much from them. The ancients excused their barbarisms by appeal to thoroughness; but when the sciences came to be better cultivated, it was seen to be necessary not to impair aesthetic perfection. All our modes of knowledge, even when they derive from experience, we must bring to concepts, and the concepts in turn back to intuitions, since otherwise we soon become confused in general concepts.",["Sentence 1: 'An Vastität geht Gelehrsamkeit der Vernunft Erkenntniß vor' is read as 'learning takes precedence over rational knowledge'; the grammar is loose in the transcript.","'Bey diesem f hielt immer jene statt': the stray 'f' is dropped as an OCR slip. 'diesem' is taken to be error, 'jene' ignorance, as the following clause requires.","The sentence on the faults ('logisch oder ästhetisch eßentiel die den Zweck selbst betreffen, und extra eßentiel') is garbled in the transcript. I have kept a loose rendering with brackets. In the preceding sentence I supplied '[whereas this is not so]' to complete the contrast.","OCR corrections made silently: 'sie dieta' to *sic dicta*, 'clefectus' to *defectus*, 'exeolirt' to *excolirt* ('cultivated'), 'Alle3o' to 'Alle'.","Long sentences were divided only lightly. *Erkenntniß* is rendered 'knowledge', and the plural *Erkenntniße* as 'modes of knowledge'."]],"poelitz-s05-005":["All knowing can have perfections of two kinds:",["'Vollkommenheiten' is rendered 'perfections' in the plural, as the text has it."]],"poelitz-s05-006":["1. A scholastic perfection, which is conformable to objective rules.",["OCR: 'Ehie' read as 'Eine'; 'objeetiven' read as 'objectiven'."]],"poelitz-s05-007":["2. A popular perfection, which is conformable to the subjective rules of social intercourse. The former is entitled truth, the latter taste.",["OCR: 'populaire', 'subjeetiven' corrected silently."]],"poelitz-s05-008":["He who observes scholastic punctiliousness, in so far as it needlessly conflicts with taste, is entitled a pedant; but not he who displays it before the connoisseur. *Definitio generalis*: needless punctiliousness in matters of form is entitled pedantry, as, for example, bringing out school maxims in the company of the unlearned.",["OCR: 'generahs' read as *generalis*; 'Schillsentenzen' read as 'Schulsentenzen' (school maxims, i.e. scholastic tags), which is a conjecture."]],"poelitz-s06-001":["On the Horizon of Knowledge.",[]],"poelitz-s06-002":["Horizon is the congruence of the limits of our knowledge with the ends of humanity. It would thus be the sum total of our knowledge which, taken together, is adequate to the end we have. If we take the ends universally, the horizon is absolute; if, however, we take the ends specially, in this or that respect, the horizon is relatively determined. To determine the horizon belongs to the cultivation of our power of judgment. Though this is a difficult task, it is nevertheless useful, because it avoids much confusion in our concepts and judgments. The horizon can be determined: 1) logically, in relation to our faculty of knowledge, [asking] how far we can get and how far we ought to go; or in relation to a logical aim, [asking] in how far modes of knowledge serve as means to our principal knowledge as our end. In the cutting-out everything must be planned for completeness; one comes to a reduction in the end. The first is necessary for philosophy. 2) Aesthetically, in relation to taste. The horizon in relation to taste might also be entitled popularity. I may determine the horizon either by the judgment of the public or by that of the connoisseurs. One who does not wish to become a scholar by profession [*von métier*], but wants only to know enough to take part in conversation in company, seeks current learning, which pleases everyone and in which even women can take an interest; he thus determines the horizon in respect of taste. Since it is more attractive to have the approval of the public than of a few closet scholars, men look solely to the aesthetic horizon, or shape the science to the taste of the public, and thereby fall into shallowness. This is the case with the French. 3) Practically, in relation to utility. The practical horizon, in respect of the influence which it has upon our morality, is pragmatic. One must not determine the horizon too early, because one then knows no science as yet and so cannot choose one. Nor must one change it too often; this happens with those who have not determined it properly and have done so too early. What I cannot know lies beyond my horizon. What I may not know lies outside my horizon. That, ignorance of which is even praiseworthy, lies beneath my horizon. In respect of historical knowledge the horizon is immeasurable, and of this one must acquire as much as possible, especially in youth, when memory still has the most receptivity and when one still finds a taste for it; afterwards, when one thinks for oneself, the taste for historical knowledge is lost. The horizon of mathematics can be determined thus: namely, [by asking] to what kind of objects mathematics cannot be extended. In respect of rational knowledge, [it is determined by asking] how far reason can go *a priori*, without experience; and this is useful so that we may not make fruitless undertakings. Error is an imperfection of privation; ignorance, an imperfection of lack; and the former is worse than the latter, for to remove error one must again procure ignorance. The latter is something negative, the former something positive. In the removal of ignorance one is always in danger of errors, and for this reason we must not remain ignorant. Two factors contribute to error: ignorance, and the presumption of wishing to know more than one can know. Ignorance is no great reproach to make to anyone; but this holds only of those who have a great deal of knowledge, and yet, for all that, are amazed at the multitude of what they do not know. The author speaks of a praiseworthy ignorance. Ignorance is never praiseworthy; to ignore something, or to be unwilling to know it, might well be praiseworthy, but then it is not ignorance that is praised, but the reflection by which I exclude the knowledge from my remaining knowledge. This is properly to abstract, not to ignore.",["The sentence 'Im Zuschnitt muß alles auf Volständigkeit angelegt werden. Zum Abschlag komts doch.' is cryptic. I take it as a tailor's image: plan for completeness in the cutting, though one ends by reducing. The reading is doubtful.","'Zum Irthum... Unwißenheit... jener ist ärger': 'jener' and 'dieser' are resolved by the argument (error is worse than ignorance). In the clause 'Diese ist etwas negatives, jener etwas positives' I have taken 'diese' as ignorance and 'jener' as error.","OCR corrections made silently: 'Avie' to 'wie', 'nehmhch' to 'nämlich'. 'Kenner' is rendered 'connoisseurs'. 'Vorwitz' is rendered 'presumption', and 'Receptivität' as 'receptivity'.","'Horizont... bestimmt' is rendered 'determined' (*Bestimmung*); 'relativ determinirt' is rendered 'relatively determined'."]],"poelitz-s06-003":["The ancients distinguished idiots and sophists. An idiot is a man who fancies that he knows a great deal and yet knows nothing. Not to know one's ignorance is a reproach, and to know it is science; for he who knows nothing does not know either that he is ignorant. The opposite of ignorance is knowing much. Historical knowing without determinate limits is polyhistory; rational knowing extended without determinate limits is entitled polymathy. The latter, however, must also have historical knowledge, since otherwise he has no application for his speculations. Both together may be entitled pansophy. To the former belongs philology, a science of the instruments of learning. Critical knowledge of books and languages makes a philologist. One part of philology is entitled *humaniora*, and he who practises it is entitled a *humanist*. *Humaniora* are the instruction in whatever serves the culture of taste. All this knowledge must be reduced to one principal science, and the others are regarded only as means of arriving at it. All the sciences stand in connection, and one can go in for sciences without connection, which is then practical polyhistory; but if one sets before oneself one principal science and regards the others as means of attaining it, that is a system. Scholars may be divided into guild scholars and scholars free of the guild. Had the sciences not become bread-winning arts, we should never have advanced so far in them. Every science as a free art has many advantages: it has its complete freedom and can purify its knowledge of all delusion and prejudice; but it lacks sufficient impulse and strain of the powers to perfect itself. The author now speaks of a fault that concerns the use of our knowledge, namely pedantry. In the use of our knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] it can be distinguished whether the use is more suited to the school than to the world. The first contains the method and the form for teaching and learning useful science, which, however, can be dropped in use. Scholastic perfection consists, then, in the didactic form, which is fitted both for learning a mode of knowledge more precisely and for teaching it to a beginner according to all the rules of the art of instruction. Our exposition can therefore be scholastic [*schulgerecht*] or popular. A scholar by profession must have the scholastic exposition. The scholastic is always first, and in scholastic perfection we observe all the rules of thoroughness; without them science would be mere trifling. But if we wish to communicate our knowledge to the world, the didactic precepts must be dropped, and those methods must be omitted which serve to maintain the didactic form. Some sciences cannot become popular, e.g. mathematics. Popular perfection, however, must not displace the scholastic; thoroughness must not be sacrificed in order to please the people. Pedantry, then, is the imperfection of wishing to present something to the public in the manner in which it is presented in the schools. Of all pedants the pedant in the learned field is the most tolerable. From him one can learn, for it is presupposed that he is learned. One does not indeed find in him so much agreeableness as in one who can express himself popularly, but",["Restated a few sentences for clarity. 'Der muß aber auch…' is taken to refer to the *Polymath* (the rational knower), since the preceding sentence speaks of Polymathie; the reference is a judgment.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'bestirnte' to 'bestimmte', 'se}\\' to 'sey', 'eine3S' to 'eine', 'erstem' to 'ersten', 'Avird' to 'wird', 'konmiuniziren', 'Volkommenlieit', 'Volle' to 'Volke'.","'Die Nichtkenntniß seiner Unwißenheit ist ein Vorwurf' rendered literally ('a reproach'); 'Wißenschaft' rendered 'science' (knowing/learning), and 'Wißen' 'knowing' to keep it distinct from *Erkenntniß* = knowledge.","'Zu der erstem' (read 'ersten') taken to mean polyhistory, the first-named, which is why philology is assigned to it; 'former' is my reading.","'Der muß aber auch…' and the sentence 'Beides kann heißen Pansophie' follow the German closely; the text is probably a loosely recorded student note.","'Die scholastische Vollkommenheit … tauglich ist ein Erkenntniß preziser zu lernen, als auch es … zu lehren' is irregular; smoothed to 'both … and'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('aber'); continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s06-004":["it is still always serviceable. Pedants in other fields are commonly empty heads. The opposite fault to pedantry is gallantry. He is gallant who attributes to someone merits that the latter would not attribute to himself, and does so from the superiority of his own refinement over the other. It extends also to manner of writing, and no one writes so gallantly as the French. It has something charming and seductive about it, which is dangerous, because one is thereby led to prize the applause of dilettanti more highly than that of connoisseurs; and hence also comes a shallowness among the French.",["Begins mid-sentence, continuing the previous passage ('aber … zu brauchen ist er doch immer').","'Verfeinrung' read as 'Verfeinerung'."]],"poelitz-s06-005":["The author's division of the magnitude of learned knowledge is poor, for he has already spoken before of the extensiveness [*Weitläuftigkeit*] of learned knowledge, and that is the same thing. A knowledge",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; 'Größe' as 'magnitude'."]],"poelitz-s06-006":["[may be great] *extensive* [1)] according to the quantity of learned knowledge, *intensive* according to its importance, that is, the magnitude of the knowledge may be according to its consequences. Logical importance is that which",["The passage is fragmentary and the opening is evidently corrupt or cut off; the bracketed '[may be great]' is supplied from the grammar of the final clause ('seyn') and is conjectural.","'1)' is a stray OCR mark (possibly an enumeration or note number), retained in brackets as doubtful.","'Wichtigkeit' = importance."]],"poelitz-s06-007":["furthers the logical perfection of the knowledge. Practical perfection consists in the utility that can be drawn from the knowledge, and this cannot be presupposed. Yet a knowledge can have sufficient logical perfection in a certain respect [*respective*] and still be logically imperfect in another regard. Content rests on the manifold which is combined as parts that make up a whole. Fruitfulness rests on the",["'Vohkommenheit' read 'Vollkommenheit'; 'Erkenntgezogen' read 'Erkenntniß gezogen' (the word 'Erkenntniß' is partly lost); 'Avas' read 'was'.","'Inhalt' rendered 'content'; 'Fruchtbahrkeit' 'fruitfulness'.","Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s06-008":["consequences. Otherwise one distinguishes weight [*Schwere*] and importance [*Wichtigkeit*]. For example, one may sooner burn even one's finger in a pot of hot water than in a roasting pan, if the intensive magnitude, i.e. the warmth, is the same.",["The opening 'der Folgen' is the continuation of the preceding fragment; the transcript's duplicated 'der' is silently corrected.","'Braatpfanne' read 'Bratpfanne', rendered 'roasting pan'; the example is obscure as recorded, and the sense of 'Schwere' (difficulty/weight) is probably 'difficulty' in the next passage. The text may be corrupt."]],"poelitz-s06-009":["Great objects do not always give great knowledge, e.g. God, and so too important objects do not always give important knowledge. A knowledge can become comparatively great according as someone has set himself an end, and for that very reason it can become too small, because we have set a great end.",["'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge' would be stiff here, so 'knowledge' is used.","'comparative' kept as 'comparatively'."]],"poelitz-s06-010":["Ambition is to have qualities suited to one's station. Deficiencies can be recognised only relatively to the object, and one need not",["Fragmentary; the connection of the first sentence with the second is unclear.","'object' is Latin in the source ('objecte'), here rendered in English."]],"poelitz-s06-011":["be ashamed of them, for it is partly not possible, partly not necessary, to exhaust everything. But a deficiency in what one has set before oneself as ends,",["Continues the previous passage; breaks off before the predicate, which appears in the next ('ein Fehler')."]],"poelitz-s06-012":["is a fault. Understanding [*Verstand*] does not come before the years. The worth of things one learns",["'Verstand' = understanding (here in the popular sense, 'wit/sense'). The final sentence breaks off."]],"poelitz-s06-013":["not before the years; and we come to the *quantum est quod nescimus* [how much it is that we do not know]",["The Latin tag is kept in italics with English supplied in brackets.","Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s06-014":["only when we have already gone through much. A knowledge can be difficult and not important, and a knowledge can be not difficult and yet important. Difficulty, then, is no worth of the knowledge, but also no objection against its worth. Importance rests on the magnitude of the consequences. The more consequences, the more importance.",["'denn' in the German is taken as 'dann/only then', joining the preceding fragment ('kommen wir denn, wenn…'); this is a judgment.","'Schwere' rendered 'difficulty' here, as the argument requires."]],"poelitz-s07-001":["On the truth of learned knowledge.",["A heading, translated as it stands."]],"poelitz-s07-002":["We come now to an essential condition of the perfection of a mode of knowledge, namely, to truth. Truth is the agreement of knowledge with the object. This, however, is merely a nominal definition [*Erklärung der Wortbedeutung*]. Truth is not the only perfection, nor is it in every respect the chief perfection. Certainly a knowledge that is wholly false is nothing, since through it I know no object; truth is therefore an inseparable condition of perfection. But the degree of truth required is not necessarily the same; it differs according to the different purposes in view. In the logical respect truth is the chief perfection.\n\nThe question 'What is truth?' amounts to this: that we are to state a sure, universal criterion of truth, which can also be serviceably applied. The demand is important, and seems a fair one to make of the logician. The material criterion would consist in the agreement of knowledge with the object; the formal criterion, in the agreement of knowledge with itself.\n\nIs there a universal material criterion of truth, or only a formal one? Answer: A universal material criterion is impossible, since it would have to be a criterion of truth irrespective of all objects, and would therefore have to abstract from every difference among them. Yet all truth consists in the agreement of knowledge with the object to which it is referred; for a knowledge that is true of one object may be false of another. A universal mark therefore cannot consist in the agreement of knowledge with the object, and consequently a universal material criterion of truth is not possible. Logic, moreover, cannot supply one in any case, since it abstracts from all content and regards the form alone. Hence only formal criteria of truth can be met with here.\n\nThe formal criteria are taken from the principle of contradiction. What contradicts itself is false. [They are taken] also from the principle of identity, as its consequence. If the opposite of a proposition contradicts itself, the proposition is true. But this is not sufficient. If a proposition does not contradict itself, it is not for that reason always true. [If the latter were the criterion, the proposition would indeed be true, but this does not always hold.] A mode of knowledge can be entitled possible only so far as it does not contradict itself; thus in possibility also there is a criterion of truth, but one far from sufficient, for I can say only that a knowledge is possible, not that the object is. Thoroughness [*Gründlichkeit*] too is a criterion, according to the principle of sufficient reason. For the merely possible [propositions] are nothing but arbitrary propositions which do not contradict themselves at all, but in which there is nothing further besides. This also is insufficient. These are the logical, objective, formal criteria of truth. We must make ourselves acquainted with objects through experience, and refer our knowledge to them.\n\nBesides these objective criteria we have also a subjective one, namely, the agreement of our judgment with the judgment of other men. With the jurist this is very frequent, but in philosophy it is ridiculous. Yet we cannot wholly dispense with it either. The formal criteria of logic are merely *conditiones sine quibus non*, namely, coherence and unity. The agreement of knowledge with itself, in accordance with the universal laws of the understanding which are contained in logic, is also a formal criterion. The rules of the agreement of knowledge with itself are",["The first sentences are kept separate; the second sentence of the opening ('Wahrheit ist die Uebereinstimmung…') was split from its comment 'das ist aber…' for clarity.","'Die Frage … heist so viel': restated as 'amounts to this'; 'in der Anwendung brauchbahres' rendered 'which can also be serviceably applied'.","'Wenn … Ist das lezte Criterium, so ist er zwar wahr': the German is elliptical and irregular; I supply 'If the latter were the criterion' in square brackets, taking 'das lezte' to refer to non-contradiction of the proposition.","'Denn die mögliche sind lauter willkührliche Säze': 'die mögliche' read as 'the merely possible [propositions]'; the connection to Gründlichkeit is loose in the transcript and the sense is partly conjectural.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'aUem' (allem), 'xmsres' (unseres), 'nehmlicli' (nehmlich). 'Kenntnißen' in the next passage and 'Erkenntniß' are rendered by 'knowledge'/'mode of knowledge' as the context suits.","'Gegen -theil' read as 'Gegentheil'; 'Erklärung der Wortbedeutung' rendered 'nominal definition' with the German given. The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is not completed; the paragraph divisions are mine."]],"poelitz-s07-003":["1) It must not contradict itself.",["'Es' is left as 'It'; the antecedent is presumably 'Erkenntniß' (knowledge) from the preceding sentence, which leads into this list, but the transcript does not state it."]],"poelitz-s07-004":["2) If a mode of knowledge is connected, as ground, with true consequences, this also is a criterion of truth.",[]],"poelitz-s07-005":["3) Among different modes of knowledge [*Kenntnisse*] there must be unity.",["'Kenntnißen' is Kant's/the transcriber's word here rather than 'Erkenntnisse'; rendered 'modes of knowledge' with the German in brackets."]],"poelitz-s07-006":["The *oppositum* of truth is falsity, and if this is held to be truth, it is error. An error therefore contains falsity together with a semblance [*Schein*] of truth. A judgment which confounds the semblance of truth with truth itself is erroneous. Semblance is a ground for holding false modes of knowledge to be true. The ground of semblance always lies in the subject. When the ground of holding-to-be-true [*Fürwahrhalten*] which lies in the subject is confounded with that which lies in the object, there is semblance. Anyone who is concerned lest he be involved in error must examine whether he may not have subjective grounds which he has wrongly taken to be objective.\n\nHow is error possible *formaliter*? Or, how is that form of thinking which is contrary to the understanding possible? It is hard to conceive how a power should depart from its essential laws. The understanding, therefore, cannot depart from its essential laws, and so far error would be impossible. Were we, then, to have no other source of knowledge than the understanding, we should never err; but error, as a judgment, has its seat in the understanding. Since, however, through the understanding alone we should have no object, we must have yet another power which gives us the material for thinking, and this is sensibility. It acts according to other laws. Sensibility and understanding, however, always act together, and this yields, as it were, a third, diagonal direction. We conclude, therefore, that sensibility is the cause of semblance and the source of all errors, although the understanding is also to some extent to blame, because it is not sufficiently attentive and cautious. No errors arise from sensibility itself, for the senses do not judge at all. One can be exempt from error in two ways: either by always judging correctly or by not judging at all. Error, then, lies neither in the understanding alone nor in the senses alone, but in the influence of sensibility upon the understanding, in that we do not notice this influence. Logic can say no more of sensibility than that it regards it as the subjective ground of judgments, and the understanding as their objective ground.\n\nIt is false to place the ground of errors in the limits [*Schranken*] of the understanding. Here lies the ground of ignorance, but not of errors. Limits are the necessary ignorance, and lack of knowledge, which cling to us; they are not the cause of errors. Nature has denied us much knowledge, but she does not cause error. It is our inclination to judge, and to decide something, where we are limited, that leads us into errors. A man, however, can never err wholly and completely; something true is always in his knowledge. All error is therefore partial. A total error would be a complete conflict with the laws of the understanding and of reason; in that case it could not come from the understanding at all, and so would be no judgment. This, then, is quite impossible.\n\n— If we speak of the criterion of truth, it can here consist only in the agreement of the knowledge with the rules of the understanding in general. The rules of the agreement of modes of knowledge with themselves are, 1) the principle of contradiction, and 2) the principle of sufficient reason, which are sufficient formally [*formaliter*] but not materially [*materialiter*]. Knowledge which contradicts itself is false; but if it does not contradict itself it is not in every case true. This is a negative criterion. If a mode of knowledge is connected with a true ground, and again if it has true consequences, then it is true. This is a positive criterion. In logic we have no others. If the opposite of a proposition is false, the proposition is certainly true. From mere non-contradiction no unity arises, and therefore I cannot infer truth from it; there must be added the connection of ground and consequences.\n\nOf the principle of sufficient reason as a criterion of truth, [drawn] from the connection of modes of knowledge as ground and consequences. The connection is twofold: *a priori*, a connection as consequence with its grounds; and *a posteriori*, a connection as ground with its consequences. A mode of knowledge is connected *a priori* if it is connected with grounds, and *a posteriori* if it is connected with consequences. How can one infer from the truth of the consequence to the truth of the mode of knowledge as ground? Negatively: if a consequence is false, the mode of knowledge is false. Under false consequences one also includes dangerous ones; but what is dangerous is not therefore false. That the inference is correct we see from this: if the ground were true, the consequence would also have to be true. The *principium rationis sufficientis* can be used well *a posteriori* as well as *a priori*. If the ground is true, the consequence is also true. But I cannot infer thus: because one consequence is true, the ground is also true; I can only say that if all the consequences are true, the ground is also true. For a mode of knowledge may contain something true from which precisely this one consequence flows. If, however, all the consequences are true, the mode of knowledge must also be true; for were there something false in it, a false consequence would also have to occur. This, then, is a positive inference. In the negative inference there is an advantage, for I need only derive one false consequence and the proposition is false; in the positive inference I am at a disadvantage, namely, the *onus probandi* rests upon me. *Ascendendo* I cannot properly infer at all, because one cannot know all the consequences of a mode of knowledge. This yields only a probable inference, and it occurs in all our hypotheses. *Directe* I cannot infer from the consequences to the truth of the proposition; but *indirecte* I can well infer, from the falsity of a consequence drawn from a proposition, to the truth of the opposite of that proposition; for since the former proposition is false, the opposite must be true. The principle of contradiction serves as a positive criterion in the case of the necessity of concepts.\n\n— The author now speaks of total and partial falsity. A mode of knowledge may be either totally or partially false, and it is sometimes of importance to us to attend also to partial truth. Although a partially false mode of knowledge is always false, degrees may nevertheless occur here. For example, he who believes in many gods errs, but he who believes in no God errs still more. In the case of errors one must always unfold and explain the semblance from which they sprang. Very few philosophers, however, have done this. They have indeed refuted the errors, but have not shown the semblance; thereby the sources of the errors, through which one may err again in other cases, are not removed. In every false mode of knowledge one finds something true. Sometimes it is not necessary to seek out the true element, but sometimes it is good to do so.\n\n— If a mode of knowledge extends to the whole object, or if I know everything that is in the object, the knowledge is totally true. If one wishes to distinguish total and partial truth, one must draw a distinction between knowledge as knowledge and knowledge with regard to the object. If the knowledge is partially true in the first respect, something false is certainly in it. In the second respect it may be true in itself; only we do not know the whole object through it.\n\n— A mode of knowledge is *tolerabiliter* true if it has a partial falsity which is, however, not wholly contrary to the end in view. In all errors one must look to what is true *tolerabiliter*.\n\n— A further distinction is made between exact and rough knowledge. A mode of knowledge is exact when it is adequate to its object, or when, with regard to its object, not the least error occurs; it is rough when errors may be present which are not prejudicial to the purpose. One must distinguish determining *late* and *stricte*. At the beginning it is sometimes necessary to determine *late*, especially in historical matters, but in modes of knowledge of reason everything must be *stricte*. To call something rough exact depends upon the purpose of the knowledge. Determination *late* always leaves a space over for error, which nevertheless has its determinate limits. Error occurs especially when determination *late* is held to be *stricte*.\n\nFrom exact knowledge the subtle can be further distinguished. Exactness is objective perfection; subtlety is subjective perfection. A mode of knowledge is subtle if it demands a high degree of attention and power of understanding in order to be perceived. Subtleties are often censured and called useless. In some matters subtlety is extremely necessary. But if one could have attained the end with less attention and less exertion of the understanding, it is called useless subtlety. One who clings to subtleties is *micrologisch* [given to trifling over minutiae]. Coarseness [*Grobheit*] is diametrically opposed to useless subtlety. A mode of knowledge is coarse in so far as it still has the marks of common understanding.\n\n— All our knowledge is historical or rational. The former we possess in so far as it is given to us. Subjectively rational modes of knowledge are those which are drawn from reason, as the mathematical and philosophical; objectively historical modes of knowledge are those which by their nature cannot be known *a priori*, but only through immediate experience, report and instruction. In some cases knowledge of reason is subjectively merely historical; here, however, we consider it as objectively rational. All certainty of historical knowledge is empirical, and that of knowledge of reason apodeictic. Apodeictic certainty is holding-to-be-true with the consciousness of necessity; empirical certainty is holding-to-be-true with the consciousness of contingency. Apodeictic truths from concepts are *dogmata*. Apodeictic truths from the construction of concepts are *mathemata*. Morals is very rich in dogmas, and theology also, in so far as it is a morals applied to God.\n\n— In mathematics, then, there are indeed apodeictic propositions, but not *dogmata*; these occur only in philosophy.",["OCR corrected silently: 'Sehern' read as 'Schein' (semblance of truth); 'Irthimi' as Irthum; 'der3» Saz' as 'der Satz'; 'materiahter liinreichend' as 'materialiter hinreichend'; 'Hegt' as 'liegt'; 'Dmgen' as 'Dingen'; 'bestirnte' as 'bestimmte'; 'glanbt' as 'glaubt'. The reading 'Sehern' is a conjecture, though a likely one.","'immer durch richtig urtheilen' looks corrupt (probably 'immer richtig urtheilen'); I translated 'by always judging correctly'. 'denn könnte er' is read as 'dann könnte er' ('in that case it could not').","Restated: the long sentence on *Schranken* and the one on the *Criterium* were divided, and a few sentences were smoothed. The brief heading 'Vom Saz des zureichenden Grundes...' is a fragment; '[drawn]' is supplied to complete it.","'Erkenntniß' is rendered 'knowledge', and 'ein Erkenntniß' / 'Erkenntniße' as 'a mode of knowledge' / 'modes of knowledge'. 'Fürwahrhalten' is rendered 'holding-to-be-true'; 'Schein' as 'semblance'; 'Schranken' as 'limits'; 'Kraft' as 'power'.","'Subjective rationale Erkenntniße ... historisch objective' is transcribed as it stands and may be a confused report by the student. 'Bei manchen ist die Vernunft Erkenntniß subjectiv blos historisch' is translated literally.","'Der Saz des Wiederspruchs dient zum positiven Criterio bei der Nothwendigkeit der Begriffe' is kept as it stands; its sense is obscure (the transcript may be defective).","Latin technical terms (*oppositum*, *formaliter*, *materialiter*, *a priori*, *a posteriori*, *onus probandi*, *ascendendo*, *directe*, *indirecte*, *late*, *stricte*, *tolerabiliter*, *dogmata*, *mathemata*) are left in italics. 'micrologisch' is German in the text, glossed in brackets. 'Z. E.' is expanded to 'for example'.","'Apodiktisch' is rendered 'apodeictic', in Kemp Smith's spelling."]],"poelitz-s07-007":["Apodeictic judgments are those whose truth is evident as sufficient from itself, that is, from inner grounds.",["Erhellet rendered 'is evident'; 'as sufficient' (als hinreichend) kept as in the text, though the construction is loose."]],"poelitz-s07-008":["Modes of knowledge [Erkenntnisse] whose truth has its grounds in experience, or in sensibility generally, are empirical.",["Erkenntniße in the plural is rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"poelitz-s07-009":["Hypotheses are judgments which we assume to be true in order to explain by them the sufficiency of certain consequences. Here, however, we must be cautious, for from a given effect we cannot infer a determinate cause. For example: false coins were found in the chest of a mint-master in England; he was executed, and after his death it came out that someone else had put them there.",["Restated the second sentence as two; the 'Denn' clause is kept as a separate sentence for clarity.","Zulänglichkeit rendered 'sufficiency'."]],"poelitz-s07-010":["On System.",[]],"poelitz-s07-011":["The author defines a system as a sum-total [Inbegriff] of dogmatic truths. The object makes no difference in systems, for everything must be made systematic if it is to be truth. In every system an idea must be present as the whole, which determines the division and the end; and this idea constitutes the systematic unity. If it is lacking, a manifold [of knowledge] is in the system connected only rhapsodically. That, however, is not yet tumultuary, for the latter is opposed to the methodical. Something can be methodical which is nevertheless not systematic. It is a mark of a system that, if anything is wanting in it, the gap is at once noticed, because we are supposed to have a concept of the whole. The system is therefore a touchstone of completeness and correctness. Truth is knowledge in a system; and common knowledge is any knowledge that is not a system. It may be very extensive, but it is not science [Wissenschaft]. We can therefore have historical science and rational science. Many believe that the systematic belongs only to exposition [Vortrag], and that knowledge itself need not arise systematically. Exposition concerns only the manner of communicating knowledge; but the origin of knowledge too is in many cases already systematic. — Aphorisms are detached propositions which are not members of a system. — The author speaks of aesthetic and of learned modes of knowledge. A whole of modes of knowledge as an aggregate is common knowledge. A whole of modes of knowledge as a system is learned knowledge, that is, when a whole is connected in accordance with a principle. For example, geography may be a rhapsody and also a system, but what makes it systematic is the globe. — False knowledge and error are also distinguished, for error is first the holding-true of false knowledge. In the case of error, therefore, besides the false knowledge there must be added semblance [Schein]. There are many kinds of semblance, but we can speak only of the logical, which arises from the agreement of the knowledge with the form. The form of a mode of knowledge may be correct while the content alone is false; and this logical semblance is commonly present in our knowledge.",["Divided the long passage into shorter sentences; the sentence on rhapsodic connection reads 'ein manifaltiges System' literally, which I render 'a manifold [of knowledge] ... in the system' by judgment.","'Probierstein' = touchstone; 'Fürwahrhalten' = holding-true; 'Schein' = semblance, as the context (logical illusion) suggests.","OCR 'undlrtum' corrected to 'und Irtum'; 'Erkenntnißes' left as 'knowledge'.","'oder wenn ein Ganzes verbunden ist gemäß einem Princip' is loosely attached in the German; I have joined it to the definition of learned knowledge. 'Kann Rhapsodie auch System seyn' is rendered as in the German, probably meaning it can be either; doubtful."]],"poelitz-s08-001":["On avoidable and unavoidable error.",[]],"poelitz-s08-002":["No error is in itself unavoidable. Ignorance, however, is at times unavoidable, for it rests not upon our will but upon the limits of our understanding. Still, there are cases in which we must judge; and if we then err, the error is relatively unavoidable but not absolutely so. — A falsity is manifest if it is clear to the common understanding; if it is not manifest, it is concealed and can be made manifest, and all refutations rest upon this. The error which has a manifest falsity is called absurd [ungereimt]; but [it is not so called] for him who accepts it as true, since to him it is not manifest. — This absurdity relates also to persons, in that they are thereby made unworthy to stand in the class of men who have only common understanding.",["'dem aber der ihn für wahr annimmt, ist er so nicht offenbar' is irregular; I supplied 'it is not so called' and reconstructed the sense from the context.","The last sentence is obscure: 'unwürdig ... in der Klaße ... zu stehen' may be a transcript slip; rendered literally."]],"poelitz-s08-003":["We must not say that the limits of our understanding are the cause of error. For, in the first place, an understanding that knows its limits will not go further than it can; and, secondly, one would then ascribe the cause of error to God. The limits of the understanding are, however, causes of ignorance.",["'und denn' (= 'und dann/zweitens') rendered as 'secondly'; the German has only 'erstens', so the numbering is mine."]],"poelitz-s08-004":["Every error nevertheless contains a partial truth, and not even the judgment of a deranged person is wholly false, for the understanding judges, that is, it declares that here or there it agrees with itself. —",["'des gestörten' rendered 'deranged person'; the German is literally 'the disturbed one'."]],"poelitz-s08-005":["Whoever wishes to free another from error must, first, *speciem evoluere*, that is, prove the falsity of a judgment held to be true; secondly, *speciem exponere*, that is, explain the semblance and show how the person was here deceived by the semblance.",["Latin terms kept; *species* here is the semblance (Schein), as the gloss indicates."]],"poelitz-s08-006":["On the clearness of modes of knowledge.",["Klarheit rendered 'clearness'."]],"poelitz-s08-007":["Marks [Merkmale] belong to clearness. Whatever can be regarded as a part of the whole possible representation of a thing is a mark of the thing. For example, red colour is a partial representation of the whole possible representation of a rose. Again, the harmfulness of the rose's exhalation is a partial representation, not of the whole actual representation which we commonly have of it (for few believe it), but of the possible one. Marks can therefore be found by analysis, in so far as they are partial representations of actual concepts; in so far as they are partial representations of possible concepts, they can be found only by synthesis, in that we add one to another; and here there is no limit, for what thing do we already know completely? We can therefore also define a distinct concept as one that has clear marks, or that can be resolved into marks. — Marks, according to their connection with one another, are divided into coordinate and subordinate. They are coordinate when each is represented as an immediate mark of the thing; they are subordinate when one mark is represented in the thing only by means of another. The former is entitled the connection of an aggregate, the latter the connection of a series. The aggregation of the marks constitutes the totality of a concept. Through the subordination of marks I know not the least more in the thing, but I do through aggregation. Through the subordination of marks I know up to the most universal; the series of marks subordinated one to another has limits (for I must at some point arrive at the simplest, which cannot be further explained), and distinctness through subordinate marks is the depth of distinctness. — A body is a whole, impenetrable and extended; these marks are *compartes* and coordinate marks. And the series of these coordinate marks is infinite. A mode of knowledge accordingly becomes distinct in two ways:",["Restated the long sentence on marks in shorter sentences where the German runs on; passage ends with a colon, continued in the next passages.","OCR corrections: 'VorsteUung' → Vorstellung, 'Begrif en' → Begriffen, 'coorclinirte' → coordinirte, 'hie' → hier, 'Vorstelungen' → Vorstellungen.","'Deutlichkeit' rendered 'distinctness'; 'deutlich' 'distinct'.","'compartes' is left in Latin; the reading is Kant's own term (parts that together make up a whole).","'Durch die Subordinirung ... bis aufs algemeinste' is elliptical; supplied 'I know up to' by judgment."]],"poelitz-s08-008":["1.) Through the aggregate of the coordinate marks; and here",["Fragment, continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s08-009":["distinctness grows extensively through the addition of each further",["Fragment; 'extensive' kept as *extensive* adverb rendered 'extensively'."]],"poelitz-s08-010":["mark.",[]],"poelitz-s08-011":["2.) Through the series of the subordinate marks; and there",[]],"poelitz-s08-012":["distinctness grows intensively. As agreeable as the first is, so dry",[]],"poelitz-s08-013":["is the last.",["'das lezte' = the second (the latter)."]],"poelitz-s08-014":["The depth of distinctness that arises here, however, contributes greatly to the thoroughness and certainty of our knowledge. It belongs to what is peculiar to logical perfection, whereas the former [extensive distinctness] belongs to aesthetic perfection. Extensive distinctness might be entitled the widespread distinctness. It is this that the poet in particular seeks, and for this purpose poetry is useful to read; for in logic too we can make use of widespread distinctness, since it is the easiest and the most entertaining. Profound distinctness, however, is a matter for philosophy. It is carried furthest in metaphysical inquiries, for here we are always asking for the why of the why. Through it our modes of knowledge become very dry, but all the more thorough.\n\nMarks [*Merkmale*] are grounds of knowledge. They are either (internal) marks for internal use, in so far as they serve to derive the manifold of the object itself or to explain the object in itself; or (external) marks for external use, in so far as they serve to compare objects with one another in respect of their identity and difference. By knowing the marks of the identity of things we form genera; by knowing the marks of the diversity of things we form species, or find the *differentia specifica*. Without a concept of the genus we cannot classify, and without a concept of the species we cannot specify. Marks that serve for internal use are good also for external use, but not conversely. The latter use is the more current, the former the more important.\n\nMarks are further divided, in respect of their relation to the thing itself, into affirmative and negative. There are negative and affirmative concepts, and so there must also be such marks. Marks are affirmative when I know what the thing is, negative when I know what it is not. In some sciences negative marks have great value, for example in natural theology. If the object is a lack, the concept is negative. When we know a thing distinctly by denying something of it, the marks are negative; when we affirm something of it, they are affirmative. It is the latter that we properly seek, for by their means we can know a thing in respect of its content with greater clearness. Negative marks are means of attaining distinctness by my excluding something from a thing; they serve to keep us from errors, whereas affirmative marks ground our knowledge. For example, 'the soul is not corporeal': by this I have not understood what the soul is, but have only removed an error, and this merit is just as important as that of the affirmative marks, for it is a *conditio sine qua non*. Negative marks can therefore be of importance only if they keep us from important errors into which we might easily fall. They are useless, then, if they keep us from an error that is impossible. Affirmative marks serve to understand concepts; negative marks, not to misunderstand them.\n\nWe can without disadvantage be ignorant of certain things that do not belong to our purpose. From ignorance we must distinguish voluntary ignoring, and this is one of the chief prudences in thinking. For example, the judge ignores it when the plaintiff accuses the defendant of all sorts of things that do not belong to the case and are meant only to win the judge to his side.",["The first sentence's 'die erstere' refers back to extensive distinctness (discussed just before the passage); I supplied '[extensive distinctness]' by judgment.","Erkenntnisse = 'modes of knowledge' (plural); Erkenntnißgründe rendered 'grounds of knowledge'. Merkmal is left as 'mark' with the German given once.","The long sentence on marks was divided into several, and the semicolons restructured; 'oder ohne Begrif vom genere...' (OCR/transcript 'oder') is rendered as 'Without...', the sense requiring 'denn/and'.","OCR 'cliff erentiam specificam' silently corrected to *differentiam specificam*; I gave the nominative *differentia specifica* in keeping with English usage.","'Gottesgelartheit' read as Gottesgelahrtheit, rendered 'natural theology'.","'Alsdenn sind sie unnüz, wenn...' translated as 'They are useless, then, if...'; the transcript's wording is slightly loose.","'removirt' rendered 'removed'; the Latin *conditio sine qua non* kept in italics."]],"poelitz-s09-001":["On important and unimportant marks.",["Merkmale = 'marks'."]],"poelitz-s09-002":["A mode of knowledge is important in so far as it is a ground of great use. Importance is therefore only relative, for what is important to one person is not so to another. (Absolute importance, or dignity, belongs to nothing but morality and the religion founded upon it.) One person has great ends, another many ends. Marks have importance in respect of internal use when they suffice for knowing a great deal in the thing itself; in respect of external use, when they serve to know its similarity to many other things and to distinguish it from many others. To convince someone of the importance of an end is difficult; to convince him of the importance of the means to an end that is already important to him is easy. Here we are speaking only of the relative importance of means.\n\nA mode of knowledge is important if it serves to make another mode of knowledge clear, and is then formally important. If it is a ground of great consequences, it is materially important; if it is a ground of numerous consequences, it is fruitful. Marks are sufficient when they accomplish everything in respect of the purpose in view. Sufficiency therefore rests on this, that they accomplish not merely something but everything, which, however, is always relative to certain ends.\n\nMarks are necessary if they cannot be separated from the concept of a thing. Those that are not *constitutiva* are contingent; the latter are entitled non-essential, the former essential. *Essentia est complexus notarum necessariarum interne sufficientium.* We are speaking here, however, of the essence of the concept, not of the essence of things.\n\nAmong the necessary marks there is a distinction. Some belong to a thing as grounds of other marks of one and the same thing; others belong to a thing as consequences of other marks. For example, the three angles belong to the triangle as *rationata* of the three sides, while the three sides belong to it as *rationes*. *Notae quae sunt rationes notarum aliarum sunt essentialia, quae sunt rationata notarum aliarum sunt attributa.* The *attributa* must therefore always be derived from the *essentialia*; they too belong to the essence, but not as *constitutiva*, rather as *consectaria*.\n\n*Extraessentialia* are also of two kinds. Either they belong to internal determination, and then they are *modi*, or to external relation, and then they are *relationes*. For example, learning is a *nota extraessentialis interna*; it belongs to internal determination. To be master or servant, on the other hand, is a *nota extraessentialis externa*; it indicates only the external relation, for man is by nature neither master nor servant, and these are therefore *relationes*.\n\nThe essence of things in themselves is the real essence; the essence of things according to the concepts we have of them is the logical essence. Although we cannot have insight into the real essence of things, we can readily know their logical essence, for it is nothing but the *complexus notarum omnium conceptuum primitive constituentium*. What is required for the logical essence is therefore the totality of the marks that belong to the thing not as consequences but as grounds, that is, as *constitutiva primitiva*. If, for example, we wish to determine the logical essence of body, we need not study it in nature; we take what the concept we have of it contains in itself. The logical essence concerns nothing but the analysis of the concept into its marks and attention to the *primitiva*. Our complaints about the limits of our understanding are thereby restricted, namely the complaint that we cannot have insight into the real essence, for that goes beyond our understanding. The real essence is otherwise also entitled nature, of which no mention can be made here in logic.",["Erkenntniß in the first sentence rendered 'mode of knowledge' (a single item of knowledge, not knowledge in general); the passage is loose on whether 'mark' is meant.","Several long sentences divided and paragraphed; 'Hinlänglichkeit/zureichend' rendered 'sufficiency/sufficient'.","'Merkmale sind noth wendig...die nicht constitutiva sind' is irregular; 'those that are not *constitutiva*' (I take 'die' to refer to marks) and 'diese...jene' resolved as 'the latter...the former' (non-essential/essential).","'Endzwecke' rendered 'ends'; 'Zweck' 'end' or 'purpose' by context.","OCR corrections: 'WTesen' as Wesen, 'üin' as ihn, 'fast' as faßt, 'Begrif s' as Begrifs, 'eßentia' normalised to *essentia* etc. in the Latin.","In 'Das Wesen der Dinge an sich selbst ist realwesen' the Latin tags are kept; 'Hiedurch...eingeschrenkt' is rendered loosely as 'restricted'."]],"poelitz-s09-003":["The author now speaks of clear and obscure modes of knowledge. This subject does not belong here, for logic cannot give us rules concerning representations of which we are not conscious at all. Logic does not teach us to make modes of knowledge clear, but to make them distinct. We must, however, be conscious of our knowledge; that is, it must be clear. A concept is entitled obscure if it does not have sufficient clarity for our purpose. There is, then, a logical obscurity, which is very different from the psychological. It is comparative obscurity, therefore, with which logic is concerned. We must always become relatively obscure if we wish to ascend to an uncommon degree of clarity. That too is entitled obscure which the common understanding does not, but the speculative understanding does, perceive. It is remarkable that the understanding often works in the dark. That this is true we can see from the improper expression: I feel that this or that is true, though one can give no ground for it. The understanding is not conscious of its reflection, but it is conscious of the result of that reflection, that something is true or false. The latter is entitled a scruple: one is conscious of a doubt against a thing, but cannot give the ground for it. If one can give the ground, it is entitled an objection. All truth and falsity lies in judgment; it cannot, therefore, be felt, and the expression is accordingly quite false. We must seek to discover what reflections the understanding has made in the dark, by which it has been led to accept or to reject something. For this a guiding thread is required: I go back to the nearest thing known, and see whether what is unknown to me is not connected with it. This procedure is entitled *evolution*, and is often very difficult, in our own case as in that of others. The author also speaks of fullness [*Ausführlichkeit*]. The division relates properly not to clearness but to distinctness. All distinctness consists in the clearness of the marks. When all the marks have attained clearness, my distinct concept of the thing is *adequate*. Distinctness through the coordination of the marks is of a different kind from that through their subordination. The adequate may rest on the coordination of the marks, and then it is *completudo*, or on their subordination, and then it is *profunditas*. Both intensive and extensive distinctness belong to logic.",["'Erkenntnißes' (\"unseres Erkenntnißes\") taken as a slip for the singular 'Erkenntnis'; 'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge' in the plural.","'Das leztere nennt man Scrupel … ist das leztere, so nennt man es Einwurf': the first 'leztere' evidently refers to the case where no ground can be given, and the second (probably a transcript slip for 'ist das erstere/ist ein Grund anzugeben') to the case where it can; I translated by the sense required. Doubtful reading.","OCR 'Avieder' read as 'wieder' (= wider, 'against'); 'Avenige' elsewhere = 'wenige'. 'Relative dunkel mus man immer werden' rendered as given, with 'werden' retained; the sense is somewhat compressed.","'Ausführlichkeit' left in German in brackets since the text breaks off from it to the division; I rendered it 'fullness'.","'Profunditaet' normalised to Latin *profunditas*; 'completudo' is the transcript's form (classical *completio*) and is kept.","'Der Reflexion des Verstandes ist er sich nicht bewust': 'er' = the understanding; I made the subject explicit."]],"poelitz-s10-001":["On Total and Partial Clearness",[]],"poelitz-s10-002":["*In cognitione quadam notae singulae possunt esse obscurae, universae, clarae.* [In a certain piece of knowledge the single marks may be obscure while the marks taken together are clear.] This is the case with an indistinct representation. For example, if one mixes blue and yellow, green results. We are conscious of the green as a whole, but not of the parts of which it consists. Again, when one uses the word 'right' [*Recht*], one always has a clear concept; but what it contains in itself few know. We have, then, a multitude of representations which are clear to us as wholes but not in their parts. Philosophy, however, must carry clearness as far as possible. If we put a concept together arbitrarily, it is clear to us also in its parts, for we know the parts of which it is composed.",["The Latin is the transcript's; 'eße' and 'vniversae' normalised to *esse*, *universae*. The bracketed English gloss is my supplied translation of the Latin.","'Avenige' (OCR) read as 'wenige'. 'Begrif ,' spacing corrected. 'Zusammen -sezen' rejoined.","'Willkührlich' rendered 'arbitrarily'; 'Recht' could also be 'law' or 'justice', here left as 'right'."]],"poelitz-s11-001":["On Vivacity",["'Lebhaftigkeit' rendered 'vivacity' (throughout: 'vivacity' for the aesthetic perfection)."]],"poelitz-s11-002":["The greater logical clearness is distinctness; the greater aesthetic clearness is vivacity. There may be vivacity of the mere impression or of the idea; this consists in the multitude of marks which make up the representation, for every mark produces a movement in us. Certain representations are connected with the interest of the mind, that is, they facilitate the free play of the powers of the mind. In an author we censure dryness when he does not accommodate himself to taste, and drowsiness when he brings forward nothing that stimulates our attention. He too is entitled dry who presents his knowledge merely in logical concepts. Vivacity is therefore entirely distinct from logical perfection and belongs to aesthetics. It is, as it were, the *vehiculum* of logic, and serves to introduce it; yet it must be employed with caution, lest the effect of the logical aim be hindered. Vivacity is thus extensive and intensive. The intensive degree is entitled strength and emotion; the extensive serves to give our concept more intuition, or to make it distinct *in concreto*, and it is therefore very important. The magnitude of intensive vivacity rests merely on feeling; through it we do not know the object any better, but are in the most unfavourable condition for knowing and judging things. Vivacity which rests on intuition requires insight, learning and thoroughness. The extensive can be produced even by a common head.\n\nSince we have said that logic makes concepts distinct, the question arises in what manner it makes them distinct. By our author and Wolff the making distinct is always regarded as analytic; they suppose, that is, that it arises merely through analysis of the concept. It does so arise in respect of the marks which we already thought in the concept; but in respect of the marks which are added to the concept as parts of the whole possible concept, it arises through synthesis. Through analytic distinctness our knowledge is not extended; we merely learn to distinguish better what was already contained in our knowledge. For example, when I say: body is an extended, impenetrable thing which has a figure, I have added nothing to the concept of body, but have only analysed what lay in it before. Logic is concerned with the form of distinctness, so that the content remains the same and the knowledge does not grow in respect of content. The analytic procedure for producing distinctness is the first and chief, and is that which is logical. Sometimes, however, it is necessary to extend one's concept; distinctness then increases, and synthetically. For example: virtue is the harmony of the power of choice [*Willkühr*] with reason; that one always thinks under the concept of virtue. If I add further that it brings advantages with it, then, although this is only a contingent property, I have made my concept synthetically distinct.\n\nThere is a difference which must be well noted between the proposition 'to make a distinct concept' (from which synthetic distinctness springs) and 'to make a concept distinct' (from which analytic distinctness springs). In the former there are as yet no marks; I begin, therefore, with the parts and proceed to the whole, and here I extend my knowledge in respect of content. In the latter it does not grow in respect of content; only the form is altered. Some maintain that making distinct harms energy; this is false, for the more distinctly I see into a thing, the stronger and more vivid it is; only the analysis must not go so far that the object finally vanishes altogether.\n\nThe mathematician proceeds synthetically, the philosopher analytically. To synthesis belongs the making distinct of objects, to analysis the making distinct of concepts. Here the whole precedes the parts, there the parts precede the whole. Sometimes one proceeds synthetically even when the concept is given, if one is not yet satisfied with the marks; this often occurs with propositions of experience.",["Divided into paragraphs for readability; the German is one block. Long sentences were left largely intact but a few were split.","'Vivacity' for *Lebhaftigkeit*; 'Deutlichkeit' = distinctness, 'Klarheit' = clearness; 'Merkmal' = mark.","'Gemütskräfte' rendered 'powers of the mind'; 'Willkühr' = 'power of choice', German given in brackets.","'Einsicht, Gelehrsamkeit und Gründlichkeit' = insight, learning, thoroughness.","'Das denkt man sich immer unter dem Begrif der Tugend' is left rather literal; the sense is that the definition is what is always thought in the concept.","'Erfarungs Säzen' rendered 'propositions of experience'. 'Teüen' (OCR) read 'Teilen' in the passage as 'Teilen'. Transcript's 'Objecte' vs 'Gegenstand' both rendered 'object'.","'Einen deutlichen Begrif machen' / 'einen Begrif deutlich machen' kept as the contrast of the original, rendered with English word order that preserves it."]],"poelitz-s11-003":["Synthesis and Analysis",[]],"poelitz-s11-004":["Analytic judgments are explicative, synthetic judgments, however, are ampliative. All analytic judgments are judgments *a priori*; and all judgments of experience are synthetic.",["'erläuternde' = explicative; 'erweiternde' = ampliative (extending), the latter chosen to match Kant's Latin *ampliativa*."]],"poelitz-s11-005":["The synthetic are either *a priori* or *a posteriori*. In arithmetic and geometry there are almost nothing but synthetic [judgments; the text reads *Begriffe*, 'concepts'] *a priori*, that is, my modes of knowledge can here be extended independently of all experience.",["The text reads 'synthetische Begriffe a priori'; I kept 'concepts' in the bracket as the transcript's wording, though 'Urteile' (judgments) is clearly meant, as the preceding passage shows. Possibly a transcript slip."]],"poelitz-s11-006":["The author now speaks of comprehending [*begreifen*] and calls it *concipere*. That word properly means to know something through concepts, i.e. through the understanding; for otherwise we find no German word for *comprehendere*. But not much depends on this. We shall take the degrees by which our knowledge can be raised in respect of its object. The first degree is to represent something to oneself. The second is to perceive [*wahrnehmen*], or to represent with consciousness, *percipere*. The third is to know something, *cognoscere*. The fourth is to understand something, *intelligere*, i.e. to know something through the understanding by means of concepts. This might also be entitled *concipere*, and it is very different from comprehending [*begreifen*]. I can conceive [*concipiren*] anything whatever, e.g. a *perpetuum mobile*, although its impossibility is demonstrated in mechanics. The fifth degree is to know something through reason, *perspicere*, when I have insight into its ground. In many things we get as far as understanding, but not as far as insight. Our modes of knowledge become ever fewer in number the more we seek to perfect them. The sixth degree is *comprehendere*, to comprehend [*begreifen*]: to know something through reason in the degree that is sufficient for our purpose. If something is known in such a degree that it is sufficient for every purpose, it is entitled simply comprehending, and that surpasses the human faculty. When we learn something, we must of necessity comprehend whatever belongs to the purpose in view; otherwise the learning is in vain and all use of it falls away. A synthetically distinct concept, in which no [mark] is contained in another and none is lacking, may be entitled a definition. Remark. We have a use of reason which seems sufficient for comprehending something when the thing is given. Yet on closer investigation we find that we should not have had insight into it *a priori*, and this is very much the case with investigators of nature. I should therefore be comprehending something which I could have determined *a priori*. What I cannot determine *a priori* I also do not comprehend, even though I can explain it.",["The first sentence is run together in the German; I have divided it, and divided the later run of sentences on the degrees as well.","*begreifen* is rendered 'comprehend' where it matches *comprehendere*. For *concipere* I use 'conceive' for the verb *concipiren*, and give the German where the contrast between the two terms matters.","'[mark]' is supplied: the German has only 'keiner' / 'keiner ... fehlt', and *Merkmal* is the evident antecedent.","'Remark' renders *Anmerkung*. 'Erkenntniß' in 'unser Erkenntniß' is singular, hence 'our knowledge'; the plural *Erkenntniße* is 'modes of knowledge'.","*Vermögen* is rendered 'faculty' here. 'Is entitled' renders *nennen/heißen*."]],"poelitz-s12-001":["On Total and Partial Distinctness.",[]],"poelitz-s12-002":["The totality of extensive distinctness rests on the multitude of coordinate marks, and that of intensive distinctness on the multitude of subordinate marks.",["*Merkmale* = 'marks'. The second 'that of' is supplied for smoothness, since the German drops the repeated noun."]],"poelitz-s13-001":["On Complete and Adequate Concepts",[]],"poelitz-s13-002":["A concept is distinct if its marks are clear. The distinct concept which contains all the marks that exhaust the whole concept is a complete concept, and this perfection is entitled *completudo*. If no mark is lacking to a distinct concept, there may still be a fault, namely that one mark is too many. The perfection by which a concept does not have too many marks is entitled *precision*. A *cognitio simul completa et precisa est adaequata* [knowledge that is at once complete and precise is adequate]. Wolff and his followers define it [the adequate concept] as a concept whose marks are distinct. This is indeed a high degree of distinctness, but it is not a fixed, determinate concept, only a wavering one; for I can think further marks again of the marks of the marks, and so on, and the concept is thereby never fixed. The correct definition is: *cognitio adaequata objectum si habet omnes notas quae conceptum exhauriunt sed non abundantes* [knowledge is adequate if it has all the marks of the object which exhaust the concept, but none superfluous]. By precision we can become certain that there are neither too many nor too few marks. The lack of precision rests on tautology, in that I state the same mark twice over, only in a different way. If I say, e.g., 'God is infinite, almighty', etc., etc., omnipotence is already contained in the concept of infinity. There are cases where precision causes some obscurity; but to a profound thinker it is all the more valuable, for precise concepts are always adequate to the thing, and in one skilled in art the lack of it is a great fault. At the end of this section the author speaks of the concepts of a lucid mind. This is a subject which, though it does not belong to logic, we shall also take up. By lucidity [*Helligkeit*] is understood popular distinctness combined with the scholastic. The more precision there is in a concept, the more it conforms to the schools. Logical distinctness may also be called scholastic, and aesthetic distinctness popular. Popular distinctness consists in combining representations *in abstracto* with those *in concreto*, and for this much talent is required. Many authors are very popular, but in so being they have neglected what is scholastically correct, and we cannot rely upon them. The most lucid mind is the one who, together with logical perfection, is at the same time so distinct that he can be understood even by the commonest understanding.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Merlanale' to *Merkmale*, 'zAvar' to *zwar*, 'bestirnter' to *bestimmter*, 'Tavtologie' to *Tautologie*, 'öhiezu' to *hiezu*, 'Averden' to *werden*.","I read 'ihn' as referring to the *adäquate Begriff* and say so in brackets. The Latin 'objectum' (as printed; classical *obiectum*) is left as it stands; the Latin definition is somewhat elliptical, and the bracketed English is my reading of it.","'Wolff' for 'Wolf'. *schulgerecht* and *scholastisch* are both rendered 'scholastic' or 'conforming to the schools', since Kant uses them as equivalent here. *Helligkeit* is rendered 'lucidity'.","Sentences divided at several points; the German is a single long paragraph."]],"poelitz-s14-001":["On the Certainty of Modes of Knowledge.",["*Erkenntniße* is plural, hence 'modes of knowledge'."]],"poelitz-s14-002":["There are three degrees of holding to be true [*Fürwahrhalten*]; it is either",["The passage breaks off at 'either'; the three alternatives follow in the next passages."]],"poelitz-s14-003":["1.) problematic, and then it is entitled opining [*meinen*]; or",["'denn' is read as 'dann' (then)."]],"poelitz-s14-004":["2.) assertoric, when I regard my knowledge not as possibly true but as actually true, and then it is entitled believing [*glauben*]; or",["'denn' is read as 'dann' (then)."]],"poelitz-s14-005":["3.) apodictic, when it is bound up with the concept of necessity, so that I declare it irrevocably true; that is entitled knowing [*wissen*].",[]],"poelitz-s14-006":["The first degree is Opining.",[]],"poelitz-s14-007":["Opining is an objectively insufficient holding to be true, held with consciousness [of this insufficiency]. For the most part we begin with opinions in our modes of knowledge. Sometimes one has an obscure presentiment, where we ourselves do not know what we really think, although the thing seems to bear a mark of truth. Opining is a provisional judgment, and we cannot easily dispense with it; but one must take care not to hold opinions to be anything more than opinions, and this too is to be observed in the case of hypotheses or philosophical opinions. In many sciences it is not permitted to opine, e.g. in metaphysics, mathematics, morals, etc., etc. In physics there are many opinions, especially regarding causes and events. From our definition it follows that opining is also a subjectively insufficient holding to be true, since we are conscious that it is objectively insufficient.",["'[of this insufficiency]' is supplied to make plain what the consciousness is of; the German 'mit Bewustsein unzureichendes' is elliptical, and the last sentence of the passage supports this reading.","Sentence divisions are mine; 'Begebenheiten' rendered 'events'."]],"poelitz-s14-008":["The second degree is Believing.",[]],"poelitz-s14-009":["Belief is a holding to be true which is indeed subjectively sufficient but objectively insufficient. Belief may be theoretical or practical. The first is grounded on the testimony of others and is the historical; the other is grounded on interest, but also on reason, and that is moral belief.",["'Glauben' is rendered 'belief' as noun and 'believing' as verb. 'Das ist der moralische Glaube' is read as referring to the practical kind."]],"poelitz-s14-010":["Remark. In mathematics one may also believe what a great man has said, but not in philosophy, because there are not there such means of detecting a false step.",[]],"poelitz-s14-011":["The third degree is Knowing.",[]],"poelitz-s14-012":["Knowing is contradistinguished from belief. One may have a belief ever so rational, and yet one cannot say that one knows. For knowing is the objectively sufficient holding to be true; I then have knowledge [*erkenne*] of something from logical grounds. Sometimes I have knowledge [*erkenne*] of something from practical grounds and believe it quite firmly, yet I do not yet know [*weiß*] it. Many truths of religion are, for example, of this kind.",["*erkennen* is rendered 'have knowledge of' and *wissen* 'know', to keep the two distinct in English; this is a choice, since Kant's wording runs them close together.","'Contradistinguished' keeps the Latinate *contradistinguirt*."]],"poelitz-s14-013":["Persuasion [*Überredung*] is the subjective [aspect] of holding to be true, in so far as no objective ground is contained in it. To be persuaded is therefore to be conscious of a subjective ground. In persuasion, then, there are no logical grounds, that is, no grounds valid for everyone. Conviction [*Überzeugung*] is the state of the subject in which it is impossible for him to hold the opposite to be true. Here there is a holding to be true that is subjectively necessary; and so it must also have its ground in the object. All conviction, or complete holding to be true, is of two kinds. Either it can be communicated, and then it is logical; or it cannot. In the latter case it rests either on merely subjective grounds, and then it is persuasion and aesthetic; or on objective and subjective practical grounds, that is, grounds which determine the will, and this is practical belief [*Glaube*].\n\nWhat is the difference between logically and practically sufficient conviction? If we are free from all interest, and conviction is nevertheless present in us, it is logical; whether we have freed ourselves from the interest, or the conviction never had any, makes no difference. This conviction is also entitled speculative. If, however, we have an interest in it, it is practical; that is, it is sufficient to determine us to something, but we cannot communicate it to anyone else, because interest is something subjective, and we cannot bring others to take an interest in what we ourselves have an interest in.\n\nTo be prepossessed [*praeoccupirt*] is to have in one's judgment a subjective ground which determines one's assent before the objective grounds have even been examined. Such prepossessions are very common in man. The teachings of youth always prepossess us, and we can only with difficulty free ourselves from them. We are further prepossessed by resentment, hatred, enmity and partiality [*Praedilection*], and the like, which exercise a very strong influence upon us. In holding to be true the hardest thing is to see whence it proceeds, whether from sensible influence or from the understanding; and often it is only by feeling that we ourselves distinguish persuasion from conviction.\n\nIn opining [*Meinen*] we do not hold the mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] to be even subjectively sufficient; in belief we do. Practical belief is often firmer than all knowledge [*Wissen*]. In the latter we still listen to counter-grounds, but in practical belief we do not. For example, belief in God and in another world is firmer than all knowledge, because we have so much interest in it. Practical conviction is the greatest possible, and only a few propositions are capable of it, those which are entitled morally certain. These are the propositions which are the ground of all morality, and which, when we follow them, agree very closely with our conscientiousness. Practical belief is therefore a firm, unalterable conviction, and this because no opponent can refute a morally certain proposition, although objections are raised against it.\n\nSensible certainty can be divided into the certainty of the senses and the certainty of experience, or empirical certainty. The senses alone do not constitute experience; this requires the judgment of the understanding upon the connection of sensible representations. Senses and understanding are the sources of our knowledge. Through the senses we have appearances; the understanding connects them, and their relation, or their unity, is experience. Experience is therefore the known connection of appearances. All experience accordingly contains judgments which come from the understanding.\n\nLogicians have busied themselves with deciding the question of the certainty of the senses. The question here is not whether there is anything true in the object which the senses represent to us, but whether they have objects at all, or whether they merely deceive us through illusion [*Schein*], with no object corresponding. This question does not belong to logic, since logic is not concerned with objects, but to metaphysics, where illusion is discussed. Yet from logic we can already see that neither falsity nor truth lies in the senses, since they do not judge at all. The question that arises here is rather that of certainty. An empirical mode of knowledge is a knowledge through the understanding of objects which are represented through sensibility. All empirical certainty therefore concerns the relation of sensible representations, and with this we must let the matter rest.\n\nSensible knowledge is distinguished from rational knowledge when its object is an object of reason, as with morals, which is therefore rational *materialiter*. Logic alone, however, is rational both *materialiter* and *formaliter*, in so far as it has the understanding for its object. Our modes of knowledge may concern objects of experience, and yet the certainty may be empirical or rational. (Empirical certainty is either *originarie*, when I know something from my own experience, or *derivative* empirical, when I know it from the experience of another.) Reason adds necessity to all certainty, which experience cannot do. For example, that a thing is useful experience teaches; but that it must necessarily be useful, reason alone teaches. We cannot have rational certainty of everything; but where we can have it, we must prefer it far to the empirical. We must not, however, confuse rational certainty with the empirical. One is rationally certain of what one would have seen *a priori* even without experience.\n\nRational certainty is again of two kinds. Since our modes of knowledge are either from concepts, and discursive, or from the construction of concepts, and intuitive, the certainty also is discursive or intuitive. The intuitive is mathematical, the discursive philosophical. Empirical certainty is assertoric, rational certainty apodeictic; that is, it is the certainty of a mode of knowledge which is combined with the consciousness of necessity. Mathematical certainty is further entitled evident, because the object is presented in intuition. An intuitive mode of knowledge is clearer than a discursive. Both are equally certain, but not equally clear; that is, the kind of their certainty is different.\n\nThe author goes on to speak of the *modi* of holding to be true. A *modus* is the difference in the manner in which I hold something to be true. The *modi* are as follows: 1.) Something is assumed [*angenommen*] when a thing receives assent without one's knowing whether it is certain.",["Paragraph breaks and some sentence divisions are mine (e.g. the long sentence on the two kinds of conviction was split); the German runs on continuously.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Avelchem' (welchem), 'nrnß' (muß), 'immöglich' (unmöglich), 'Avir/Avar' (wir), 'geAvis' (gewiß), '«erden' (werden), 'verbindt' (verbindet), 'klärer' (klarer). Elsewhere 'Erkenntniß' (singular) is rendered 'mode of knowledge' where it is a count noun, 'knowledge' otherwise.","'Fürwahrhalten' rendered 'holding to be true'; 'Glaube' = 'belief'; 'Wissen' = 'knowledge'; 'Meinen' = 'opining'.","'Das Subjective des Fürwahrhaltens': 'aspect' is supplied in brackets to complete the sense.","'Hier ist ein subjectiv nothwendiges Fürwahrhalten, also muß sie ...': 'sie' (Ueberzeugung) shifts from the neuter noun; I rendered 'it'. The 'also' inference is Kant's/the student's, kept as stated.","'aesthetisch' kept as 'aesthetic' (here: pertaining to feeling/sensibility, not communicable). 'Schein' rendered 'illusion'.","'Alle empirische Gewisheit ... bewenden laßen' rendered 'let the matter rest'. The sentence 'Hier kommt vielmehr die Frage von der Gewisheit vor' is slightly loose in the transcript; rendered as it stands.","Passage ends with item 1 of the *modi*; items 2 and 3 are in the following passages."]],"poelitz-s14-014":["2.) Something is settled [*ausgemacht*] when it is assumed because its truth is known.",["'Ausgemacht' rendered 'settled'; the sense is that the assumption now rests on known truth."]],"poelitz-s14-015":["3.) Something is undeniable [*unläugbar*] if it is impossible to hold it to be false.",[]],"poelitz-s14-016":["These are the three degrees of assent. When something is settled [*ausgemacht*], it is presupposed to be true both objectively and subjectively. Propositions that are undoubted are not called settled, because no controversies have ever been raised against them; for example, the Pythagorean theorem. A mode of knowledge can be thorough [*gründlich*] in different ways; for example, a thorough moral discourse is of a different kind from a mathematical one. Thoroughness does not signify a logical completeness of certainty. A mode of knowledge is thorough if it has the perfection that is appropriate to its nature and purpose. Certain modes of knowledge are shallow [*seicht*], and these are also called by another name, superficial, although the two expressions differ greatly. The first indicates a lack of depth in the knowledge. What offers itself at once to the common understanding is often quite appropriate to the purpose. But suppose a mode of knowledge is to go beyond the perfection suited to the purpose for a common understanding, and yet one takes only what is equally distinct to the common understanding. Then it is superficial.\n\nThe author now speaks of assent, of the withholding of assent, and of the postponement of our judgment. The expressions 'I give assent', 'I reject', 'I withhold my assent' indicate that there is something voluntary in our judgment, in that we hold something true because we will it. Or, in short, the question is whether willing has an influence on our judgment. The will has no immediate influence on our understanding, as is shown, for example, by mathematical truths, which remain true even if they are repugnant to us. But it does have an influence on the use of our understanding, in spurring it to the investigation of a truth or in holding it back from it. It has none on the understanding's conviction. Since, however, conviction depends greatly on the use of our understanding, the will has an indirect influence on conviction also. If the will had such an influence as to convince us of what we gladly wished, we should constantly fashion for ourselves chimeras of a happy condition, and should always hold them true. But it cannot contend against convincing proofs of things that we do not gladly wish; these always remain in the right.",["Sentences broken up in the last section for clarity; 'Er' in 'Er kann aber nicht…' is resolved as 'the will' (der Wille), since the argument concerns its power against proofs.","'deßelben' (conviction 'of it') is referred to the understanding (Verstand), the nearest antecedent that fits the argument.","'Ein Erkenntniß' rendered 'a mode of knowledge'; 'Erkenntniß' alone as 'knowledge'.","The last clause, 'die behalten doch immer recht', is loosely constructed in the transcript; 'these' is supplied for the proofs.","'Gründlichkeit' is rendered 'thoroughness' and 'gründlich' 'thorough'; 'seicht' and 'superficiell' are given as 'shallow' and 'superficial'.","'Zweckmäßige Vollkommenheit' is rendered 'perfection suited to the purpose'."]],"poelitz-s14-017":["On the suspension of judgment [*De suspensione judicii*]",["Latin heading kept in italics with the English beside it in square brackets."]],"poelitz-s14-018":["If we are at times able to discover errors in our knowledge, we may by chance come, through comparison with other modes of knowledge, to distrust our judgment. From this arises *suspensio judicii nostri*, which is the resolve not to let a provisional judgment become a determining one. I judge definitively if I determine outright: this is true. I judge problematically if I declare that I have grounds for assuming something to be true which are, however, not yet sufficient. The consciousness that my judgment is problematic is *suspensio judicii*. The suspension can take place with a twofold aim: (1) in order to seek out the grounds of the determining judgment, *suspensio judicii indagatoria*; (2) or never to judge, which is *renunciatio judicii*. The first is entitled critical, the other sceptical suspension.\n\nOne can also demand and command assent. That is, the other, once he has seen my grounds, cannot reject them without falling into absurdity; the mathematician is an example. The philosopher does not have this good fortune; his propositions too are apodictically certain, but not evident. In philosophical modes of knowledge one can give one's assent only conditionally, [waiting] to see whether everything will maintain itself in the sequel. For a proposition often seems quite correct, yet difficulties are found in its use, that is, when one draws consequences from it. One must always investigate how a mode of knowledge behaves in the whole of its use, and this always requires a completion and a survey of the whole. Only a practised power of judgment can withhold assent in accordance with maxims, and this is found only with advancing age. Our understanding is eager to extend itself and to enrich itself by judgments. Hence it comes about that one holds true a thing that has the semblance of truth. This way of judging is hard to remove in lively minds. When such a mind has become wise through harm, however, it will be careful not to give its assent quickly, having often had to take it back.\n\nTo leave something *in dubio* is again different from *suspensio judicii*; for in the former case I have an interest, whereas in the latter it is not always in keeping with the purpose to decide whether something is true or not.\n\nJudgments are divided into provisional (*praevia, antecedentia*) and determining (*determinantia, consequentia*). The former precede the investigation, the latter follow upon it. Provisional judgments are very necessary in the sciences, so that one can say that logic would be much enriched by their treatment, if only it were not too deep for it. For the objects must be given me here, about which I can judge provisionally or determinately. A man judges at once, however few data he has, and makes, as it were, a trial whether his judgment will be right or not. This has a strong influence on him, so that often the determining judgment, with all its grounds, cannot dislodge it. For example, one judges of almost the majority of books by their titles. If one hears that the author is a man of merit, I put more confidence in the book; and if the subject-matter is new, I have still more right to a favourable judgment. Such judgments are necessary in meditating, when I make my plan, although much must often be dropped afterwards. Since these judgments serve to direct the understanding to various means, they are very necessary for that purpose. Determining judgments are those that suffice for assent.\n\nThe provisional judgments may be called *judicia praevia*, and we must distinguish them from prejudices. By means of provisional judgments one can conceive maxims for the investigation of a thing. One could also call these judgments *anticipationes*, because one anticipates one's judgments before one yet has the determining ones. They are a holding-true on grounds that one is conscious are insufficient, and that are to incite us to seek the sufficient ones. They are also otherwise called philosophy of the common understanding, for it alone is capable of them, since it cannot engage in deeper investigations.",["'entstellt' read as 'entsteht' (OCR/transcript slip); 'judicn.' read as 'judicii'; 'Aveil' read as 'weil'.","Several long sentences were divided; 'Beifall fordern und gebieten' is rendered 'demand and command assent' and the 'd. h.' clause is kept as an explanation.","'ob sich alles in der Folge so werde mainteniren' is elliptical; '[waiting]' is supplied, and 'mainteniren' is rendered 'maintain itself'.","'er' in 'wenn er aber durch Schaden klug geworden' is resolved as the lively mind (lebhafter Kopf), not the understanding.","'In dubio etwas laßen' is obscure; I take it as leaving something undecided in doubt. 'hier'/'bei jenem' are resolved as the former and latter cases, but this is a judgment.","'wenn es nur nicht zu tief für sie wäre': 'es' and 'sie' are resolved as the matter and logic respectively, and the sense is doubtful.","'Urteils Kraft' is rendered 'power of judgment'; 'Erkenntniß' in the plural as 'modes of knowledge'; 'Fürwahrhalten' as 'holding-true'."]],"poelitz-s15-001":["On Prejudices.",[]],"poelitz-s15-002":["Remark. There is a difference between investigating [*untersuchen*] and reflecting [*überlegen*]. Many propositions can be accepted without investigation; for example, that the whole is equal to all its parts taken together. But I must nevertheless reflect on it, that is, I must be conscious of what it is that I think. Reflection is a comparing of the knowledge with the faculty of knowledge from which it is supposed to spring. For example, when someone says that between two points only one straight line is possible, an operation arises in my mind whereby I compare this with intuition (that is, here, with the faculty of knowledge) and hold the two together. I thus try it out, as it were, in thought, and this always happens even when we do not investigate the knowledge. If a mode of knowledge springs from a faculty of knowledge that is invalid in respect of the object, then the knowledge must either be rejected or the judgment must be suspended. One must also distinguish which faculty of knowledge has an influence here, and this distinction is necessary even where no investigation takes place. We also accept judgments without reflection; and then they are prejudices.",["'Erkenntnißkraft' and 'Erkenntnißvermögen' are both rendered 'faculty of knowledge'; 'Gemüt' as 'mind'; 'Anschauung' as 'intuition'.","'Zusammenhaltung' is rendered 'comparing'; 'und zusammenhalte' is rendered 'hold the two together'.","Some sentences were divided; the sense is otherwise unchanged."]],"poelitz-s15-003":["What are prejudices? Prejudices can be taken either in the subjective or in the objective sense [*Verstande*]. A prejudice *in sensu subjectivo* is (to put it briefly) the propensity to persuasion [*Überredung*]. Persuasion is holding-to-be-true on merely subjective grounds which are falsely taken to be objective. Prejudice arises either from imitation, from custom, or from inclination. A prejudice *in sensu objectivo* is illusion [*Schein*] in so far as it is made a principle [*Principium*] of truth.",["OCR 'subjeetiven/objeetiven' silently corrected to subjectiven/objectiven.","'Verstande' (for Sinne) is rendered 'sense', as the context requires; 'Schein' rendered 'illusion'.","The sentence 'Die Ueberredung ist … gehalten werden' is kept as one sentence, with the relative clause smoothed."]],"poelitz-s15-004":["Prejudices are provisional judgments [*vorläufige Urteile*], in so far as they are adopted as fundamental principles. We must distinguish between a prejudice and the false knowledge that arises from the prejudice. A mode of knowledge that is determinate in regard to particular objects is no prejudice. Prejudices are at times true provisional judgments, e.g. 'What is learnt young is done when old' [*Jung gewohnt alt gethan*]; but it is wrong when they are adopted as fundamental principles. This comes about because subjective grounds are falsely taken to be objective, and this in turn because we have not reflected. Every prejudice is a *principium* of erroneous judgments. Hence what arises from prejudices is not [further] prejudices but erroneous judgments, and prejudices are always the sources of the latter. For example, 'The apple does not fall far from the tree' may always be accepted as a prejudice, since it is not a universal proposition and yet passes for a *principium*. In the case of erroneous judgments we must always look to the prejudice from which they have sprung.—The three sources of prejudices are imitation, custom and inclination.—Imitation has a universal influence. The understanding seeks to extend itself, and does so through imitation, especially in the child and the common man. Imitation is a strong ground for holding to be true what others give out as true.—Custom [*Gewohnheit*] is facility in doing something, and this is good; but habituation [*Angewohnheit*] is not, for it is the necessity of doing something as one has done it before. Custom works more strongly in the old than in the young, because the latter have not yet lived long enough to make anything habitual to themselves. If a piece of knowledge has become customary, and has moreover arisen from imitation, such a man is wholly incurable. Prejudices of custom can be eradicated only by [lapse of] time, when the understanding is gradually checked and retarded by counter-grounds.—In regard to the prejudice of imitation we might distinguish the active and the passive use of our reason; for prejudice from imitation is nothing other than the propensity to the passive use of our reason. I act actively when I derive something from the natural rules of our reason. The first [i.e. the passive use] is a *contradictio in adjecto*, and yet it is widespread among very many men. Reason is an active *principium* which can borrow nothing from another, not even from experience, if we wish to employ pure reason. But it comes from men's laziness that they prefer to tread in the footsteps of others rather than exert their own powers. For example, the certainty of a future world is an objectively rational, but for the great majority a historical, knowledge.—Some means by which we may accustom ourselves to imitation are: 1.) Formulas, rules, the expression of which serves as a model of imitation",["'Ein Erkenntniß … was bestimmt ist' is elliptical; I read 'was' as 'etwas' (something determinate). Doubtful.","'Das erste ist contradictio in adjecto': the antecedent is not stated in the text; the passive use of reason is evidently meant. The passage seems to have lost a clause, and I have supplied the bracket.","'durch Länge' completed as '[lapse of] time' in brackets.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the next passage begins 'dient.' Not completed.","Long sentences divided. 'Aus Vorurteilen entspringen also nicht Vorurtheile' kept literally; '[further]' supplied."]],"poelitz-s15-005":["serves. Formulas are a relief when something is complicated, and",["Fragment: continues from the preceding passage and breaks off in mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s15-006":["the most enlightened head seeks to invent them. 2.) A saying [*Spruch*], the expression of which has a fitting measure [*Abgemessenheit*] of its pregnant",["Fragment, breaking off mid-sentence. 'Abgemeßenheit' rendered 'fitting measure'."]],"poelitz-s15-007":["meaning; *dicta* are utterances that serve as a rule or as a",["Fragment."]],"poelitz-s15-008":["law. Sayings, *dicta*, must always be borrowed from others,",["Fragment."]],"poelitz-s15-009":["and to these we must ascribe a certain infallibility, since we accept them because he says it. 3.) Sentences [*Sentenzen*], propositions which have their authority because they commend themselves of their own accord through the force of the thoughts expressed in them.",["The passage's final clause is carried through to the next line in the source; I translate it here as printed."]],"poelitz-s15-010":["(Sentences might be called in German *Sinnsprüche*.)",["The text here is a fragment: 'Nachdruk der Gedanken von selbst empfehlen' belongs to the preceding passage and is only partly given in the source line. I have translated the parenthesis, the only complete matter."]],"poelitz-s15-011":["They are distinguished from *bons mots*, which are propositions",["Fragment; 'bon mots' kept in French, italicised."]],"poelitz-s15-012":["that commend themselves more by wit than by",["Fragment."]],"poelitz-s15-013":["mature power of judgment [*Urteilskraft*]. Sentences maintain themselves for centuries,",[]],"poelitz-s15-014":["because a judgment which is an original product of reason, and",["Fragment, breaking off mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s15-015":["in which much meaning is comprehended in a concise expression, commends itself very",["Fragment."]],"poelitz-s15-016":["strongly.",["Completes the sentence begun two lines above."]],"poelitz-s15-017":["*Canones* (*Canones*, in German *Lehrsprüche*, and *Proverbia*,",["Fragment; the parenthesis is left open, as in the source."]],"poelitz-s15-018":["*Denksprüche*) are universal doctrinal maxims which serve as a foundation for the sciences;",["'Lehrsprüche' rendered 'doctrinal maxims'. Syntax of the parenthesis is slightly confused in the transcript: the German equivalents seem to be given in the order Canones = Lehrsprüche, Proverbia = Denksprüche."]],"poelitz-s15-019":["they can also be expressed in the form of sentences,",[]],"poelitz-s15-020":["and then they please the more.",[]],"poelitz-s15-021":["*Proverbia*, proverbs [*Sprüchwörter*], are the customary expressions for",["OCR 'Sprüclrwörter' silently corrected."]],"poelitz-s15-022":["a popular judgment of common understanding. They contain the",[]],"poelitz-s15-023":["current wit of the common understanding and serve to make its understanding known. They are provincial, whereas sentences are universal. Although they serve also as sentences and canons for the commonest rabble, for which reason they are not to be met with among people of finer education. Prejudices are established when one places one's wisdom in sentences and proverbs, regulates oneself by them, and never undertakes to think for oneself. The author [i.e. Meier] now speaks of the prejudice of authority [*Ansehn*]. Authority is of many kinds: that of times, of persons, of the multitude, of an age, and of a whole people. From this arise prejudices; or here the senses run ahead of the understanding, which is why they are also entitled *praejudicia*, the judgment being as it were decided beforehand in favour of the multitude, the person, etc. These are founded on the propensity (*Hang*) to assent to the judgments of others, and such a judgment from imitation might be called servile. There are also prejudices from self-love, when one is prepossessed by predilection against [i.e. in favour of] that which is the product of one's own understanding.",["The sentence 'Obgleich sie auch … nicht anzutrefen sind' is an anacoluthon in the transcript (no main clause); it is kept as a fragment rather than completed.","'der Autor' identified as Meier from the context of the lectures; bracket supplied.","'gegen das … von Praedilection eingenommen ist' is awkward ('prepossessed against'); I have rendered it literally and added a bracket giving the evident sense. Doubtful.","'Hieraus entstehen Vorurteile oder hier laufen die Sinne…': 'oder' is taken as 'or rather'; 'Sinne' (senses) is the transcript's reading and may be corrupt."]],"poelitz-s16-001":["On the authority of the person.",[]],"poelitz-s16-002":["In historical matters this must be the foundation of our judgments; for in things that rest upon experience and testimony we cannot comprehend everything with our understanding, but must build our knowledge upon the authority of other persons, which, however, is not a *praejudicium*. But when modes of knowledge are of such a nature that they can be settled by the understanding alone, the authority of others is no genuine ground of holding to be true; and if it is a ground which produces in our understanding a *propensio* toward the thing, then it is a *praejudicium auctoritatis*. Truths of reason hold anonymously; that is, we must not ask who said them, but what he said. The authority of the person has something in agreement with our inclination, since it serves indirectly to flatter our vanity. When a man is exalted as learned, he is highly praised because all praise him highly, and they are on a level in this because they are nothing beside the great man, and the difference which the admirers have among themselves is reckoned insignificant. Citations of learned men can serve as no proof in modes of knowledge of reason, although they have a certain weight in strengthening our belief; the universal agreement of the human understanding is an external criterion of truth. In metaphysical propositions, however, this will not do, though it will in jurisprudence, whence one may see how uncertain juristic judgment is. The cause here is presumably the manifold of cases, which can be neither determined in one law nor foreseen.",["'propension' written 'propensio' in the Latin form of the transcript's 'propension'; I follow the German-Latin form of the text, given in italics. 'Erkenntniße' in the plural rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","'Es ist ein äußeres Criterium…' follows 'Glauben zu verstärken' without a stop in the transcript; I divided the sentence. 'Bei metaphysischen Säzen geht dies aber nicht an' has no full stop before it in the source.","'jurisprudence' kept as in the source (French form).","'Vernunft Erkenntnißen' rendered 'modes of knowledge of reason'."]],"poelitz-s17-001":["On the authority of the multitude.",[]],"poelitz-s17-002":["To this the rabble is inclined; for it cannot judge the merits, abilities, etc. of a person, and therefore holds to the multitude. It presupposes that what all men say is true, but only in historical matters; in matters of religion, where it is itself interested, it leaves this to the learned.",["The last clause is doubtful: 'überlast' probably = 'überläßt' (leaves to); 'dies' is taken to refer to the judging. I read the sense as stated."]],"poelitz-s17-003":["Prejudices need not always be false. If I accept a thing from prejudice, it may nevertheless be true; but that I accept it *with* prejudice is logically harmful. — As regards the kind of knowledge, it is peculiar that the unlearned man has a prejudice in favour of learning, in that he credits the scholar with more knowledge even where the scholar can have no more. For example, concerning the state after death. The scholar, in turn, has a prejudice in favour of common understanding, in that he comes to distrust learning. This happens when all his efforts fail to give him proper satisfaction. It is found, then, in one who has already run fairly thoroughly through the circle of the sciences, and in speculations whose concepts cannot be made sensible and whose foundations are wavering (as happens in metaphysics and morals). He believes, then, that the key to these must be found somewhere, and he seeks it in common understanding. But this is deceptive; for where the cultivated faculty accomplishes nothing, the uncultivated will certainly accomplish nothing. The appeal to healthy human understanding is nothing other than an appeal to the multitude, for everyone lays claim to healthy human understanding. In metaphysics this is quite inadmissible, since no case can there be presented *in concreto*. In morals, however, it is peculiar that all cases can be given *in concreto*, and so common understanding here often judges more correctly than the speculative. For example, the juristic proposition *casum sentit dominus* is correct for the speculative understanding, but common understanding sees well enough that it is false, although it cannot state the reason. In general, the practical use of reason conforms more to the use of common than of speculative reason, and all rules of morality *in abstracto*, if they are not weighed *in concreto*, are faulty. Opposed to the prejudice of authority is logical egoism. This is the *principium* of indifference with regard to all the judgments of others as criteria of truth—",["'Vorurteile dürfen nicht immer falsch seyn' rendered 'need not always be false' (dürfen in the older sense of 'need').","'In Ansehung der Erkenntniß Art' is awkward in the German; I read it as 'as regards the kind of knowledge' and flag it as slightly doubtful. Kenntniß is rendered 'knowledge' here, since 'cognizance' would be too stilted.","The sentence beginning 'The scholar, in turn…' is restated as three. In the German 'sie findt sich' has no clear antecedent; I take it to refer to the distrust of learning, hence 'It is found, then, in one who…'.","'Gesunden Menschenverstand' = 'healthy human understanding'; 'gemeiner Verstand' = 'common understanding'. 'kern Fall' (OCR) read as 'kein Fall'.","The passage breaks off at 'Wahr-' (Wahrheit); it is left unfinished and continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s17-005":["…, but it is nevertheless a criterion, because my knowledge can be greatly falsified by subjective grounds which do not occur in the other person, though he has others of his own. There is only one science in which we can rely on our own judgment alone without committing this error, namely mathematics. But in philosophical and discursive knowledge we cannot always regard the assent of others as dispensable, since the oversights which are here so easy are impossible in intuitive modes of knowledge. Providence has willed that we should submit our judgments to the tribunal of common human reason, that is, in so far as many agree therein. Vanity, it is true, has some influence here also, but we are nevertheless instructed by it. The prejudice of antiquity is one of the most important. It arises because the ancients, so to speak, will not be forged into any new mould. Here there is also something more general, which can affect even young people. Its ground is the opinion that everything grows worse with time, which is, however, an idle complaint. Remark. There is one thing in nature which is imperishable, unchangeable, and an unfathomable mystery, namely that the species always remain the same although the individuals change. The consideration of the subjective causes of the use of understanding and reason is the explanation of how it comes about that one makes use of one's understanding in this way and not otherwise. Logic shows how we ought to use the understanding, not how we do use it. Logic does not consider whether the understanding is corrupted or not. The doctrine of prejudices therefore properly belongs not to logic but to anthropology. We include it here, however, because it can be very useful in applying the precepts of logic. It is a peculiar phenomenon [*Erscheinung*] that we esteem a thing too highly if we previously believed it unworthy of esteem; and conversely, if we expect much of a thing and afterwards do not find it so, we rate it far lower than it deserves. — The prejudice of antiquity springs from the delusion, mentioned above, that everything is growing worse. — Sometimes the prejudice arises from precisely the opposite cause. These prejudices often spring from an unfavourable judgment about the thing itself. Admiration [*Bewunderung*] is here mixed with wonder [*Verwunderung*]. The latter arises when we find something where we did not look for it; admiration is the general feeling when we find something very great. Yet it often happens that wonder degenerates into admiration, although the two are of different kinds, and this holds here also. Of the ancients we mostly have an unfavourable judgment, in view of the circumstances of the times in which they lived. If we find something in them which we had not looked for, we wonder at it; or we hold them to be wiser than they really are, whence admiration arises, and then it becomes prejudice. — Another cause of the prejudice of antiquity is this: the knowledge which we have of the ancients is, with us, learning, and learning always commands respect. From this springs an inclination and partiality towards the ancients themselves, in order that the study which one has devoted to them may not have been in vain.",["The passage opens mid-sentence ('zu, ist aber doch…'), carrying on from the preceding passage; the opening ellipsis is mine and marks the break, and nothing is supplied.","OCR garble 'discur-* 3 *. *• ** ^ 5> \\> <x >$ siven' silently read as 'discursiven'; 'kerne' read as 'keine'; 'Versehn' taken as 'Versehen' (oversights).","'weil sich die Alten sozusagen in keine neue Form schmieden wollen' is literal and somewhat odd (possibly 'man' was meant, or the transcript is loose); I keep the literal sense and flag it as doubtful.","'Erscheinung' here is used in the ordinary sense of 'phenomenon', not Kant's technical 'appearance'; Bewunderung/Verwunderung rendered 'admiration'/'wonder', the German given because the distinction is the point.","'Anmerkung' is rendered 'Remark'; the sentence 'Eins ist in der Natur…' is reworded slightly as 'There is one thing in nature which…'.","'Diese Vorurtheile entpringen öfters aus einem nachteiligen Urteil über daßelbe' is obscure in the transcript ('daßelbe' has no clear antecedent); I take it as 'the thing itself'. Doubtful.","'Kenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; 'Gelehrsamkeit' as 'learning'; the long final sentences are divided for clarity."]],"poelitz-s17-006":["A proposition that comes down from the ancients may in itself be quite commonplace and poor, yet it displays learning and wide reading. This extends even to distance of place: if, for example, someone cites what Confucius said, this lends the proposition itself a certain favour. We have a ground for judging antiquity favourably without any prejudice lying behind it; but it is only a ground for a moderate esteem, which we all too often exceed. For since time, as it were, sifts everything, and only what has inner worth is preserved, we have only the best writings of the ancients, and we are mistaken if we believe that they all wrote in that fashion. They have no absolute worth, but only a relative one, even the poor writings that have been lost. With the philosophers especially, this relative worth is made into an absolutely high one. Thus there is the prejudice that our posterity will think us giants in understanding, and we stand in just the same relation to antiquity. This leads us either to entrust ourselves to the passive guidance of antiquity or to neglect the use of our own talents. — At times antiquity, and the prejudice in its favour, has also fallen, namely at the beginning of this century, when the famous Fontenelle took the side of the moderns. In instruction the ancients are and remain our teachers, and are to be retained for that purpose, but not as *tresoriers* [treasurers] of the sciences and of knowledge. What they could teach has long since been picked out, and to go back to them still is to lead the understanding away from the right path. — A third cause of the prejudice for the ancients is gratitude that they have broken the path for us to much knowledge [*Kenntnisse*]; it seems only fair to show them honour on that account, and in doing so we then often exceed the measure. To this is added the consciousness that we too shall one day grow old. The last cause of the prejudice for the ancients is envy of our contemporaries. He who cannot hold his own against the moderns disparages them in comparison with the ancients, so that they may not raise themselves above him. All this shows upon what an illusion the high praise of the ancients rests.",["Sentence \"Dies geht so gar auf die Entfernung des Orts\" rendered \"This extends even to distance of place\"; the sense is that remoteness (as with Confucius) lends authority as antiquity does.","\"so ist das Vorurteil unsere Nachkommen werden denken…\" is irregular in the German; I have recast it as \"Thus there is the prejudice that…\" and the transcript's sense may be somewhat compressed here.","\"Zuweilen fiel auch das Altertum und das Vorurteil\": \"fiel\" taken as \"fell\" (lost credit); the following \"nehmlich\" clause dates this to the early eighteenth century.","*tresoriers* is French, kept in italics with English supplied in brackets.","\"Erkenntniße\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Kenntnißen\" rendered \"knowledge\" with the German given, to distinguish it from *Erkenntnis*.","OCR \"aUe\" corrected to \"alle\"; \"Ableitung\" in \"Ableitung des Verstandes von der rechten Bahn\" is read as \"leading away\" (Ableitung)."]],"poelitz-s17-007":["Remark. Of all branches of learning none puffs up more than the philological, because there is here a certain universality of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*], although it is only historical. Philosophy beats down pride, and rather tends to produce misology. Philologists, however, do not advance the understanding any further.",["\"Erkennt -nißes\" is a line-break split in the OCR, silently joined.","\"Wißen\" rendered \"learning\" in the sense of a branch of learning; \"Erkenntniß\" kept as \"knowledge\".","\"Misologie\" (hatred of reasoning) kept as \"misology\"."]],"poelitz-s17-008":["The prejudice of novelty is the opposite of the preceding. If a man's knowledge is capable of extension, it is natural that we can place more confidence in the moderns than in the ancients, since we are in advantageous circumstances. That is a correct provisional judgment, but not yet a sufficient one. To the prejudice there is added the inclination towards what is new, because we are in tune with the way of thinking of our own age, and not because the understanding sees it to be right. This prejudice, however, is also made very suspect, because one must always suppose that the author has been driven by a lust for innovation (as often really happens) to set a [*Sehern*] against a truth long since proved, one which may well maintain itself for a time. — The prejudice in favour of an accepted system of doctrine is still to be noted. A system of doctrine [*Lehrgebäude*] is the derivation of much knowledge from one *principio* [principle]; for this we acquire a *praedilection*, since we overlook many faults. The *praedilection* is good if the system does not rest on false *principiis*. Remark. Propositions are not always false because they come from a prejudice; often they are correct, only the *modus acquirendi* (as the jurists say) is illegal. By this I have opened a broad road for false knowledge, and the manner [of acquiring] is often more important than the knowledge itself.",["\"Sehern\" is evidently an OCR or transcript corruption (perhaps \"Schein\" or \"Sophisma\", or \"Seher\"); I leave it untranslated in brackets and do not guess.","OCR \"prineipio/prineipien\" corrected to *principio/principiis*; Latin kept in italics; the transcript's German \"prineipien\" rendered as \"principiis\" for consistency with the Latin form.","\"Erkenntniß\" in \"der Art ... als das Erkenntniß selbst\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"die Art\" is completed by the bracketed \"[of acquiring]\", supplied from *modus acquirendi*.","\"ich habe dadurch dem falschen Erkenntniß einen breiten Weg gebahnt\" is slightly elliptical; the sense is that one who acquires a proposition illegitimately opens the way to false knowledge.","\"Hang gegen Neue\" taken as inclination toward novelties."]],"poelitz-s18-001":["Of probable knowledge.",["Heading; \"wahrscheinliche Erkenntniß\" rendered \"probable knowledge\"."]],"poelitz-s18-002":["Here, as in all the logics, there is a fault in expression. Instead of saying 'the knowledge of the probable', they say 'probable knowledge'. My knowledge of a probable object may be certain and correct; it is only that through it I hold the object to be probable. The knowledge of the probable is good, but the logic of it cannot be a pure logic, since objects are already involved. That on whose side there are more grounds of its happening than on the other, I hold to be probable.\n\nAll probability can be expressed as a fraction: the denominator is the number of all possible cases, the numerator the number of actual cases. Probability is therefore the holding-to-be-true on insufficient grounds, which, however, stand to the sufficient grounds in a greater ratio than the grounds of the opposite. Probable modes of knowledge may be practically sufficient, but they are not logically sufficient.\n\nPlausibility [*Scheinbarkeit*] is the validity of a mode of knowledge on insufficient grounds, in so far as these are greater than the grounds of the opposite. Here, then, I compare them not with the sufficient grounds but only with the grounds of the opposite; I do not know, therefore, how much is required for certainty, as I do in the case of probable knowledge. Plausibility yields a provisional judgment; probability yields a determining, though not an apodeictic, judgment. Probability can also be expressed mathematically, namely thus: it is more than half of certainty. Half of certainty is doubtful.\n\nThe degree of holding-to-be-true is objectively valid in the case of probability, subjectively valid in the case of plausibility. In every probability there must always be a standard, namely certainty; but this differs in different men, one demanding more for it than another. If the grounds of holding-to-be-true are homogeneous, the degree of it depends on their number; that is, they have only to be counted. If they are heterogeneous, they must be weighed. With the former we are soon done, since the grounds are all in agreement; with the latter it is harder. Probability therefore cannot be estimated in philosophical modes of knowledge, though it can be in mathematics. Much has been said of a *logica probabilium*, but, for the reason just given, it is not possible.\n\nIn the case of probability one abstracts rules of the understanding from the ordinary procedure and adopts them as logical rules; but they are nothing but rules abstracted from the ordinary practice of the understanding and brought into general formulae. The knowledge of the probable must be certain; that is, I must be certain that it is probable. A merely probable mode of knowledge must never be followed, though it can spur us to inquire into the grounds.\n\nA ground against holding-to-be-true is an obstacle. A subjective obstacle is doubt; an objective obstacle is an objection. Doubt is as yet no ground for holding anything false, but only a ground for suspension of judgment. In ordinary speech *dubium* (objective ground) and *dubitatio* (subjective ground) are often confused. A subjective ground of doubt is valid only for me, whereas the objective ground must be valid for everyone else.\n\nA scruple is an obstacle to holding-to-be-true where I do not know whether it is objective or subjective. If it is merely subjective, it is not valid, since it is mere semblance [*Schein*]; and if we know that it is merely subjective, it cannot deceive us. Its cause is inclination, habit, and so on. Suppose, for example, we come upon a new judgment and have at the same time the strongest grounds for it; we may yet be unable to reconcile ourselves to it, and this is a scruple. But if I see that something subjective in me is at work here, it ceases to be a scruple; I have then detected the semblance. In many cases, however, we cannot get so far, because we cannot always compare our knowledge with the object, but only the modes of knowledge with one another. In every scruple there is an obstacle in the subject; the only point is that I want to know whether it lies merely in the subject.\n\nA scruple cannot be removed until it has been made into an objection, and it is a great talent to anticipate scruples that might arise. By objections I do not wish to refute, but only to weaken certainty; and through them certainty is brought to distinctness and completeness. Everyone must therefore listen to objections, and no one can be certain of a thing unless counter-grounds have been called forth, by which it can be determined how far one still is from certainty, or how near to it.\n\nA knot (an objection) can be cut through, if one shows that there is no need to occupy oneself with untying it; for example, how the evil in the world can be reconciled with the wisdom of God.\n\nOne must distinguish between resolving an objection [*einen Einwurf beantworten*] and replying to it [*auf ihn antworten*]. Even against objections to the most groundless opinions the man who holds them will be able to say something in reply, otherwise he becomes ridiculous; whether he will be able to resolve them is another question. To resolve an objection [*beantworten*] means to show how the scruple arose. If this is not done, the objection is repelled but not removed.\n\nIn Latin the words are *verisimilitudo* and *probabilitas*. The former would be better rendered by 'plausible' [*scheinbar*], as opposed to the true, and it concerns only the knowledge. The object is either *probabilis* or *improbabilis*.",["Restated: the opening sentences and several long runs were divided into separate sentences and paragraphs (the German is a single block); the paragraphing is mine.","*Fürwahrhalten* rendered 'holding-to-be-true'; *Scheinbarkeit* rendered 'plausibility', with the German given, since Kant ties it to *verisimilitudo* and *Schein* (semblance) is used separately below. *Wahrscheinlichkeit* = probability.","'Mein Erkenntniß' (as OCR'd) is translated as 'My knowledge'; the original may have read 'Mein' or 'Ein', which is doubtful but the sense is the same.","OCR slips silently corrected: dasObject, Objeete, WTenn, fem (= fern, 'in so far as'), probabüium (= probabilium), Erkennt nißen; 'Zehler' is Zähler (numerator). The stray mark ■ before 'Auch auf die Einwürfe' is omitted.","'oder sie müßen denn numerirt werden' is smoothed to 'that is, they have only to be counted'; 'sich nicht drin schicken' is rendered 'reconcile ourselves to it'; 'zusammen räumen' is taken as zusammenreimen, 'reconcile'.","*beantworten* / *antworten auf*: the distinction is kept by 'resolve' and 'reply', with the German in brackets; this is a choice made from Kant's own gloss that to *beantworten* is to show how the scruple arose.","'Scheinbar... und es betrift nur das Erkenntniß' is ambiguous about the antecedent of 'es'; I take it as *verisimilitudo* (the better rendering of which would be *scheinbar*)."]],"poelitz-s18-003":["We come now to a principle of doubt in relation to the principle of asserting. He who, in the case of his knowledge, has to choose assertion as his aim is a dogmatist. But he who has the maxim of treating knowledge with the aim of making it uncertain, and of showing the impossibility of attaining certainty, is a sceptic. [Greek word, garbled in the source] means as much as *indagare* [to investigate]. The first sceptics therefore investigated things, and in the meantime suspended their judgment. Scepticism was the maxim of suspending judgment for the sake of investigation.",["The Greek word is unreadable in the OCR ('CTKayacTÖoü'); it is probably a form of σκέπτεσθαι ('to examine'), which would fit the gloss *indagare*, but I have not restored it.","'die Behauptung zur Absicht zu wälen hat' (wälen = wählen) is rendered 'has to choose assertion as his aim'; the construction is slightly irregular and was smoothed.","*Maxime* is rendered 'maxim'; *Skeptiker* / *Skepticismus* as 'sceptic' / 'scepticism' in British spelling."]],"poelitz-s18-004":["What is a maxim? When objective rules become subjective, that is, when we act in accordance with them, this is a maxim. To doubt as a matter of maxim is something other than to doubt the insight into the ground of a thing. I doubt my attentiveness, [that is, I doubt] whether I shall have fulfilled everything that the understanding demands of me before I am to pass a determining judgment. There are maxims of the dogmatic and of the sceptical mode of thought. Between them stands the critical mode of thought; that is, one treats something for a time as problematic, until one arrives at complete certainty about it. The first method is very harmful to the sciences, because it gives free rein to all errors, provided only that they have some semblance of truth. Scepticism, too, is not good, for there may yet be something true in the knowledge. The critical procedure is the best. It springs from distrust of our judgment, especially when we are apprehensive that subjective grounds may be falsely taken for objective. Since this often happens, this method always has reason on its side. The sceptical method is very useful to the critical. When, namely, one has accepted a truth, one puts oneself in the place of the opponent, and seeks out all possible grounds for overthrowing one's proposition, and so for finding the truth. It often happens that one is brought over to the opposite opinion.\n\nAbsolute and brutal scepticism says: there is no sufficient *criterion* of truth. To be able to say this, it must have a standard. The standard must be the sufficient *criterion* of truth; so it has truth after all, and thus contradicts itself. In mathematics and physics scepticism is not necessary: in the latter, experience at once sets us right; in the former it is unnecessary in any case, because of evidence. But the knowledge which is neither mathematical nor empirical, and so is philosophical, has given occasion to scepticism. Pure philosophy, in so far as it is speculative, is entitled metaphysics. From this point men went further, and set distrust even in experience and in mathematics, for which they made use of the most ridiculous grounds. The sceptics had the proposition: Everything is so uncertain that it is uncertain whether everything is uncertain. This destroys scepticism itself. They entitled it *kathartikon* (a purgative). The first sceptics must have been men of insight; for to discover the conflict of reason, and to spy out the grounds that can be adduced against a proposition, is difficult.",["The OCR 'KO0apKTiKÖv' is garbled; I read it as Greek *kathartikon*, which the parenthesis 'Reinigungsmittel' supports. Transliterated and glossed 'purgative'.","Silently corrected OCR/spelling: 'sein-' to 'sehr', 'drin' (darin), 'dochwas' to 'doch was'.","Divided the long opening and the sceptical-method sentences. Supplied '[that is, I doubt]' to make the elliptical 'ob ich alles werde erfüllt haben' read plainly.","'Maxime zu zweifeln' rendered 'to doubt as a matter of maxim'; 'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; 'Denkart' as 'mode of thought'."]],"poelitz-s19-001":["On Hypotheses.",[]],"poelitz-s19-002":["The author defines hypothesis as an opinion which is philosophical, that is, which is used to explain appearances in the world. [Mere] opinion does not explain, as hypothesis does. Strictly, hypothesis is a holding-true (*Fürwahrhalten*) of the judgment of the truth of a ground, on account of the sufficiency of the consequences. Or: one accepts something as true because a sufficient ground can be drawn from it out of given consequences. Or again: it is the holding-true of a presupposition as ground. If something is mediately certain, it is true as a consequence; if something is held true as a ground, this is hypothesis. I assume a thing as actual without proof; if it is a sufficient ground from which to derive consequences, it is accepted as true on account of the consequences, and not because it is itself the consequence of a true mode of knowledge. If the consequence is true, the ground is not always true, for there may be several grounds for the same consequence. All holding-true of a hypothesis rests on this, that it suffices as ground to explain other modes of knowledge as its consequences. Here, then, we infer from the truth of the consequence to the truth of the ground. It was noted above that this is indeed a criterion of truth, but not a sufficient one. This inference is entirely correct only if all possible consequences are true; for if there were something false in the ground, a false consequence would also have to be derivable from it. But since we can never determine all possible consequences, this gives no secure inference either. We see, then, that hypotheses always remain hypotheses. For I may have overlooked some small circumstance which experience has shown, and which perhaps could not be explained from the hypothesis; and we can never really attain complete certainty regarding a hypothesis, although there is a ground of probability. If, however, all the consequences that have come before me can be explained from the hypothesis, the probability of the hypothesis grows, and there is no reason why I should not assume that all possible consequences will be capable of explanation. I therefore conclude that all possible consequences are true. They are thus accepted as true, not apodictically, but *per inductionem*. Something in a hypothesis must nevertheless be apodictic, namely: (a) the possibility of the presupposition. For example, if, to explain",["Restated the long middle section as several sentences; the German is a loose run of alternative definitions, which I have kept as 'Or… Or again…'.","'Meinung erklärt nicht wie Hypothese': I supplied '[Mere]' to mark the contrast; flagged as a judgment.","'daraus aus gegebenen Folgen' is garbled in the German (doubled 'aus'); I render the sense, 'out of given consequences'. Corrected OCR 'grün dt', 'liier'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence, as in the source."]],"poelitz-s19-003":["earthquakes and volcanoes we assume a subterranean fire,",["Fragment; continues the previous sentence. Line-break fragment of the OCR text."]],"poelitz-s19-004":["it must still be possible that there should be such a fire,",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s19-005":["[exist], or at least it must be a heated body.",["The line begins 'könne' ('can'); the verb of the preceding clause is evidently displaced. I supplied '[exist]' in brackets to make sense; the construction is doubtful (possible lost text)."]],"poelitz-s19-006":["But another [assumes] that the earth is an animal, in which the inner juices",["'[assumes]' is supplied; the German has no verb. Fragment, continued below."]],"poelitz-s19-007":["produce warmth; this is not possible. [One] can invent actualities,",["'[One]' supplied: the German 'Würklichkeiten kann' lacks a subject."]],"poelitz-s19-008":["but not possibilities; these must be certain. The",["Fragment; continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s19-009":["touchstone of possibility is experience.",[]],"poelitz-s19-010":["Consequence. From the assumed ground the consequence must flow correctly, otherwise it is a chimera. Next, unity is required; that is, there must be only one hypothesis. If I have to assume many, it loses much of its probability. For I infer: the more consequences flow from a hypothesis, the more probable it is; the fewer, the more improbable. For example, Tycho de Brahe's hypothesis, that the sun, planets and fixed stars move around the earth, was not sufficient for many appearances, and he therefore always had to assume more [hypotheses]. In that case it can already be guessed that this is not the true ground. The Copernican system, on the other hand, is a hypothesis from which everything that has so far occurred has been explained. Here there is no need of *hypotheses subsidiariae*; these are such as",["'Consequenz' rendered 'Consequence' as the heading of the second requirement (the first being the possibility of the presupposition). Supplied '[hypotheses]' after 'more'. Restated 'Denn ists schon zu errathen' as 'In that case it can already be guessed'.","Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s19-011":["are assumed in order to come to the help of one already assumed.",[]],"poelitz-s19-012":["In metaphysics hypotheses are something absurd.",["'ungereimt' rendered 'absurd'."]],"poelitz-s19-013":["But nowhere are there more hypotheses than in natural science;",["OCR 'H3q:>othesen' read as 'Hypothesen'. 'Naturlehre' rendered 'natural science'."]],"poelitz-s19-014":["they are also accepted among the commentators of the classical authors.",[]],"poelitz-s19-015":["The author now speaks of persuasion (*Überredung*). We have already mentioned it above.",[]],"poelitz-s19-016":["It is the holding-true on insufficient grounds, of which one does not know whether they are objective or subjective. We believe something to be certain, and yet hold it to be only persuasion, because we cannot give an account of the grounds. Many modes of knowledge begin in this way, that we are conscious of them only as persuasion. Persuasion is followed by reflection (*Überlegung*); that is, we see to which faculty of knowledge the knowledge belongs. Reflection is followed by investigation (*Untersuchung*); that is, we see whether the grounds are sufficient or insufficient in respect of the object. With many it remains at persuasion, with some it comes to reflection, with few to investigation. Persuasions often precede conviction (*Überzeugung*), but among those who know what belongs to certainty they are rare; these will",["Divided into shorter sentences; supplied nothing beyond the parenthetical German terms. 'Erkenntnißkraft' rendered 'faculty of knowledge'.","Breaks off mid-sentence; the clause continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s19-017":["not easily confuse conviction and reflection. He is least persuaded who has often been deceived. That people",["'Ueberzeugung und Ueberlegung' is as printed; one would expect 'Überredung und Überzeugung', so the text may be corrupt or a slip of the transcriber. Translated as it stands. The next passage resumes the sentence on being persuaded."]],"poelitz-s19-018":["least. [Of one] who is so easily persuaded, that one lets oneself and can be so easily persuaded",["The OCR line is garbled: 'wenigsten überredt, der oft betrogen ist' appears to be duplicated into the preceding passage. What can be made out is translated; the reading is too uncertain to be sure of. See the preceding and following passages."]],"poelitz-s19-019":["indicates a shallow mode of thought. One must therefore not blame everyone who does not give his assent to a matter as quickly as another.",["Reconstruction of the join between the last three fragments is doubtful because of the line-break damage; the closing sentence is clear."]],"poelitz-s19-020":["Sciences have already been spoken of above. A sum-total of modes of knowledge [*Inbegrif von Erkenntnißen*] as an aggregate is common knowledge; a sum-total of modes of knowledge as a system is science. Every science must be capable of being divided *a priori* in accordance with its principle; I must know how many parts belong to the science before it is even treated. The system rests upon an idea of the whole. In the case of modes of knowledge as an aggregate, the parts come first; in the system, however, the idea of the whole comes first, and this is what causes the greatest difficulty. One may rightly say that metaphysics is a rhapsody.",["Erkenntnißen/Erkenntniß rendered 'modes of knowledge' in the plural and 'knowledge' in the singular, per the method.","'Inbegrif' rendered 'sum-total'; 'gemeine Erkenntniß' is rendered 'common knowledge' in the sense of ordinary, unsystematic knowledge.","'Bei Erkenntnißen als Aggregat sind die Teile vorher, beim System aber die Idee des Ganzen, die ... macht' is elliptical; I supplied 'come first' and 'the idea of the whole comes first' and joined the relative clause to it with 'this'."]],"poelitz-s19-021":["The author speaks further of art. Knowing [*Wißen*] and being able [*Können*] are different perfections. I can sometimes do a thing at once, as soon as I know it; but there are things I cannot do, although I know them. Practice is required for this, and that is art. The author is not right in understanding by science only knowledge of reason [*Vernunft Erkenntniß*]; historical modes of knowledge, too, can be scientific.",["'Der Autor' is Meier, whose Auszug is the textbook of the lectures (an inference from the Pölitz context, not stated in the passage).","'Uebung' rendered 'practice'; 'Rechtthun' idiom 'thut nicht Recht' rendered 'is not right'."]],"poelitz-s20-001":["On Certainty.",[]],"poelitz-s20-002":["It is either empirical or apodeictic. Experience is either our own or that of others, which we have through testimony. Empirical certainty is therefore certainty through our own experience or through the testimony of others. This is historical certainty, and we can often put more trust in the testimony of others than in our own experience. The author speaks of mathematical certainty of the first and second rank, but he is not right: all mathematical certainty is of one and the same kind. Certainty of reason is at all times apodeictic, that is, absolute necessity is to be found in it. Intuitive certainty, or certainty through the construction of concepts, is the mathematical; discursive certainty is the philosophical. The author now speaks of arbitrary truths [*willkürliche Wahrheiten*], which is a peculiar expression; we prefer to say that arbitrary propositions, or *positiones*, are arbitrary rules of use. But this is nothing more than an imperative; it is a command of my reason, not a mode of knowledge of the thing itself. The author defines them as truths which are true through our arbitrary choice [*Willkühr*]. To these he reckons hypotheses, which are adopted arbitrarily, but not as truths; rather, this is first determined through involuntary consequences.",["OCR corrected silently: 'Noth wendigkeit', 'Heber' (lieber), 'willkührliehe', 'wilUüihrliche', 'willkührhch' (willkührlich).","'Willkühr' rendered 'arbitrary choice', 'willkührlich' 'arbitrary'; 'unwillkührliche Folgen' rendered 'involuntary consequences'.","'Erkenntniß Sache selbst' appears to lack a preposition (probably 'der Sache selbst'); I read it as 'knowledge of the thing itself' and supplied 'of' in the translation without brackets, as the sense requires.","'apodiktisch' rendered 'apodeictic', 'Gewisheit' 'certainty'."]],"poelitz-s21-001":["On Proofs, and in particular on the Apodeictic.",[]],"poelitz-s21-002":["They are divided into acroamatic proofs, which are discursive and can be expressed through words, and mathematical proofs, which are intuitive, [proceeding] from the construction of concepts. In a proof there are therefore:",["'[proceeding]' is supplied to complete the elliptical 'die intuitiv sind aus der Construction der Begriffe'.","'acroamatisch' kept in the Greek-derived form 'acroamatic'."]],"poelitz-s21-003":["1.) the thing that is to be proved;",[]],"poelitz-s21-004":["2.) the ground of proof [*Beweisgrund*]; and",[]],"poelitz-s21-005":["3.) the consequence, [that is,] how the proposition follows from the ground of proof.",[]],"poelitz-s21-006":["One can know the ground of proof without knowing the form of the proof, or the consequence. When, in disputation, one puts forward merely the ground of proof, or *materia probationis*, this is entitled disputing *per terminos medios*. According to the author's definition, modes of knowledge are 'unproveable' [*unerweißlich*] which are completely certain even without proof. In German, however, this expression signifies a thing which indeed requires a proof but has none. An immediately certain proposition is therefore indemonstrable. All our modes of knowledge must begin from immediately [*immediate*] certain propositions. However much may be mediately certain, that is, certain through a proof, something must nevertheless be indemonstrable, or immediately certain. A proof is either direct, which is at the same time positive, and proves that the proposition is true; or apagogic, which is negative, and refutes the opposite, or shows that the opposite of the proposition is false. Apagogic proofs are convincing in mathematics; in philosophy one is glad to be spared them, for positive proofs show me not only the thing but also the sources of its truth. The apagogic proof shows only the opposite, when I infer the truth of my proposition from the falsity of the latter. If the apagogic proof is to hold, the propositions must be opposed *contradictorie, diametraliter*, not *contrarie*; otherwise both may be false. A proof which is the ground of mathematical certainty is entitled demonstration, and a proof which is the ground of philosophical certainty is entitled acroamatic proof. The author now speaks of the sources of knowledge [*Erkenntniß Quellen*], and reckons among them knowledge of reason and belief. Belief, however, belongs to experience; for I believe on account of the testimony of others, and this I must indeed experience.",["'heist' rendered 'is entitled', per the method.","'Glaube' rendered 'belief'; the following heading speaks of 'historischer Glaube' (historical belief).","'Die ... Erkenntniße sind ... unerweißlich, die auch ohne Beweis völlig gewis sind' restated with the relative clause after the predicate for English order.","'diametraliter' is given as in the source; the sentence 'sich ... opponirt seyn' is slightly irregular and smoothed to 'must be opposed'.","'des Sazes' in 'Gegenteil des Sazes' resolved as the proposition; 'deßelben' ('the latter') refers to the opposite."]],"poelitz-s21-007":["On Historical Belief.",[]],"poelitz-s21-008":["The author relates belief [*Glaube*] solely to testimony, but this is false. We distinguish between the two expressions, to believe something and to believe someone. In the first, believing is distinguished only from knowing; the second presupposes that another person has said it. To believe something relates to the mode of knowledge and to the ground of holding-to-be-true, which may well derive from reason. If the grounds of holding-to-be-true are insufficient for the logical use, that is, for the pure understanding, but are sufficient for the practical use, that is, for application, then it is belief. There is no theoretical belief of reason, for in speculation reason gives us either knowledge or nothing, and even opining, but not conviction. But there is a practical belief of reason. Logical grounds must not be sufficient, but reason can, with regard to our interest, have grounds for assuming something to be true. Propositions of reason are not in themselves an object of belief, but in mathematics it may well be admitted, because errors in application do not so easily remain hidden. When we believe something because we believe another person, that is historical belief, or when one holds something to be true solely on account of testimony. There is a thoroughly grounded belief of reason, which can never rise to knowledge; and in the practical respect it can be as strong a holding-to-be-true as the greatest certainty, only the logical requisites [*requisita*] are lacking to it. Historical belief can be actual knowledge and need not be distinguished from it; belief of reason, however, must be distinguished. If we contrast [*contradistinguiren*] belief with knowledge, we must take it as a holding-to-be-true that is logically insufficient but practically sufficient. For historical belief a witness is required, whom one can believe historically if he is of infallible reason or relates his own experience. In philosophical knowledge we can hold nothing to be true on the ground of testimony except the testimony of God. All citations involve a historical belief, and the calling of witnesses is quite necessary in regard to what is empirical, since we cannot experience everything ourselves. For the credibility of a witness there is required competence [*Tüchtigkeit*], that he could have told the truth, or authenticity [*authenticitaet*], and sincerity, that he was willing to tell it, or integrity [*integritaet*]. He must be competent in that he was able to make the experience, and he must also be able to declare it so that he is understood. It is not so easy to make experiences as people suppose. As regards the sincerity of the witness, one cannot rely on it in the common man; he is too crude to set a value on truth. The ancient times were so disposed, and almost all ancient writers were not very punctilious about the truth, for example Herodotus and Livy. In this respect the more recent times have much the advantage over the ancients. We are more honest in our way of thinking, and compelled to avow the truth, because otherwise we lose our good name. This change took place at the end of the last century; and exactness of testimony is found especially in experimental physics, which extended also to history, to which printing and the newspapers contributed much, these being means of soon refuting lies. In regard to sincerity one must inquire whether the witness had an interest in departing from the truth; but this is so manifold that one can hardly judge it, for what we do not regard as an interest at all is one for another person. Witnesses are divided into ear-witnesses and eye-witnesses: the latter attests his own experience of a thing, the former his experience of the testimony of others. In the Greek legend and tradition there is no proper witness. In the former one believes not on account of the validity of the witness but on account of their multitude; in the latter there is a multitude of subordinate witnesses. If unbelief is a ground of reproach, belief must be moral. In Latin one is not able to translate it. Belief is either theoretical or practical, that is, moral. One calls unbelieving him who has no moral belief, that is, who has no moral interest; and this can be demanded of everyone, and such unbelief is a ground of reproach, namely when a man does not believe in virtue; and this unbelief is harmful, because by it he gives up all resolve toward virtue, since he believes there is none.",["Glaube is rendered 'belief' throughout; 'Vernunftglaube' as 'belief of reason'; Fürwahrhalten as 'holding-to-be-true'. Erkenntniß in 'bezieht sich aufs Erkenntniß' is rendered 'mode of knowledge', though the singular could also be 'knowledge'.","Several long sentences were left intact in order but punctuated more plainly; 'Erkenntniß und den Grund' is treated as a single reference, as the sense requires.","'Wenn Unglaube ein Tadel ist so muß der Glaube moralisch seyn' is rendered as given; 'ground of reproach' for Tadel is my choice. 'Im lateinischen ist man nicht fähig es zu übersezen' is obscure (the antecedent of 'es', presumably Glaube/Unglaube, is not stated) and is translated literally.","'Ohren- und Augenzeugen': the transcript's definitions seem to be transposed or loosely worded ('dieser'/'jener' referring to Augenzeuge/Ohrenzeuge). I have translated as the text stands, taking 'dieser' as the nearer (eye-witness) and 'jener' as the ear-witness.","'Bei jener ... bei dieser' in the Greek legend/tradition sentence is likewise translated as it stands (Sage / Ueberlieferung); the sense is somewhat uncertain.","OCR corrections made silently: 'f indt' → 'findet', 'noth wendig', 'ver laßen', 'Ueber lieferung', 'DamitErfahrungsgrund' etc.; 'last' read as 'läßt'; 'betritt' as 'betrifft'; 'Intereße' and 'avthenticitaet' as in the source. 'Die alten Zeiten waren also gestimmt' is translated as 'were so disposed'.","Latin terms kept in italics with English beside them: *requisita*, *contradistinguiren* (German form of a Latin verb); *authenticitaet*, *integritaet* are given with bracketed forms."]],"poelitz-s22-001":["On the Reconciliation of Reason and Experience.",["'Vereinbarung' rendered 'reconciliation'; 'union' or 'agreement' would also be possible."]],"poelitz-s22-002":["When something is inferred from experience by means of reason, one cannot say that they are reconciled; rather, experience is made use of by reason. In order that experience may become a ground of knowledge, reason is required. Experience is then the sufficient ground, and reason makes use of it for modes of knowledge. Reason is the faculty by which we can use experience as a ground for modes of knowledge. Reason and experience are then not reconciled, except when one faculty of knowledge gives the other the *complementum ad sufficientiam* [supplement to sufficiency]. If a mode of knowledge from experience and reason is insufficient, and the one supplements the other, then reason and experience are reconciled. So it is with hypotheses.",["'DamitErfahrungsgrund Erkenntniß werde' is an OCR run-together; read as 'Damit Erfahrungsgrund [zum] Erkenntniß werde' and rendered 'in order that experience may become a ground of knowledge'. The reading is conjectural.","'denn werden aber' is taken as 'dann werden aber', i.e. 'then ... are not reconciled except when'; the sense is somewhat uncertain.","*complementum ad sufficientiam* kept in Latin with English in square brackets.","'Erkenntnißvermögen' rendered 'faculty of knowledge'; 'Erkenntnißen' as 'modes of knowledge'."]],"poelitz-s23-001":["On Practical Knowledge.",[]],"poelitz-s23-002":["A mode of knowledge is entitled practical in contrast with the theoretical, but also in contrast with the speculative. If a proposition is an imperative, it is practical. The 'ought' here signifies a free action which is good for a certain purpose, and a proposition that expresses such an action is an imperative. This imperative is practical as opposed to the theoretical; for the theoretical does not say what ought to happen, but what has happened. Practical knowledge is also opposed to the speculative, and in this case it may even be theoretical, provided only that imperatives can be derived from it; for then the knowledge is entitled objectively practical, namely when it contains the ground of possible imperatives. Remark. What is speculative is always theoretical, but not conversely, for the theoretical may be theoretical in some other respect. — A speculative mode of knowledge would accordingly be one from which no rules of conduct [*Verhältniß*] can be derived, or which is not a ground of possible imperatives. In theology, for example, there are such merely speculative modes of knowledge, which have no influence upon the conduct of life: for instance, whether God is present in space, or whether omnipresence is only an influence upon all creatures, without God being determined through space. We may also define as follows: a mode of knowledge is theoretical if it does not indicate what ought to happen, but contains the knowledge of an object as it is. The use of knowledge is either theoretical or practical. Morality, for example, is a practical mode of knowledge, yet the use made of it may nevertheless be speculative; and it is only in expectation of practical use that our modes of knowledge acquire a value, and everything speculative looks, in the distance, towards the practical. (Speculation is also good for this purpose, since the understanding is thereby exercised for practical inquiries.) This value may be merely mediate, or it may be immediate, and in that case it is dignity. We do not, strictly speaking, know any thing that has dignity except man, for he does not belong, like all other things, among the means to other ends. All the acquirements of knowledge and whatever man gains for himself have only a mediate value, and all this must be referred to an immediate value. If the will makes good use of the knowledge acquired, it is worth more than all knowledge. The dignity of man therefore rests upon morality; and this we entitle [*kat' exochen*] practical. The part of philosophy which deals with morals is entitled practical philosophy, although there is something practical in the other parts also.",["'Erkenntniß' is rendered 'mode of knowledge' where it is counted or predicated of (e.g. 'wird praktisch genannt'); 'knowledge' elsewhere.","'denn heist' (twice 'denn' = 'dann') read as 'for then ... is entitled'; the first 'denn ist er Würde' read as 'in that case it is dignity'.","OCR garbled 'Korr 'e^ox^l v'; read as *kat' exochen* (κατ' ἐξοχήν), 'par excellence'. Placed in square brackets as a conjectural reading; the sentence's sense ('this we entitle practical') is otherwise unaffected.","'Wüle' read as 'Wille'; 'Imparativen' as 'Imperativen'; 'Algegenwart' as 'Allgegenwart'.","'Verhältnißes' (rules of conduct) is a choice; the German could also be 'relation'; German given in brackets.","'Anmerkung' retained as 'Remark'; the sentence 'denn das theoretische kann in einer andern Absicht theoretisch seyn' is rendered literally and is obscure in the original."]],"poelitz-s23-003":["The author [Meier] has a further special section on the use of our understanding and reason, in contrast with their speculative use. This does not belong in logic at all; logic considers merely the form of thinking, and can have no propositions except those which rest on principles *a priori*. But since everything practical has for its aim our will and the various ends which we must come to know through observation, the practical is distinguished from the speculative in respect of content. This we see from the treatment itself: the author speaks of pleasure, desire, and so on, none of which belongs here. — There follows also the division of modes of knowledge into dead, which do not move our will, and living, which do move it. This is good, only that here too the limits of logic are overstepped. We therefore omit this section. It belongs to psychology.",["'Der Autor' is Meier, whose *Auszug* underlies the lectures; the name is supplied in square brackets by judgment.","Restated lightly: 'dies sehn wir aus der Abhandelung selbst' is attached to the preceding sentence; 'Ferner ist hier die Einteilung' rendered 'There follows also the division'.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'betracht' to 'betrachtet'."]],"poelitz-s24-001":["On Concepts.",[]],"poelitz-s24-002":["Hitherto we have spoken of knowledge in its many kinds; now the author divides modes of knowledge into their elements, which, in fairness, ought to have been done earlier. 1) The first perfection, says the author, is when a mode of knowledge becomes representation and concept. 2) When concepts are combined, and judgments thus arise. 3) When judgments are combined, and inferences thus arise. The ancients said: *quot sunt mentis operationes? tres, apprehensio simplex, judicium, et ratiocinium* [How many are the operations of the mind? Three: simple apprehension, judgment, and ratiocination]. In order to explain a concept we note the following. 1) *Repraesentatio* is the first and most general, and cannot be explained. 2) A representation with consciousness is *perceptio*, perception. 3) Representation, so far as it is considered as referred to an object, is *cognitio*, or perception so far as it is objective. I must therefore become conscious also of the object. We have many representations which refer to no object, for example all inner sensations. They refer to the subject. If someone speaks to me, I have a representation referred to the object, and so knowledge; if he shouts so loudly that my ears hurt, that is sensation, and I feel my own state. Knowledge is either *intuitus* or *conceptus*: *intuitus*, when I have only single representations; *conceptus*, when I have representations that are common to many, or *repraesentatio communis*. *Conceptus est repraesentatio communis*, because the concept relates to the mark [*Merkmal*] of the object, and so represents the object mediately [*mediate*] through the mark, and this mark may be common to many things. *Intuitus* is *repraesentatio singularis*, for it is always a single object of the senses.",["'Erkennt niß' (OCR break) silently joined. 'beAvust' read as 'bewußt'.","Kant's 'intuitus' is kept in Latin as the lecture does; it corresponds to *Anschauung* = intuition.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge' / 'mode of knowledge' (plural 'Erkenntniße'); 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Empfindung' = sensation; 'Merkmal' = mark, German supplied.","'Wahrnehmung' glossed 'perception' beside *perceptio*; the English rendering of 'Begrif' is 'concept'.","The sentence beginning 'Um einen Begrif zu erklären merken wir' is divided into numbered points as in the source; the English translation of the Latin quotation is supplied in square brackets."]],"poelitz-s24-003":["We always begin from intuitions. Every *conceptus* is either *empiricus*, when it has arisen from the senses and has acquired mere universality through the understanding, or has arisen through comparison of the objects of experience; or it is *purus*, when it does not arise through the senses. In this division what matters is the origin, and the division therefore belongs not to logic but to metaphysics. We have many empirical concepts, namely of colour, sensation, etc. A *purus* [concept] is one that arises from the understanding independently of experience. Concepts are again of two kinds: those whose objects can be given in experience, for instance of substances, causes, effects, etc.; and those whose objects can be given in no experience. The latter is a concept of mere reason. A *conceptus purus* is a *notio*. A *notio* is thus after all more than a *conceptus*, but in German there is no corresponding expression. A *notio* whose object can be exhibited *in concreto* is a *notio intellectus*; where the object can be given in no experience, it is an idea. An idea may be entitled a concept of reason, for through reason I think something *a priori*. People otherwise speak of the idea of the colour red, etc., but that is wrong. — The whole of morals rests upon ideas, for pure virtue is not possible in any experience. A notion is sensitive if its objects can be made sensible, if not in empirical, then at least in pure intuition. For example, the concept of a triangle. — How do concepts arise, or how can representations become concepts? This I can ask only in logic: *per reflectionem, comparationem, separationem seu abstractionem*. I reflect upon things; that is, I become gradually conscious of various representations. Or I compare various representations with my consciousness. This being done, I compare them with one another, and that is comparison. Where I find identity of consciousness, I separate that out, or abstract from the rest; and so I have a concept. For example, I see a fir, a willow or a lime-tree. I see that they have a trunk, branches and leaves, which are different; one has more branches than another, etc. I now attend to what they have in common, namely trunk, branches and leaves, and abstract from the shape [of these]; thus I obtain the concept of a tree. Remark. In logic the word *abstrahere* is not used quite correctly. We ought not to say *abstrahere aliquid* but *abstrahere ab aliquo*. In the foregoing example we do not abstract the trunk, the branches, etc.; these we separate. What we abstract from is the shape of the leaves, etc. Abstraction thus adds nothing to our knowledge, but limits it. Attention to what is similar is entitled wit [*Witz*]. To make concepts, then, one must be able to compare, to attend and to abstract. For example, if in scarlet I think only of the red colour, I abstract from the cloth; if I abstract from this [colour] also, and think it only as a being [*Wesen*], I abstract from still more; and a concept of this kind is entitled abstract.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'meineni', 'solchenBegrif', 'abstahir', 'Verstände'; the editors' apparatus and page/line numbers omitted.","'purus war ein solcher': the past tense 'war' is odd in the German; I render it as a present-tense definition.","'Sie sind wieder solche…': 'Sie' has no clear antecedent; I take it to mean concepts in general, and supplied 'Concepts are again of two kinds'. The sentence 'das ist ein Begrif der bloßen Vernunft' is read as referring to the second kind.","'ist das, so vergleiche ich sie untereinander' is elliptical; rendered 'This being done'. The bracketed 'these' after 'shape' and the bracketed '[concept]' are supplied.","'abstrahir ich von dem, und denk ihn mir nur als ein Wesen': 'dem' and 'ihn' are ambiguous (colour, scarlet or cloth); I take them to be the red colour, but the reading is a judgment. 'Wiz' is modernised to *Witz*.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; 'Gemeingültigkeit' etc. appear in the next passages. Latin technical terms are left in italics."]],"poelitz-s24-004":["Every concept has an object of which it is the mark [*Merkmal*], and it can be given to me only in the object. If I consider the concept without the thing of which it is a mark, I consider it *in abstracto*; if I consider the thing at the same time, I consider it *in concreto*. And we must be able to consider it *in concreto*, otherwise it has no meaning at all.",["'kann mir nur in dem Gegenstande gegeben werden': the subject is taken to be the concept; the German could also be read of the mark. The colon before 'so betracht ich' is replaced by a comma and semicolon for English punctuation."]],"poelitz-s24-005":["The use of a concept is either *in abstracto* or *in concreto*. It is *in concreto* when I apply the concept to what is contained in it; *in abstracto* when one abstracts from the cases in which it could be applied. Modes of knowledge [*Erkenntniße*] are abstract when they are used *in abstracto*. Logic deals only with the concept as concept; it does not concern itself with ideas, etc. How, for example, we can represent through reason something that the understanding cannot even grasp is an important question, but it does not belong to logic. Logic does not deal with the origin of concepts as regards content, or with differences in respect of objects, but with the form of a concept as concept, which consists in universal validity [*Gemeingültigkeit*]. Every *conceptus* is a *repraesentatio communis*; that lies already in the definition. But the use may be *communis aut singularis*, that is, *in individuo*. If I apply the concept to many kinds of object, the use is *communis*, that is, *in abstracto*. It is a fault in logic that universal, particular and singular concepts are assumed, for there are none such; it is their use that can be so divided. Accordingly we shall make this division not of the *conceptus* but of the *judicia*, because these are the relations of concepts; for I can compare one concept with another wholly, or only in some parts, or only in a single part. This fault, however, has become so firmly established that it cannot be avoided. Now the question is: How do concepts arise? Here we speak of the logical form of concepts, which consists, as has already been said, in its being common to many. This is a prerogative of the understanding, which has therefore the faculty of making concepts, and it is so defined. We defined it at the outset, indeed, as the faculty of rules, but this comes to the same thing, for concepts serve as rules. Understanding is the faculty of discursive knowledge, the senses the faculty of intuitive knowledge. An abstract concept is a tautology, for every concept is abstract, and a concept that is not abstract is a *contradictio in adjecto*. How do concepts arise as regards content? (1) Through experience. This does not produce them, but merely lies at their foundation; it gives the representations out of which we make concepts. Their origin is empirical. (2) From the understanding, in such a way that the representation is not given through experience at all. For example, the concept of possibility, of necessity. (3) Through arbitrary invention [*willkürliche Erdichtung*]. In accordance with this, concepts can be divided into *dati* and *factitii* or *arbitrarii*. *Dati* are given either through the mere understanding, that is *a priori*, or through experience, *a posteriori*. This matter does not belong to logic, but we have touched upon it because it is needed in the chapter on definitions. Invented concepts arise through composition. New simple concepts cannot be invented, but they can be variously combined; and here the rule is that we can combine nothing that contradicts itself. Yet concepts that contradict one another are combined, not in order to hold them true, but to show how they would agree with other concepts, so that their falsity may be inferred from this. Every concept contains under it several possible concepts; that is, it contains what is common to several representations of different things. It is therefore itself in turn contained in others. Every *conceptus communis* is contained in the concepts from which it is abstracted, for it is a part of them. The *conceptus communis* thus has many things under it; that is, they can all be represented through it, in respect of what they agree in, and that is just what constitutes its usefulness. —",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Begrif s', 'Hegt' (liegt), 'Tavtologie', and the stray spacing in 'Begrif e'.","'er hat also das Vermögen Begriffe zu machen, so definirt man ihn auch': 'er' taken to be the understanding (Verstand), as the next sentences show; the sentence is split and the pronoun supplied.","'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge' (plural); 'Erkenntniß' singular as 'knowledge'. 'Gemeingültigkeit' rendered 'universal validity'; the German is added in brackets.","'sie giebt die Vorstellungen woraus…' and 'der Ursprung ist empirisch' are run on in the German; I divided them into separate sentences.","'Diese Sache… Capitel von den Definitionen': the reference is to the later chapter on definitions in the lectures. 'mehrern Vorstellungen' rendered 'several representations'.","'Dati sind gegeben entweder durch den bloßen Verstand oder a priori': the 'oder' is explanatory (i.e.), not disjunctive, in my reading; stated plainly as 'that is'.","The final dash is the transcript's own and is kept; the passage ends there as printed."]],"poelitz-s24-006":["A concept is entitled higher in so far as it contains others under it; a lower concept is one that is contained under another. For example, *man* is a lower concept in respect of what is predicated of *animal*. We have, then, *conceptus superiores et inferiores*, and they always stand in relation. A concept may be superior in relation to one concept and inferior in relation to another. For example, *man* is a *conceptus superior* in respect of a Negro, but inferior in respect of an animal. From this there arises a series, for example: iron, metal, body, thing. The *conceptus infimus* cannot be determined. I might well speak of a *cognitio infima*, for modes of knowledge contain intuitions as well as concepts. A *cognitio infima* would therefore be immediate intuition, since this contains only a single thing. A *conceptus infimus* would be one under which no other is any longer contained, or one which is employed in dealing with an individual (*individuum*). For example, in 'the philosopher Wolff' the concept *philosopher* is applied to the *individuum* Wolff. Such a concept is impossible to determine. For even if we have a concept which we apply immediately to individuals (*individua*), there may still be differences which we do not notice or which we neglect (*negligiren*). In the series of subordinated concepts there is therefore no *conceptus infimus*. But I can arrive at a highest, since I must in the end reach a concept from which I can abstract nothing further without the whole of the concept vanishing; and this is the *conceptus summus*. In use there are *conceptus infimi* which have become, as it were, lowest by convention, in that we have agreed to go no further down. The higher concept is called, in respect of its lower, a *genus*; the lower is called, in respect of its higher, a *species*. *Genus* and *species* therefore make in themselves no difference in concepts, but only in their relation. For example, a scholar is a *genus* in respect of philosophers, but a *species* in respect of men. Now we can think a series of *species* and *genera*, and so we shall be able to have higher genera (*genera superiora*). Every *species* is always a *genus* by reason of its lower concept (*conceptus inferior*). A *species infima* is likewise only comparative; that is, in use there is strictly none. A *genus summum* we can have. A *species* which I consider in immediate relation to individuals is *infima*.\n\nIn all concepts we have to note the extent (*sphaera*). A concept is entitled *latior* [wider] in respect of what is contained under it, and the *conceptus inferior* in respect of its superior is entitled *angustior* [narrower]. This relation of subordinated concepts, in which one is contained under another and [the other contains] still more besides, and in which one has the other under it and [is itself capable of] still more besides, is the *sphaera*, or the extent of the use of the concept. We note further the content, that is, the number of marks contained in it; and here it is to be observed that the greater the extent of a concept, the smaller is its content. A *conceptus superior* always has a great extent, but its content is the smaller; for the higher the concept, the more I must abstract. For example, the concept of *something* extends to everything, but has no content. Lower concepts thus [arise] when one determines the higher. To determine is to add more marks, in order to distinguish better the concepts contained under the *conceptus superior*. By continued abstraction higher concepts arise, and conversely by continued determination lower concepts (*inferiores*) arise. A *conceptus omni modo determinatus* is a *conceptus infimus*.\n\nNow the question arises: which is better, a very concrete or a very abstract representation? [By the latter I mean] one which comes very near to the *genus summum*; the former comes very near to the individual. Through very abstract modes of knowledge we know little in many things, and through very concrete modes of knowledge we know much in few things. What we gain on the one side, we therefore lose again on the other.\n\nTwo concepts, each of which can be both superior and inferior [to the other], are entitled *reciproci*. This always requires that they signify one and the same thing.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'last' to 'lässt', 'endüch' to 'endlich', 'Begrif s' to 'Begriffs', 'ver Hehren' to 'verlieren', 'sein:' to 'sehr'. 'Wolf' is Kant's/transcriber's spelling; I give 'Wolff'.","Restated as several sentences: the long opening discussion of *conceptus infimus*, and the sentence on the *sphaera*. The latter is irregular in the German (repeated 'und noch mehr dazu'); I supplied '[the other contains]' and '[is itself capable of]' to complete the sense, as the transcript seems to garble Kant's point that a concept is both contained under a higher and contains a lower.","'Niedrige Begriffe werden also dadurch, wenn man die höhern bestimmt' is elliptical; '[arise]' is supplied.","'Conceptus infimus war ein solcher' : 'war' is rendered 'would be' (probably a slip for 'ist' or a conditional). 'in Ansehung deßen vom Thier' is obscure; rendered 'in respect of what is predicated of *animal*' by judgment, and the reading is uncertain.","In 'eine solche, die dem generi summo ... jene ...' the German places the abstract first and 'jene' (the former) refers to concrete as stated; I supplied '[By the latter I mean]', and the pronoun order in the German is loose, so the assignment of 'former/latter' is my resolution.","'Neger' rendered 'Negro' as the period term; 'Erkenntniße' = 'modes of knowledge'; 'Vorstellung' = 'representation'; 'Anschauung' = 'intuition'; 'Umfang' = 'extent'; 'Inhalt' = 'content'; 'genannt/heißen' = 'is entitled'."]],"poelitz-s25-001":["Of Definitions.",[]],"poelitz-s25-002":["A definition is a logically perfect concept. It rests on the extent to which a concept serves for judging well. For this, distinctness is required, that I become conscious of the manifold in the concept; and this is a quality. I can now also think a quantity of distinctness, namely to represent clearly all the marks of a concept; the distinctness is then complete, and this is the second thing required for logical perfection. The third is precision. For absolute perfection (relative perfection may vary) I can demand nothing more than that the concept be whole or complete, or adequate to the thing. This requires that there be neither too much nor too little. The author [Meier] and others entitle the determination of the concept 'precision'; this is false. The logically perfect concept is a *conceptus rem adaequans*, and in this way one might well [reach] a definition. The essential perfection of a concept is distinctness; that of a definition is completeness (*completudo*) and precision (*praecisio*); and every definition is to be examined accordingly.\n\nFirst, considered as a proposition, it must be true: the mark must belong to the concept of the thing. If it does not, everything falls away. For certain purposes, however, bad definitions are also good, for we do learn new marks of the thing which we should not otherwise have hit upon, and our knowledge of the thing gains in distinctness. A false definition can accordingly be correct as a true proposition.\n\nDefinitions are tautological when I must lay the concept at the basis in order to understand the mark, whereas I was seeking the mark in order to understand the concept. Tautology can be very well concealed, if one merely takes other words that include the same concept. For example, when *ratio* is explained thus: *ratio est id ex quo cognoscitur, cur aliquid sit* [reason is that from which it is known why something is]; but *cur* signifies nothing other than *ex qua ratione* [from what reason]. Such definitions do not yield distinctness. Of the completeness (*completudo*) of distinctness we can never become quite certain.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Begrif s' to 'Begriffs', 'niehr' to 'mehr', 'tavtologisch' kept as 'tautological'; 'def innen' is too garbled to be sure of (perhaps 'definiren' or 'finden'); I translated 'one might well [reach] a definition', so the reading is doubtful.","'Der Autor' is probably Meier, whose text underlies the lectures; the name is supplied in square brackets by judgment, not given in the passage.","'Qualitaet' and 'Quantitaet' are rendered 'quality' and 'quantity'; 'Deutlichkeit' = 'distinctness'; 'Erkenntniß' = 'knowledge'.","'In der Absicht' is rendered 'For certain purposes' by judgment; the sense could also be 'in intention/regard'.","'Praecision die Determination des Begrifs' is rendered 'entitle the determination ... precision'; the sense seems to be that they confuse precision with determination.","Restated: the opening and the sentence on 'absolute Vollkommenheit' were left close to the German but divided into shorter sentences. In 'der definition completudo, und praecision' I supplied 'that of' to complete the construction."]],"poelitz-s25-003":["The logical rule for constructing definitions is this: Make your concept distinct, and make the distinctness complete and precise. Remark. All logical rules suffer from the defect that they presuppose that one can already accomplish the very things one wishes to learn from them. Here, for example, one asks: How do I make the concept distinct? By what do I recognise the completeness (*completudo*) of the distinctness? How do I make it precise? Logic cannot teach any of this. All our concepts are either given or made. Those are given which do not depend on our arbitrary choice [Willkür]; they are given either merely in my understanding, *a priori*, or in experience, *a posteriori*. The concept of metal is given *a posteriori*; the concept of cause and effect, however, is given *a priori*. The made concepts, *factitii*, are likewise made either *a priori* or *a posteriori*, [the latter] when the object is given in experience. In the first case the concept is made through mere thinking, without the object being given in experience. All our concepts, so far as they are given, whether *a priori* or *a posteriori*, can be defined only *per analysin*. Since the concept is given, one can make it distinct only by making its marks clear one after another. If all the marks are made clear, the concept becomes completely distinct; and if, further, it contains not too much, it is precise, and a definition results. If the concepts are made, the definition is brought about *per synthesin*. For example, if I wish to conceive a thinking being that is surrounded by no body, I have first made the concept for myself through the definition, and made it synthetically. The whole of mathematics is full of such made concepts, and it defines everything *per synthesin*. When can I say that a concept becomes a definition? When its marks are complete and precise. Can one also become certain by tests that all the marks have been analysed? That is not possible. From this it follows that all analytic definitions are uncertain, since one cannot become sure whether all the marks have been analysed. Synthetic definitions, so far as the object is invented, can never fail; for I say: so it shall be. Finally, definitions of concepts that are to be drawn from experience are synthetically impossible, because one keeps discovering still more marks.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'liier' → 'hier', 'Wülkühr' → 'Willkür', 'hegen' (next passage) → 'liegen'. The Pölitz spelling 'Begrif' is rendered 'concept'.","'Die gemachten Begriffe … a posteriori gemacht, wenn der Gegenstand in der Erfahrung gegeben ist': the German is elliptical; I supply '[the latter]' to show the 'wenn' clause qualifies the *a posteriori* case, as the next sentence ('In the first case…') requires.","'Merkmal' = 'mark' (Latin *nota*); 'deutlich' = 'distinct'; 'Willkür' given in brackets as a choice ('arbitrary choice').","Several short questions and answers are kept as in the German; 'Das geht nicht an' rendered 'That is not possible'."]],"poelitz-s25-004":["There are certain kinds of procedure by which concepts become distinct and approach completeness (*completudo*); or, in other words, there are certain logical approximations to definition. (1) *Expositiones*. An *expositio* is the successive analysis of the concept; it precedes the definition and can sometimes take its place. To know what belongs to a concept, definition is not necessary; but to know to what thing the concept belongs, it is necessary. For example, if I wish to know what belongs to virtue, it is not necessary; but in order to be able to say that this or that is virtue, it is necessary. The proposition that a definition must always precede before one can say anything about a thing is false. One can have well-grounded insight into much about a thing without having a definition; for an *expositio* can precede the definition, and this may be very incomplete for my purpose and yet very good. (2) *Descriptiones*. A description is not a definition, because it lacks precision; it may also be incomplete. The chief mark, however, is precision. Before everything that is said by means of an arbitrary concept (*conceptus arbitrarius*) the definition must come first, for one must surely give an account of what one has thought. The definition might better be entitled a declaration, since by it one merely declares one's thoughts; and that is what definition is for mathematicians. Arbitrary concepts, then, are not only always possible but also necessary. In philosophy definition is the latest thing, which can be attained only after long-continued analysis of concepts.",["'Es sind ... oder es sind' restated as 'or, in other words' to smooth the repetition.","'Die ist nicht Definition' (descriptiones): the feminine/plural subject is resolved as 'A description'; the Latin plural heading is kept in italics.","'conceptus arbitrarii' (plural in the next passage) / 'arbitrarii' rendered as 'arbitrary concept' in singular here for the German 'conceptus arbitrarii' genitive; 'declaration' kept as Kant's own word (*declaration* in the German).","'in Philosophy ... das späteste': 'latest thing' is a literal choice; 'last of all' would also serve."]],"poelitz-s25-005":["The objects of analytic definition are pure concepts of the understanding, for example substance. Of these one can give definitions because the whole concept must lie in the understanding. Metaphysics therefore has concepts all of which are capable of an analytic definition. One must never begin from an analytically defined concept, even if one could, for to say that something is a definition requires a proof that I have analysed completely. I must therefore analyse first. Can not concepts of experience also be analytically defined? Concepts of experience ought not to be defined analytically, for one gets nowhere with that. Of water, for example, we say: it is a fluid, transparent substance which has no colour, smell or taste. Here we must go beyond the concept and consult experience. To explain something by means of experience is entitled defining synthetically. But neither can it be defined synthetically, since we should then have to know everything that every possible experience can teach concerning the object. Only arbitrary concepts (*conceptus arbitrarii*) can be defined synthetically, and here the definition must always come first, because by it I have to say what I understand by the word.",["OCR corrected: 'hegen' → 'liegen', 'defmirten' → 'definirten'.","'Von einem analytisch definirten Begrif muß man nie anfangen': the sense is that one must not start with such a concept as already defined; rendered literally and kept close.","'heist' rendered 'is entitled' per the method; 'Erfahrungs Begriffe' = 'concepts of experience'.","'die nicht Farbe, Geruch und Geschmack hat': the German is as printed; sense is 'has no colour, smell or taste'."]],"poelitz-s25-006":["All definitions are divided into nominal and real (*nominales et reales*); the latter are also entitled genetic (*geneticae*), because when the marks are taken together the concept of the thing is generated anew. A nominal definition is the distinct concept that suffices to distinguish the thing from others. A real definition is the distinct concept that suffices to explain everything that belongs to the thing, or from which everything that belongs to the thing can be derived. The latter is internally sufficient, the former externally, in comparison with other things. The latter is essential; by the former I do not understand a thing any better, but can only distinguish it from others. Nominal definitions, however, can be sufficient only comparatively. By them one cannot distinguish the thing from all possible things; but one can by the marks which, taken together, make up the whole essence. That, however, is the real definition. Wolff defines it as one which explains the origin [Entstehn] of the thing; but that is false. For example, he says: The circle is the curved line all of whose points are equally distant from the centre. That he calls a nominal definition; and the real definition, accordingly, runs: it arises when a straight line moves about a fixed point. This is not right. The first is the real definition, and the origin, or the other definition, already follows from it. If I take a property (*attributum proprium*) of a thing, I can define it by a single one. For example: man is an animal that reasons [schließt]; and such definitions flow from the whole essence. I therefore always define through the whole essence, because in defining through an attribute I actually always have it before me. It is difficult to distinguish nominal and real definitions, and hence most authors err here. The definition of a made concept (*conceptus factitius*) is always real. If a concept is internally sufficient to distinguish the thing, it is certainly so externally also; but if it is not internally sufficient, it can nevertheless be externally sufficient, though only comparatively. In moral matters real definitions must always be sought; all our endeavour must be directed to that. In empirical matters, however, they are scarcely possible, or one may say not possible at all.",["'conrparativ' is an OCR slip for 'comparativ'; corrected silently.","'Sie ist innerlich, die erste äußerlich': 'sie' (real definition) and 'die erste' (nominal) are resolved by the following sentence; 'The latter … the former' keeps the order clear. Kant's 'die erste' strictly refers to the nominal definition, mentioned first.","'Das Entstehn erklärt' / 'sich ... bewegt': Wolff's genetic definition; I supply no content beyond the German. 'Mensch ist ein Thier das schließt' rendered 'an animal that reasons'; *schließen* = to draw inferences, a choice.","The ambiguous clause 'Man kann durch sie ... aber wol durch die Merkmale' is read as contrasting nominal definitions with the marks constituting the whole essence, as the following sentence ('That is the real definition') indicates."]],"poelitz-s25-007":["Of description. Description is the exposition of the marks of a thing, so far as it contains the materials of a definition; it has no determinate rules. In definition, on the other hand, everything is determinate. In the case of empirical concepts, therefore, as has already been said above, what must be sought is not definition but description. Concepts *a priori*, however, can become definitions, and *factitii* [concepts made by us] must become definitions. With concepts *a priori* one may also digress into description; but in a speculative science this cannot be done, for there definitions are demanded.",["'Materiahen' is an OCR slip for 'Materialien'; corrected silently.","'excurriren' rendered 'digress'; the Latin verb (*excurrere*) is Germanized in the original.","I added the bracketed gloss on *factitii*, which Kant leaves untranslated; the gloss is mine."]],"poelitz-s25-008":["Precision belongs to definition; completeness (*completudo*) is more important, and distinctness is the most important of all. Precision is in many cases very difficult to attain. Sometimes it is lacking because we have unwittingly taken the same mark twice. We sometimes hold things to be different merely because they have different names. For example, 'Body is extended and divisible': here precision is already lacking, for the last mark is contained in the first. — How is a definition that is put before us to be judged?",["'Wiebeurtheilt' read as 'Wie beurtheilt'. 'Praecision' rendered 'precision'."]],"poelitz-s25-009":["1.) First one asks whether the definition is true as a proposition,",[]],"poelitz-s25-010":["2.) whether it is distinct as a concept. If I want to explain *idem per idem*,",["The sentence continues in the next passage; translated as it stands."]],"poelitz-s25-011":["the concept has not yet been made distinct; and this is",[]],"poelitz-s25-012":["a principal fault in definition.",[]],"poelitz-s25-013":["3.) If a definition is true as a proposition and distinct as a concept, I then ask",[]],"poelitz-s25-014":["whether it is complete as a distinct concept. That is a hard",[]],"poelitz-s25-015":["task where the concepts are given.",[]],"poelitz-s25-016":["4.) Finally it is inquired whether the exhaustive [*ausführliche*] concept is determinate.",["'ausführlich' rendered 'exhaustive'; 'full' or 'detailed' is also possible."]],"poelitz-s25-017":["That can readily be ascertained. If it is not found to be so, not",["'last sich wol finden' read as 'lässt sich wohl finden'."]],"poelitz-s25-018":["much depends upon it.",[]],"poelitz-s25-019":["Precision may be defined as the reduction of a definition *ad minimos terminos* [to the fewest terms].",["The bracketed English is my gloss on the Latin."]],"poelitz-s25-020":["The rules by which a definition can be made are the following. It is understood that they hold only of analytic definitions:",[]],"poelitz-s25-021":["Rule 1.) Seek true marks of things. The propositions that are obtained by analysing the concept might be entitled elementary propositions, since they contain the elements for a definition; and of these one must seek as many as one can find.",["'Säze' rendered 'propositions'."]],"poelitz-s25-022":["Rule 2.) One must see whether one mark is not already contained in another.",[]],"poelitz-s25-023":["Rule 3.) One must collect the marks of a thing and see whether they constitute the concept of the thing, or, what is the same, whether they are equal to the concept of the thing. This is the most difficult step. Here we must be most distrustful whether our analysis has been complete or not. When, therefore, one sees how uncertain it is to assert that the analysis is complete, one must put forward one's definition as a tentative attempt, and not rely upon it at all as if it were free from error, but use it as though it were not a definition. Even so I still have a distinct and true proposition of which I can make useful employment. I shall be able to draw corollaries from the marks. I shall be able to say, *cui competit definitum, competit definitio*; that is, to whatever the concept of the *definitum* applies, the definition also applies. But conversely I shall not be able to say, *cui competit definitio, competit definitum*. My definition does not exhaust the whole *definitum*.",["'Hier lo müßen' contains a stray OCR fragment 'lo'; read as 'Hier müssen'.","I joined the clauses 'so muß man ... aufstellen, ... sondern ... bedienen' into shorter sentences, and the 'denn' clause I rendered 'Even so'.","'corrolaria' read as *corollaria* and rendered 'corollaries'."]],"poelitz-s25-024":["Rule 4.) The definition must be precise, though not much depends on this. We see from this that by means of a distinct concept which comes very near to a definition, but is not yet one, we can accomplish a great deal. The logical rules of a definition are:",["'hegt' read as 'liegt'."]],"poelitz-s25-025":["1.) The concept must become distinct through it. If it does not become distinct, this happens either immediately, in which case there is a *circulus in definiendo* [circle in defining], when I employ the concept of the *definitum* in the explanation or lay it at the foundation; or mediately, in which case it is tautology. This circle occurs very frequently, more frequently than we think.",["'circulus in definiendo' is partly supplied from 'circulus in definiendo' in the source with a Latin gloss in brackets by me. The text reads 'circulus in definiendo'."]],"poelitz-s25-026":["2.) *Definitio nec sit angustior nec latior suo definito* [let the definition be neither narrower nor wider than what it defines]. This rule is superfluous; it is already contained in the concept of definition. *Definitio* and *definitum* must be reciprocal, or have one and the same sphere. A definition becomes *angustior* when it contains more determinations; for example, 'Men are rational beings who can speak.' This definition is *angustior suo definito*, for by it the dumb are excluded from men. It is *latior* when the concept of the *definitum* is not determined precisely enough by the definition; for example, 'Men are living beings.'",["'nee' read as 'nec' (OCR).","'Bestimmungen' rendered 'determinations'; 'reciprocable' rendered 'reciprocal'; 'sphaeram' as 'sphere'. The bracketed English is my gloss."]],"poelitz-s26-001":["Of logical division.",["'Einteilung' rendered 'division'."]],"poelitz-s26-002":["Every concept contains under it a manifold, in so far as this manifold agrees, but also in so far as it differs; and the consideration of the concept in respect of all the manifold that it contains under it, in so far as this differs, is division. Or again: division is the complete exposition of the manifold contained under a concept, in so far as it is different. All the manifold contained under a concept, in so far as it is different, is opposed to itself. Division can therefore also be defined as the complete sum of the *opposita* [opposites], in so far as they are contained under a *conceptus communis* [common concept]. For example, the learned and the unlearned are *opposita*, and are contained under the concept of men. In every division I must therefore see that the *membra dividentia* [dividing members] are *opposita*, that they belong under a *conceptus communis*, and that they make up the sphere of the *conceptus divisus* [the concept divided]. For example, besides right-angled, acute-angled and obtuse-angled triangles there must be none others. The opposition must always be the contradictory opposite, not the *contrarium* or contrary. For example, not knowledge and error, since both are something positive, and the latter is false knowledge; but knowledge and ignorance. The concept divided must therefore contain neither less nor more than all the members taken together. The members must therefore be opposed, and one must not be contained in another. For example, if one wished to divide men into the learned, the unlearned and philosophers, the last are already contained under the first. — We must distinguish division, co-division [*Nebeneinteilung*] and subdivision [*Untereinteilung*]. Co-division occurs when I divide the concept several times, or with different aims; for example, when I divide triangles *ratione laterum et angulorum* [according to sides and angles]. Subdivision consists in my dividing the *membra dividentia*. Subdivision can be continued to infinity; comparatively it may be finite. Co-division also proceeds to infinity, especially with empirical concepts [*Erfahrungs Begriffen*]; for who can exhaust all the relations of concepts? All division is a division into two members, or dichotomy; division into more than two members is polytomy. Every polytomy is a mediate, not a primitive, division. The first division is dichotomy, for the *membra dividentia* are to be *opposita*. Of *a* the opposite is nothing more than *non-a*. Triangles must be divided into right-angled and not right-angled, or oblique-angled. Every polytomy has the disadvantage that it is empirical, whereas dichotomy is apodeictic, for every thing must be either *a* or *non-a*.",["The passage was long and I kept it as one paragraph, though I split the original's longer runs of clauses at the semicolons and full stops that Kant's transcriber already gives.","'Begrif s' and 'f ortgesezt' are OCR breaks; corrected silently.","'Wiederspiel' (contrary) is rendered through the Latin *contrarium*. 'Erkenntniß' is 'knowledge'.","'comparativ kann sie endlich seyn' is rendered literally ('comparatively it may be finite'); the sense is probably that in a relative sense a subdivision may come to an end.","The sentence 'Von a ist doch das Gegenteil nichts mehr als non a' is left as it stands; 'doch' is not separately rendered."]],"poelitz-s27-001":["Of judgments.",["Heading only. 'Urteile' rendered 'judgments'."]],"poelitz-s27-002":["The representation of the relation of different concepts to one consciousness is a judgment. For example, I have two concepts, man and mortal, and I consider their relation to a consciousness to which they belong; for what I think through the concept of man I also think through the concept of mortality, and the judgment runs thus: Men are mortal. One can also say: judgment is the unification of different concepts in one consciousness. Every concept is of such a kind that it unites different representations in one consciousness; if different concepts can in turn be united in one consciousness, we have the judgment. — We now come to the acts of the understanding that are present in every judgment. They can be reduced to the following four. Judgments can be considered:",["Restated the closing run-on sentence of the example ('so betracht ich ... dazu sie gehören') as a slightly smoother clause; no content added.","Verstandes Handlungen = 'acts of the understanding'; Vorstellung = representation; Begriff = concept; Bewußtsein = consciousness."]],"poelitz-s27-003":["1.) In respect of quality they are *affirmantes*, *negantes* and *infinitae*.",["The Latin terms are kept in italics as Kant's technical terms: affirmative, negative, infinite (limitative)."]],"poelitz-s27-004":["2.) In respect of quantity they are *universales*, *particulares* and *singulares*.",["The footnote sign '1)' after *particulares* is editorial apparatus and is omitted; the footnote text itself has run into the following passages (see s27-009 and s27-010)."]],"poelitz-s27-005":["3.) In respect of relation they are categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive.",["OCR 'Ivypothetische' and 'disjuncti\\e' silently corrected to hypothetische and disjunctive."]],"poelitz-s27-006":["4.) In respect of modality they are problematic, assertoric and apodictic.",[]],"poelitz-s27-007":["Remark: Although the logicians show that in use the *affirmantes* count for as much as the *infinitae*, and the *universales* for as much as the *singulares*, there is nevertheless a logical difference here. The *actus* of the understanding are nevertheless threefold, although they count for the same.",["OCR 'äff irmantes' and 'vniversales' silently corrected.","'gelten' rendered 'count for'; the sense is that they are equivalent in use but not in logical form."]],"poelitz-s27-008":["Each of these four functions of the understanding gives a distinction",["The sentence breaks off here and the next passage begins with 'Urteile'; a word such as 'der' (of) may have dropped out. Translated as it stands, without completion."]],"poelitz-s27-009":["[of] judgments. In affirmative judgments I compare one concept with another positively; judgments are negative when I separate one concept from another; infinite, when I limit one concept through another. For example: all men are mortal; men are not immortal, or *anima est non mortalis*. All affirmative propositions show [here the text of footnote 1, to *particulares*, intrudes:] it would be better to call them *plurales*, for here it is not seen what holds of all,",["'[of]' is supplied to connect with the broken-off preceding passage (s27-008); doubtful.","The OCR shows the footnote text ('i) beßer hieße es Plurales ...') running into the body text in mid-sentence; I have marked the intrusion in brackets rather than dropping it. The main sentence resumes in s27-010 with 'ihre Verbindung durch die copula'.","'beßer hieße es Plurales' is read as referring to *particulares* in the list of quantities."]],"poelitz-s27-010":["if it holds of many; yet often it holds also of all. But that expression has once for all been accredited. [End of footnote.] [All affirmative propositions show] their connection through the copula: 'Is' 'an'. When they are affected with *non*, they signify the opposition of concepts and the judgment is negative; through this the subject is always excluded from the sphere of the predicate, as in *anima non est mortalis*. Here I exclude the soul from the sphere of the concept of mortality, or say: the soul is not contained under the concept of mortality. In infinite judgments I think that the subject is contained in a different sphere from that of the predicate. For example, *anima est non mortalis*: here I think that the soul does not belong to the mortals, but I think still more, namely that it belongs to the immortals; I think it as contained in a different sphere from that [which is contained] in the concept [of the predicate]. Affirmation and negation are qualities of judgments. If the negation does not affect the copula, the judgment is not a negative but an affirmative one, for the copula serves for connection. On this ground the affirmative judgment counts for as much as the infinite. Yet through the latter one thinks more than through a negative judgment, as has been shown above. They are entitled *infinita* because of one subject infinitely many predicates affected with *non* can be asserted. For example: a stone is not iron, not gold, not flesh, and so on and so on.",["The footnote's end and the resumption of the main sentence are marked by my bracketed insertions; the text is run together in the OCR, and the connection ('ihre Verbindung ...') is clear only by judgment.","'Ist an' is doubtful (possibly a garbled or elliptical expression for the copula 'ist' as affirming/attaching); left in quotation marks.","'als im Begrif enthalten' is elliptical; I supplied '[which is contained] ... [of the predicate]'. OCR 'Unsterbliehen' corrected to Unsterblichen.","'Aus dem Grunde gilt das bejahende so viel als das unendliche' kept literally, though the reasoning is compressed in the student's notes."]],"poelitz-s27-011":["In respect of quantity they are universal, particular and singular judgments; for example, All men, some men, Caius is mortal. In *universales* and *singulares* the predicate holds of the subject without exception. For in the former I say: all men are mortal; in the latter there is a *conceptus singularis*, of which too no exception is possible, since there is only one Caius. Hence in use they also count for the same. The logicians are accustomed to indicate quantity and quality by vowels. The verse for this is:",["'Es ist nur ein Cajus' given a causal 'since' to make the connection plain.","OCR 'coneeptus' corrected to *conceptus*."]],"poelitz-s27-012":["*Asserit A, negat E, sed universaliter ambo.* (A affirms, E denies, but both universally.)",["Mnemonic verse kept in Latin; the English gloss in parentheses is mine. OCR 'Aßerit' normalised to the Latin spelling."]],"poelitz-s27-013":["*Asserit I, negat O, sed particulariter ambo.* (I affirms, O denies, but both particularly.)",["OCR '0' read as the vowel O, as the verse requires."]],"poelitz-s27-014":["In respect of relation they are categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive. Categorical judgments are those whose matter consists of two concepts, one of which is the subject, the other the predicate; for example, all men are mortal. Here 'all' indicates the quantity, 'are' is the copula, and man and mortal are the matter, the former being the subject, the latter the predicate. Hypothetical judgments are those whose matter consists of two judgments; for example, if God is just, evil will be punished. If the first proposition is assumed, the second must also be assumed. There are here two modes of knowledge [*Erkenntniße*] which are connected with one another as ground and consequents. Disjunctive judgments are those whose matter consists of two or more judgments considered in opposition, where I think that, when they are taken together, they make up everything that can be thought of an object. They express a logical division; for example, if the world were not the best, God either could not or would not have created a better. The division into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive must properly be put first, because relation is what is essential in judgments. The author speaks at once of subject and predicate, as if these made up the matter of all judgments. It has been believed that all other judgments are also categorical and only differently dressed up, but that is false: they are quite different species. The matter of the categorical judgment is subject and predicate. The form is the copula *est*, and it expresses the relation of the subject to the predicate.",["Divided the long sentence into several, and supplied 'Categorical judgments are those', 'Hypothetical judgments are those', 'Disjunctive judgments are those' to carry the construction of the German list; no content added.","'Erkenntniße' = 'modes of knowledge' (here simply the two judgments); 'Grund und Folgen' = 'ground and consequents'.","'der Autor' is Meier, whose handbook Kant is lecturing on. OCR slips (Siedrucken, eingekleiclt, disjunetive) silently corrected; 'billig' rendered 'properly'."]],"poelitz-s27-015":["In respect of modality they are problematic, assertoric and apodictic. Modal judgments are those in which the copula is affected with a limitation; for example, all men are necessarily immortal. Here 'necessarily' is the limitation, which shows the manner in which I assert or deny something. A problematic judgment is one about whose truth or untruth I determine nothing, or one that is thought only in respect of possibility. By this I understand not that the thing is possible, but that the judgment is possible, that it may come into someone's thoughts; for example, The soul is corporeal. An assertoric judgment is one about whose truth I settle something. An apodictic judgment is one in which necessity is found. For example, 'the soul of man may be mortal' is problematic, 'it is mortal' is assertoric, 'it is necessarily mortal' is apodictic. In hypothetical judgments the matter consists of two judgments which are connected with one another as ground and consequent. In the disjunctive I consider the judgments in the relation of the parts to the whole, and in the categorical there are two concepts in the relation of subject and predicate; and these are the *bases* of the two preceding. In the hypothetical I make use of the word 'if'. 'If' indicates only the relation in which the two judgments are thought. The first judgment of a hypothetical is problematic: I leave it undecided whether it is true or not. To speak assertorically is also to speak *per thesin*; to speak problematically is to speak *per hypothesin*. The connection of the ground and the consequents is the consequence [*Consequenz*]. The form of a hypothetical judgment therefore consists in the consequence. The judgment which contains the ground is entitled *antecedens*, *prius*; the consequent is entitled *consequens*, *posterius*. In hypothetical judgments nothing is said assertorically except the consequence. Remark. It is good when we have a distinctive word for each of our concepts; often, however, this cannot be done. —",["Divided the long opening sentences, and supplied 'A problematic judgment is one', 'An assertoric judgment is one', 'An apodictic judgment is one' for the elliptical German.","The example 'alle Menschen sind nothwendig unsterblich' is as printed (the student's notes may be inaccurate); retained.","'die sind die bases der beiden vorhergehenden' is ambiguous (the antecedent of 'die' is probably the categorical relation, which is the basis of the hypothetical and disjunctive); left literal.","Folge = 'consequent' and Consequenz = 'consequence' are my choices to keep the two German words apart.","OCR 'limitalötion', 'h\\qiothetischen', 'Urteüen' silently corrected."]],"poelitz-s27-016":["The logicians define a proposition [*Satz*] as *Judicium verbis prolatum*, which is however false; we should not judge at all if we had no words. The difference is this: a judgment, in the relation of two concepts, is regarded as problematic; a proposition, as assertoric. Before I have a proposition I must already have judged; I judge about many things which I do not settle. But in every proposition I must settle the truth of the concepts. A problematic proposition is therefore a contradiction. In hypothetical judgments the consequence is a proposition. Some believe it easy to convert a hypothetical proposition into a categorical one. Professor Kant maintains that this cannot be done at all, since by their nature they are wholly different. They seem to be of one kind, but on closer examination they are not; for in categorical judgments nothing is problematic, everything is assertoric. For example, there is a great difference between the proposition 'All bodies are divisible' and the proposition 'If all bodies are composite, they are divisible'. In the first I assert outright that they are divisible. In all judgments that are hypothetical there are two *modi*: the *modus ponens*, [namely] if the antecedent is true, the consequent is also true; and the *modus tollens*, [namely] if the latter is false, the former is also false. This comes up again in syllogistic. — In the disjunctive judgment various propositions are considered in so far as they are opposed, and in so far as, taken together, they make up the sphere of the *conceptus divisus*.",["'Einer ley' read as 'einerlei' ('of one kind'). I divided the sentence on the two *modi* and supplied 'namely' in brackets.","'Ein problematischer Saz ist also eine Contradiction' is kept as it stands; the transcript's sense is that a proposition, being assertoric, cannot be problematic. 'Saz' is rendered 'proposition' and 'Urteil' 'judgment'."]],"poelitz-s27-017":["Some judgments contain in themselves an affirmation and a negation at once, but in a concealed manner, and these are entitled exponible judgments. For example, 'Few men are learned': here the negation lies hidden in it, 'Many men are not learned', and the affirmation, 'Some men are learned'. Exponible judgments do not belong to logic, for their character belongs not to logic but to language, since two judgments can be expressed briefly at once. For example, 'Few men are virtuous' consists of two judgments, namely 'Some men are virtuous' and 'Many men are not virtuous'. It belongs properly, therefore, to grammar that in our language there can be judgments which must be expounded. Something may attach to a judgment that is not logical, that is, [something] aesthetic. The logical is what belongs to the understanding, the aesthetic what belongs to the senses. The aesthetic, or what is for the sensations, must be carefully separated from the logical; one must consider solely the relation of knowledge to the understanding. It is ridiculous, for example, to use exclamations where what matters is correctness of understanding.",["OCR 'emige' corrected to 'einige'. 'Das Aesthetische oder was für die Empfindungen ist' is kept close to the German. 'Verstandesrichtigkeit' is rendered 'correctness of understanding'."]],"poelitz-s27-018":["*Judicia practica* contain an ought, or the prescription of a possible action; *theoretica* say how things are or are not. The former always presuppose an end [*Absicht*]. *Judicia demonstrabilia* are those which are capable of proof, *indemonstrabilia* those which are not capable of proof; for example, 'A body is extended'. An immediately certain judgment is indemonstrable. The author [Meier] speaks further of empty judgments. Some immediately certain judgments have their immediate certainty from identity, as, for example, the instance just cited. The *identitas* is either *explicita* or *implicita*. If the *identitas* is *explicita*, the proposition is tautological; for example, 'Man is a man'. Such a proposition is unprovable and also needs no proof, and it is wholly impossible, for one cannot go higher than to show the identity of subject and predicate. The implicitly identical proposition is demonstrable; for example, 'Man is a rational being'. The tautological proposition is *virtualiter* empty, that is, it is without use, utility or consequences. For example, if I know nothing more to say of man than that he is a man, I know nothing at all. The implicitly identical proposition I must analyse so that it becomes explicitly identical. The proof therefore rests on this: *identitatem implicite in explicite* to transform. Implicitly identical propositions are not empty, for they make clear the predicate which lay in the concept of the thing. The author says: 'The greatest part of our modes of knowledge rests on empty propositions'; in this he has expressed himself very incorrectly. He should have said: on *implicite* identity. A great part of our modes of knowledge therefore rests on the analysis of them, in so far as I thereby see the identity of subject and predicate. —",["'Und er ist völlig unmöglich' is translated as printed; the sense may be 'unnecessary' ('unnöthig'), but I do not emend. Flagged as doubtful.","OCR 'Avenn' read as 'Wenn'; 'inrpliciter' read as 'implicite'. 'Ein größer Teil' is rendered 'a great part'; the comparative may be an OCR slip for 'großer'.","Square brackets around [Meier] and [*Absicht*] are mine, for the author and the term; 'that is' before 'aesthetic' is not at issue here. 'Unerweißlich' is rendered 'unprovable' and 'erweislich' 'demonstrable'."]],"poelitz-s27-019":["Judgments can be empty of sense, when one does not understand them, or empty of fruit, and that is the case with tautologies. All judgments serve to give us a distinct concept of the thing, and we never arrive at a distinct concept except through a judgment. That judgment is empty which cannot bring about a distinct concept. Hence tautologies are also excluded from philosophy. *Battologia* bears a resemblance to tautology; that is, the use of many useless words which, in themselves, nevertheless do not make the concept distinct.",["'Sinnleer' and 'fruchtleer' are rendered 'empty of sense' and 'empty of fruit'. 'Deutlich' is rendered 'distinct'."]],"poelitz-s27-020":["Of immediately certain judgments. These are such as are not capable of proofs; they are therefore elementary propositions. They are either analytic or synthetic: analytic, when I see the certainty through identity; synthetic, when I add something to the concept. In the analytic [judgment] I take something out of the concept. Immediately certain judgments *a priori* may be entitled principles [*Grundsätze*], because others can be proved from them, and because they cannot be subordinated to any other. Hence they are also entitled *principia* (beginnings). They are either *intuitiva*, which can be exhibited in intuition, and then they are entitled *axiomata*; or discursive, which can be expressed only through concepts, and then they are entitled *acroamata*. Remark. This distinction between *axiomata* and *acroamata* has never before been made in logic, except by Professor Kant. A postulate is a practical principle which represents a possible action, where it is presupposed that the manner of executing it is immediately certain. Where the manner is not immediately certain, that is entitled a problem. Modes of knowledge, in so far as they are given immediately *a posteriori*, are entitled experience.",["'Denn' in 'denn heißen sie' is read as 'dann' (then). 'Heißen' is rendered 'is entitled' throughout.","I divided the long sentence on the two classes of principles and supplied 'judgment' in brackets after 'analytic'."]],"poelitz-s27-021":["Of the parts of a system. — The parts are entitled members [*Glieder*], because they belong to a whole and are therefore determined. The members are either *principalia* or *minus principalia*. To the former belong definitions, axioms, acroams, *theoremata*, that is, a theoretical judgment which is capable of proof, and *problemata*, which are practical propositions in so far as they are demonstrable. To the theorem belong *thesis* and *demonstratio*. To the problem belong *quaestio*, which contains the proposition that is to be accomplished; *resolutio*, which contains the manner in which the proposition can be accomplished; and *demonstratio*, [showing] that if I proceed in this way, what was required will have been done. To the *membra minus principalia* belong *corollaria*, which are immediate consequences from one of the preceding propositions. Sometimes the author also calls that a corollary which cannot be proved by a single inference alone, because he believes that the reader will be able to run through the whole series of inferences as quickly as if it were a single one. — Theorems and corollaries differ in this, that the latter are inferred immediately, the former are drawn, through a series [of inferences], as consequences from immediate propositions. *Lemmata* are propositions which are not native to the science; for example, 'There is a God and another life' in morals. *Qualitas occulta* is the name of a force unknown in itself, in so far as it is used as a ground of explanation; this belongs properly to physics. — To a system there are added also *scholia*, which are not members but serve only to illustrate the system; for example, they relate when the proposition was discovered, was contested, and so forth.",["The first sentence lacks a full stop in the source before 'Die Glieder'; I supplied it. 'Bestimmt' is rendered 'determined', possibly with the sense 'destined'.","I supplied 'showing' and 'of inferences' in brackets; 'erstem' is read as 'erstern' (the former). The 'Autor' is Meier.","Stray quotation mark before 'wenn es ein einziger wäre' and the 'Systehm' slip silently corrected."]],"poelitz-s27-022":["Of inferences, *seu de consequentiis immediatis* [or, of immediate consequences].",["The bracketed gloss on the Latin is mine."]],"poelitz-s27-023":["The inference of one judgment from another is entitled an inference [*Schluss*]. Inferences are either mediate, when, besides the concept which a judgment contains in itself, still others are needed to derive a mode of knowledge from it; or immediate, when one proposition follows from another, the two differing only in form while the matters, that is, subject and predicate, remain the same. This is the *consequentia immediata*. One can also construct a *judicium intermedium*, but that is merely tautological; for example: All men are mortal; some men are men; therefore some [men] are mortal. — An immediate inference is also entitled an inference of the understanding [*Verstandesschluss*], and is distinguished from the *ratiocinium*, the inference of reason [*Vernunftschluss*]. In the latter there is always a further *judicium medium*, and the two judgments differ in matter as well. For example, if I say: All men are mortal, therefore Caius also is mortal, this is no *consequentia immediata*; for here I must have in addition a *judicium medium*: Caius is a man. *Consequentiae immediatae* occur:",["Long opening sentence divided into several; \"d. h.\" glosses kept as \"that is\".","\"sind\" supplied in brackets after \"einige\" in the tautological example: \"some [men] are mortal\" (text: \"einige sterblich\").","Schluss/Verstandesschluss/Vernunftschluss: \"inference\" and \"inference of the understanding/of reason\"; Latin technical terms left in italics."]],"poelitz-s27-024":["1.) *per judicia aequipollentia*, which, however, is really no inference. For example: Some men are virtuous, and: Not all men are virtuous, are one and the same. Here, then, no consequence takes place; there is only a substitution of words which indicate the same concept.",["\"Schluß\" and \"Folge\" rendered \"inference\" and \"consequence\"."]],"poelitz-s27-025":["2.) *per judicia subalternata*. A *judicium subalternatum* is a judgment in so far as it is contained under another, as, for example, particular under universal judgments; and the representation that the particular judgment follows from the universal is the *consequentia subalternata*. The two judgments are distinguished in respect of quantity; for example: All men are mortal, therefore some men are mortal. Thus *ab universali ad particularem valet consequentia*.",["The sentence \"Ein Judicium subalternatum ist. so fern …\" is slightly irregular in the German; smoothed with \"a judgment\" supplied."]],"poelitz-s27-026":["3.) *per judicia opposita*. Here the change concerns quality; for example: Some men are learned, therefore some men are not learned. The opposition is threefold:",["\"betritt\" read as *betrifft*; \"dieQualitaet\" is an OCR run-together.","Kant's example is as the transcript has it; it is not an inference in the strict sense."]],"poelitz-s27-027":["a.) *per judicia contradictorie opposita*, when in one the",["Fragment; breaks off, continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s27-028":["particular or universal is denied or affirmed, which in the",["Fragment of a sentence, translated as it stands."]],"poelitz-s27-029":["other is affirmed or denied universally or particularly.",[]],"poelitz-s27-030":["This is the genuine opposition, because it contains neither more nor less than is required for an opposition. They can",["Breaks off; continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s27-031":["[not] both be true, nor yet both false; and if one is true,",["The German reads \"beide wahr auch nicht falsch seyn\"; the sense (contradictories cannot both be true nor both false) requires a negative before \"wahr\", so \"[not]\" is supplied. Possible transcript or OCR fault."]],"poelitz-s27-032":["the other is false, and conversely. For if I affirm something universally, I say that of everything contained under a concept this or that can be posited. The other, who wishes to contradict me, need only say: In some cases there is an exception; then my proposition is already false; or if his is false, mine is true. Apagogic proofs are those in which I infer the truth of my proposition from the falsity of the *contradictorie oppositum*. These proofs are good, but one must take care in the sciences not to hold propositions to be *contradictorie opposita* which are not so. [b.)] *per judicia subcontrarie opposita*. When in one the",["\"[b.)]\" supplied in brackets: the transcript gives no letter before the second kind of opposition, which runs on directly in the text.","\"seiner\" read as *seiner* (his proposition); \"Apagogische\" rendered \"Apagogic\".","The passage breaks off mid-sentence; not completed."]],"poelitz-s27-033":["particularly is affirmed or denied what the other affirms or denies particularly; for example: Some men are learned,",["Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s27-034":["some men are not learned. Here there is no opposition",["\"kerne\" read as *keine*."]],"poelitz-s27-035":["in the strict sense; for in the second the same man is not denied of whom the first affirms it. They can",["Rendered with \"the same man\" for \"demselben Menschen\"; the German clause is compressed."]],"poelitz-s27-036":["both be true, but not both be false; for were they false,",[]],"poelitz-s27-037":["then the two propositions would have to be true: No man is learned,",[]],"poelitz-s27-038":["and: All men are learned. — But these are false; therefore",[]],"poelitz-s27-039":["by virtue of the contradictory opposition the propositions previously",[]],"poelitz-s27-040":["adduced must be true. Hence I can infer that, if",[]],"poelitz-s27-041":["the one is false, the other is true, but not conversely. c.) *per judicia contrarie opposita*. Both *judicia opposita*",["\"eme\" read as *eine* (OCR).","The line break joins the end of the previous sentence and the opening of c.)."]],"poelitz-s27-042":["are here universal, the one affirmative, the other negative.",[]],"poelitz-s27-043":["For example: All men are learned; all men are not learned. These can both be false, because one of them says more",["\"alle Menschen sind nicht gelehrt\" kept literally in the sense of the universal negative (no man is learned)."]],"poelitz-s27-044":["than is necessary for opposition, and that in which",[]],"poelitz-s27-045":["the falsity lies is superfluous; but they cannot both be true.",["\"stekt\" (= *steckt*) taken with the previous line: \"in which the falsity lies\"."]],"poelitz-s27-046":["4.) *per judicia conversa seu per conversionem*. This concerns relation. In conversion the quality of the judgments remains the same; in contraposition it is changed. I infer one judgment from",["\"con versa\" is an OCR break; read *conversa*.","\"Relation\" retained as in the transcript, though conversion would more usually be associated with the form of the judgment generally; flagged only as the text stands."]],"poelitz-s27-047":["the other *per conversionem* when I make the subject the predicate",[]],"poelitz-s27-048":["and conversely. In a conversion either the quality is changed [sic], and then the *conversum* is distinguished from the *convertens* in respect of",["The German reads \"Qualitaet\", but the sense of the clause, and what follows (\"der Quantitaet nach\"), require *quantity*; the transcript is probably at fault. Translated as it stands, marked [sic].","\"denn\" read as *dann* (then); \"convertente\" is the ablative of *convertens*, rendered as the Latin nominative."]],"poelitz-s27-049":["quantity, and the conversion is entitled *per accidens*;",[]],"poelitz-s27-050":["if it remains [unchanged], then it is *conversio simpliciter talis*. For example: All men are mortal, therefore some mortals are men; that is *per accidens*. No man is without sin, therefore nothing that is without sin is a man; that is *simpliciter talis*. Universal affirmative judgments can be converted only *per accidens*; for the subject is contained under the sphere of the predicate, since the latter must always be *latior* than the subject. The subject belongs, then, to what is contained under the predicate, but is not the only thing contained under it. If I now make the subject the predicate and the predicate the subject, then, since the sphere of the predicate cannot be smaller than that of the subject, nor the sphere of the subject greater [than that of the predicate], I must limit the subject. For example: All men are mortal. If I converted it and wished to say: Everything that is mortal is man, the subject would be *latior* than the predicate; I must therefore limit the subject and say: Some mortals are men. Universal negative judgments can be converted only *simpliciter*; for I remove the subject from the sphere of the predicate. If I convert it, I can therefore say: Everything that belongs under the sphere of the predicate does not belong under the sphere of the subject. For example, *omnes homines non sunt irrationales*: here I can say: Everything to which the predicate 'irrational' belongs does not belong under the concept of man, just as everything that belongs under the concept of man does not belong under the concept of the irrational. For two concepts that universally contradict each other contradict each other nevertheless in equal extent. I can therefore say: *omne irrationale non est homo*. All particular propositions can be converted *simpliciter*; for in the *judicium convertens* I had to limit the subject, otherwise it would have been *latior* than the predicate; in the *judicium conversum* the predicate becomes subject and the subject predicate; the present subject was therefore *latior* than the present predicate, and so must be limited. For example: Some men are learned; therefore some learned [persons] are men.",["\"unchanged\" supplied in brackets for \"wenn sie bleibt\" (the quantity); the subject of \"bleibt\" is read as the quantity, per the context.","\"nicht größer\" completed with \"[than that of the predicate]\" for sense; the German is elliptical.","\"honiines\" read as *homines*; \"m gleichem\" read as *in gleichem*; \"Sterbliche\" taken as \"mortals\".","The passage on particular propositions (\"im judicio convertente muste ich das Subject einschrenken\") is obscure and apparently confused in the transcript (it seems to treat the *conversum* as if it were the *convertens*); translated as it stands, without correction.","\"Sphäre\" and \"Umfang\" both rendered \"sphere\" / \"extent\" respectively; *latior* kept in Latin as a technical term (\"wider\")."]],"poelitz-s27-051":["5.) *Per judicia contraposita.* Here there takes place a *metathesis*, that is, a transposition of the *termini*, in which the quality is also changed: an affirmative proposition becomes a negative one. The quantity, however, remains, because only a universal proposition can be contraposed. For example: All men are mortal; what is not mortal is not a man. All universal affirmative judgments admit of contraposition. They say: where the subject is, there the predicate is also; and where the predicate is therefore not, there the subject also is not (and this is just the contraposition); for if the subject were, the predicate would also have to be. It can also be defined thus: *propositio A contraponitur, si praedicatum ejus mutetur in conceptum negativum, hic fiat subiectum novae propositionis, de quo subjectum prioris A universaliter in hac negatur.* In contraposition the change concerns only the modality; it signifies the necessity of an affirmative concept. All *consequentiae immediatae* have this character, that no subsumption can take place except one that is tautological; and that is not a true subsumption. Remark. Some universal affirmative judgments can also be converted *simpliciter*; but this follows not from their form but from the particular character of their matter. For example: Everything unchangeable is necessary, and everything necessary is unchangeable.",["The Latin definition is kept in Latin as Kant's technical formula; it is given as transcribed and left untranslated. The OCR forms 'hie' and 'subjeetum' are corrected to 'hic' and 'subjectum'.","The sentence 'es wird nehmlich ein negativer Saz Die Quantitaet aber bleibt' is garbled in the transcript; I have rendered it as 'an affirmative proposition becomes a negative one', supplying 'affirmative' [bejahender] from the sense of the argument, since the example turns an affirmative into a negative.","The sentence 'Sie bedeut die Noth wendigkeit eines bejahenden Begrifs' is obscure; rendered literally. 'Modalität' is the OCR-corrected reading of 'ModaHtaet'; one might expect 'Qualität', so the reading is doubtful.","'tavtologiseh' corrected to 'tautologisch'; 'eontraposition' to 'contraposition'. The sentence on subsumption was run together in the German and is divided in two in English.","'Versezung der terminorum' rendered 'transposition of the *termini*', keeping the Latin plural as a technical term."]],"poelitz-s28-001":["On Inferences of Reason.",["*Vernunftschlüsse* is rendered 'inferences of reason' (Latin *ratiocinia*), as distinct from immediate inferences of the understanding."]],"poelitz-s28-002":["An inference of reason is the truth of a judgment cognized in such a way that its subsumption stands under the condition of a universal rule. That is to say: what holds of the mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] is that it is a condition under which a universal rule affirms or denies something; if now the condition belongs to the mode of knowledge, then what the rule states belongs to it also. Caius is not identical with the concept of man. The rule is [this]: I must show that the concept of man is found in Caius, and then I can say that he is mortal. First, then, in every inference there is a universal rule, the *major propositio*. The proposition which subsumes a mode of knowledge under the condition of a rule is the *minor*. To subsume [*subsumiren*] means to make known that this or that is contained under the condition of this rule. The proposition which affirms or denies the predicate of the rule of the subsumed mode of knowledge is entitled the conclusion, *conclusio*. We have, then, two antecedent propositions, which are entitled the premisses. In an inference of reason the *conclusio* is given at once, as soon as the premisses and the *consequentia* are given. Logic therefore considers two premisses, their truth, and the correctness of the consequence from them. In rejecting an inference of reason one must never reject the conclusion first, but either the premisses or the consequence. The premisses constitute the matter, the consequence the form, of the inference of reason.",["The first sentence is tangled in the transcript; I have divided it and rendered 'Erkenntniß' by 'mode of knowledge' where it stands for a particular cognition, supplying 'this' in square brackets before the statement of the rule.","'Cajus ist mit dem Begrif des Menschen nicht einerley' is rendered literally; it is the example showing why a subsumption is needed. 'Caius' is the usual English spelling.","'Vorsäze' rendered 'antecedent propositions' (Latin *praemissae*); 'heist' rendered 'is entitled'.","The stray bullet before 'conclusio' in the OCR is dropped. 'consequenz' is given as *consequentia* in the first mention and 'consequence' thereafter, as it is the Latin equivalent Kant's usage suggests."]],"poelitz-s28-003":["Inferences cannot be divided in respect of quality [*ratione qualitatis*], because every major premiss is a rule and therefore universal; nor in respect of quantity, because it is all one whether the conclusion is affirmative or negative; nor in respect of modality, because every conclusion contains a necessity, and so all are apodeictic. In respect of relation they are divided into categorical inferences, in which the major is a categorical proposition; hypothetical, in which the major is a hypothetical proposition, [and] the minor either problematic or assertoric; and disjunctive, in which the major is a disjunctive proposition. The author entitles the categorical inferences 'regular' and the others 'irregular'. This is false, however: all three are perfectly correct acts of the understanding [*Verstandes Handlungen*], differing from one another. This error of the logicians arises from their believing that they have always to do only with categorical inferences. For that reason they also go wrong in their definition of propositions. — An inference of reason [*Vernunftschluss*] arises when a remote mark [*nota remota*] belongs to the subject by reason of an intermediate mark [*nota intermedia*], or is removed from it because it contradicts the intermediate mark. —",["The text reads 'oder ein problematischer, der minor ist aßertorisch'; I took 'ein problematischer' as a second possible kind of major (a problematic proposition) with the minor assertoric, and so supplied no word, only restructured; the sentence is irregular and the construction is doubtful. My bracketed 'and' is an addition, and the clause 'the minor either problematic or assertoric' in the translation is therefore a misreading risk: the German more probably means 'the major is a hypothetical proposition, or a problematic one; the minor is assertoric'.","Sentence divided and punctuated with semicolons to carry the three-fold classification; 'ordentliche'/'außerordentliche' rendered 'regular'/'irregular' as a choice (literally 'ordinary'/'extraordinary').","'Vernunftschluss' is rendered 'inference of reason', with the German given; 'Schluss' elsewhere is 'inference'. 'Verstandes Handlungen' is rendered 'acts of the understanding'.","'Der Autor' is presumably Meier, whose Auszug the lecture follows; not named in the passage.","'removirt' rendered 'is removed' (Latin *removere*, to deny of a subject); 'nota' rendered 'mark' following Kant's Latin parallel with Merkmal.","'Sie fehlen daher auch in der Definition der propositionen': the sense of 'fehlen' (go wrong) is taken from context; 'propositionen' is left in lower-case lowercase as in the OCR text and rendered 'propositions'."]],"poelitz-s28-004":["The predicate in the conclusion is entitled the *terminus major*, because it has a greater sphere than the subject. The subject is therefore entitled the *terminus minor*, and the *nota intermedia* is entitled the *terminus medius*. The logicians say: the first proposition is entitled the major, because the predicate of the conclusion, the *terminus major*, is contained in it; and the second premiss is entitled the minor, because the subject of the conclusion, the *terminus minor*, is contained in it. That the terms must be distributed in this way is easy to see. I connect the more remote mark (major) with a nearer one (*medius*); major and *medius* therefore occur in the *major* [premiss], because I want to predicate the major [term] of the subject in the conclusion, and to say that the subject is contained under it. In the *minor* [premiss] I connect the *nota intermedia* (*medius*) with the subject (minor); here, therefore, the *terminus medius* and the minor occur. In the conclusion I then say that the remote mark (major) belongs to the thing (minor); here major and minor come together. These designations must apply to all syllogisms, categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive alike. By this definition the hypothetical proposition, in which the major consists of two judgments, would have neither a major nor a minor. Hence these definitions [of the logicians] fall away, and ours are the correct ones. For if it had no minor and no major, it would not be an inference at all. In the categorical syllogism there are two concepts in the major, and so two terms occur there, major and *medius*. In the hypothetical syllogism there are two judgments, e.g. if God is just, he punishes the persistently wicked; here there are four terms. In the categorical syllogism there are always three concepts. The conclusion does not belong to the syllogism as a part, but as the consequence; for when the premisses are given, the conclusion is also given. The correct inference of the conclusion, or the correctness of the consequence, constitutes the form of the syllogism. Everything depends on the conclusion, and all syllogisms issue in it. Sometimes the conclusion is true, and the premisses also, and yet the inference, that is the consequence, is false; and such inferences must always be disputed. — An inference of reason is divided into true and false (*verum et falsum*). An inference can be false in respect of matter and of form (*ratione materiae et formae*). Every *ratiocinium* is either pure or hybrid (*vel purum vel hybridum*) when, besides the three propositions of the inference of reason, an immediate inference (*consequentia immediata*) is mixed in; e.g. no thinking being is composite — (nothing composite is a thinking being) — all bodies are composite, therefore no bodies are thinking beings. This is correct as regards content and consequence, and yet it is not a proper inference of reason. It belongs to a *ratiocinium*, therefore, that it be *purum*. We shall show below that all inferences of reason in the last three figures are *hybrida*, because a *consequentia immediata* lies tacitly in them. A formal *ratiocinium* is a syllogism (*Ratiocinium formale est Syllogismus*); it is otherwise said to be expressed in words (*verbis expressum*), but that is false, since without words one cannot think at all. In ordinary life we are always making inferences of reason, only not formally; if they are formal *ratiocinia*, they are entitled syllogisms. The author goes on to speak of the formal principles of all inferences, for the material [principle] is the rule; and he cites as such the principle of contradiction, which is indeed the principle of judgments but not of inferences. There are properly as many formal principles as there are species of inference. For the categorical inference the author gives the *dictum de omni et nullo*. We maintain, however, that it extends to all inferences, since we always infer from the universal to the particular. If the *dictum de omni et nullo* is taken thus, namely, *quidquid valet de genere et specie, valet quoque de omnibus sub isto genere et specie contentis* — *quidquid non valet de genere et specie* etc. *non valet* etc. — then there is a principle of categorical inferences of reason, since here there is a relation of subject and predicate. A proposition that is to become the principle of the possibility of inferences of reason cannot be proved by any inference of reason. The *dictum de omni et nullo*, however, can be proved, and indeed by the principle which we shall adduce. That of affirmative inferences runs: *nota notae est nota rei ipsius*, what belongs to the marks belongs to the things themselves. That of negative inferences: *repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi*, what does not belong to the mark does not belong to the thing either. E.g.",["'Termhii' read as 'Termini' and 'Schkißes' as 'Schlußes' (OCR slips), silently corrected.","Sentences beginning 'Der hypothetische Satz...' and 'Denn hätte er nicht...' are left in order; I supplied '[of the logicians]' to resolve 'diese Definitionen' against 'unsere'.","'major'/'minor' as premisses: bracketed '[premiss]' and '[term]' supplied to distinguish the premiss from the term; Kant's *in majori*/*in minori* rendered accordingly.","'Die Logiker... heiße' is subjunctive reporting; kept as indirect report. 'quid quid' normalised to *quidquid*; the Latin ellipses ('etc. etc.') are the transcript's own.","Long sentences were divided and some run-on constructions smoothed; 'Gattungen der Schlüße' rendered 'species of inference'. The 'Autor' is Meier. 'Praemisse' rendered 'premiss'."]],"poelitz-s28-005":["Nothing composite is a thinking being",["'zusaminengeseztes' is an OCR slip for 'zusammengesetztes'."]],"poelitz-s28-006":["All bodies are composite",[]],"poelitz-s28-007":["Therefore no body is a thinking being. Thinking being conflicts with the composite; the composite is a mark of body. Thinking *repugnat notae et rei ipsi*, that is, [it conflicts with the mark and with the thing itself,] with body.",["The bracketed gloss renders the Latin *repugnat notae et rei ipsi*, which I have kept in Latin as the technical formula and translated in brackets."]],"poelitz-s28-008":["Everything composite is divisible",[]],"poelitz-s28-009":["All bodies are composite,",[]],"poelitz-s28-010":["Therefore all bodies are divisible.",[]],"poelitz-s28-011":["Divisibility does not flow from body; it is a remote mark (*nota remota*). It must be ascribed to body through the intermediate [mark] (*intermedia*). Divisible is a mark of the composite, composite is a mark of body; hence here *nota notae est nota rei ipsius*, that is, of body. From this the *dictum de omni et nullo* is now derived. The genus and the species is, after all, a mark of the thing; it is the universal mark. *Quicquid competit generi seu speciei est nota notae, competit illis sub eo contentis*, that is, *nota rei ipsius*. These are the principles of all categorical inferences, and indeed the highest. In every hypothetical proposition there is, after all, an antecedent and a consequent, and the principle of hypothetical inferences is this: *a positione antecedentis ad positionem consequentis valet consequentia*; this is the principle of all affirmative inferences; and *a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis*; that is the principle of all negative inferences. This flows from the first, for if the antecedent were, the consequent too must be. In every disjunctive inference the principle is *a veritate unius membri divisionis ad remotionem ceterorum* (because they are all opposed) *et a remotione ceterorum ad positionem unius quod manet*. The position of the terms of a categorical syllogism in the premisses is a figure. In the first premiss the major term occurs, in the second the minor; but since every judgment must have two terms, the *terminus medius* is added in both. In the figures, therefore, everything will depend on the position of the *terminus medius*. It can be in one proposition in the place of subject (*loco subjecti*), in the other in the place of predicate (*loco praedicati*), or in both in the place of subject, or in both in the place of predicate:",["OCR corrected: 'speis cies' → 'species'; 'Ulis' → *illis*; 'vnius' → *unius*; 'lsten' → 'ersten'; 'terrninus' → 'terminus'.","'Positus' (nominative) rendered 'position'; 'Der Positus der terminorum' kept as one sentence. '[mark]' after 'intermediate' is supplied."]],"poelitz-s28-013":["E.g. No body is a thinking substance; *medius*, composite. The inference in the first figure is: Nothing composite is a thinking substance; All bodies are composite; No body is a thinking substance. E.g. No body is a thinking being; *medius*, composite. The inference in the second figure is: No thinking being is composite; All bodies are composite; No body is a thinking being. E.g. Some virtuous persons are imperfect; *medius*, they are human beings. The inference in the third figure is:",["OCR 'Kern Körper' read as 'Kein Körper'; 'zusamniengesezt', 'ziasammengesezt' corrected.","The syllogisms are run together in the transcript; I keep them as they stand, separated by semicolons. The first-figure example is internally inconsistent as printed (the first-figure major has the *medius* as subject), but the text is translated as it stands."]],"poelitz-s28-014":["All human beings are imperfect",[]],"poelitz-s28-015":["Some human beings are virtuous",[]],"poelitz-s28-016":["Some virtuous persons are imperfect.",[]],"poelitz-s28-017":["E.g. Something that has a power to act is a spirit; *medius*:",["The fragment breaks off here and continues in the next passage ('er eine Substanz ist'); the *medius* is not completed in this passage."]],"poelitz-s28-018":["[that] it is a substance. The inference in the fourth figure is this:",["'[that]' supplied; the sentence continues from the preceding fragment ('medius: [that] it is a substance')."]],"poelitz-s28-019":["Every spirit is a substance",[]],"poelitz-s28-020":["Every substance has the power to act",[]],"poelitz-s28-021":["Something that has a power to act is a spirit.",["Kant's text has 'Einiges' (some); 'Something' renders it in this particular proposition, i.e. 'Some things that have a power to act are spirits'."]],"poelitz-s28-022":["These figures rest on the fact that, by means of a *nota intermedia* [intermediate mark], we arrive at the character of the thing, under the rule: *nota notae est nota rei ipsius, et nota repugnans repugnat rei ipsi*. The major says that this mark belongs to this or that subject, and the conclusion says that the *nota notae* belongs to the thing itself, as has already been shown above.",["The sentence ends without a stop in the OCR; I have supplied one. 'Zukomme' is rendered 'belongs to'."]],"poelitz-s28-023":["If the *terminus medius* is to take the place of the *nota intermedia*, it must be the subject in the major premiss [*propositio major*] and the predicate in the minor. For in the major I see whether the *nota remota* belongs to the intermediate mark, and the intermediate must therefore be the subject. In the minor I compare the intermediate with the thing itself, and it must therefore be the predicate in the minor. In the second figure I have not proceeded from the *nota intermedia ad remotam*; the inference had to run otherwise. I say, according to the second figure:",["Restated as shorter sentences. 'Sondern der Schluß muste so seyn' is elliptical; I read it as 'the inference had to run otherwise', which the example that follows illustrates.","'Zukomme' is rendered 'belongs to'. The 'intermediate' in the last two sentences is the *nota intermedia*; I supply 'mark' in the first."]],"poelitz-s28-024":["No thinking being is composite; converted *simpliciter*: Nothing composite is a thinking being,",[]],"poelitz-s28-025":["All bodies are composite,",[]],"poelitz-s28-026":["No body is a thinking being. And so in the remaining figures. This difference of the figures rests on the intermixture of a *consequentia immediata* [immediate inference].",["OCR 'WTesen' corrected to 'Wesen'. The plural 'consequentiae immediatae' stands in the genitive in the German; I have rendered it as a singular, 'a *consequentia immediata*'."]],"poelitz-s28-027":["All four figures are entirely correct, only not logically perfect; they are not *ratiocinia pura* but *hybrida*. —",[]],"poelitz-s28-028":["A *modus* consists of the quantity and quality of the propositions of an inference, which are expressed by the vowels. Let us set down some: aaa, aae, aai, aao, aea, aee, eaa, etc., etc. Of such *modi* there are 4 classes, because there are 4 vowels; and in this way one obtains 64 *modi* that can be thought, 16 in each figure. Only 19 of them, however, agree with the general rules of inferences of reason, and among these only 10 are *utiles*.",["OCR 'vtiles' corrected to 'utiles'. 'Klaßen' is rendered 'classes'; the transcript's arithmetic (4 vowels, 64 modi) is left as it stands.","'Vernunftschlüße' is rendered 'inferences of reason'; 'Schluß' alone is 'inference'."]],"poelitz-s29-001":["Rules of categorical inferences.",[]],"poelitz-s29-002":["1.) The author says: in every regular syllogism there must be no more than 3 *termini*, for I am to connect 2 concepts by means of a *nota intermedia*. That lies in the definition of syllogisms. By regular syllogisms the author, like all others, understands the categorical syllogism. To this they wish to reduce everything, so that they may carry on their play of figures, which is not possible with the hypothetical and disjunctive syllogisms.",["OCR 'interlsmediam' corrected to 'intermediam'. 'Der Autor' is Meier, whose textbook Kant lectures on. The last sentence of the German is divided in two."]],"poelitz-s29-003":["2.) The *terminus medius* must not be in the conclusion. This oversight does not readily occur, because it is too obvious.",[]],"poelitz-s30-001":["Rules of inferences of reason.",[]],"poelitz-s30-002":["1.) *Ex puris negativis nihil sequitur*. For if everything is negative, the subsumption must nevertheless be affirmative, since it says that something stands under the condition of the rule. Or, if something was negative, there was no subsumption.",["The final clause keeps Kant's past tense ('war'), which seems to mean 'if it turned out to be negative, there was no subsumption'."]],"poelitz-s30-003":["2.) *Ex puris particularibus nihil sequitur*. Then again there was no rule, for every rule must be universal.",[]],"poelitz-s30-004":["3.) *Conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*. The *pars debilior* is the negative and the particular. If a proposition is negative or particular, the conclusion also is negative or particular. For if a proposition is particular, the subsumption also is particular; and if I say that only some things are contained under the rule, I can say in the conclusion also only that the predicate of the rule belongs to some. If the rule is negative, the conclusion also must be negative. For the rule says that of everything which stands under the condition of the rule this or that must be denied; the conclusion therefore must likewise deny the predicate, since the subsumption says that this thing stands under the condition of the rule.",["Restated as shorter sentences. 'Pars debilior' is glossed by Kant as the negative and particular; 'in der conclusion auch' is taken to mean that the conclusion can likewise assert only a particular predication."]],"poelitz-s30-005":["The rule of the first figure is: *major sit universalis*, because it is a rule; *minor affirmans*, because it is the subsumption. The first figure can have conclusions of every quantity and quality, but in all the other figures there are only conclusions of a certain kind. The rule of the second figure is: *major sit universalis, conclusio negativa*. The rule of the third figure is: *major sit universalis, conclusio particularis*. That of the fourth figure: *major sit universalis, conclusio* either *negativa* or *particularis*, never universally affirmative. These restrictions already indicate that in these inferences there are certain conditions under which they can be brought into the first figure. —",["OCR 'vniversalis' read as 'universalis', 'lsten' as 'ersten', and 'Emschrenkungen' as 'Einschränkungen'. The rules for the third and fourth figures are given as the transcript has them (they are not the standard scholastic rules, and the text may be corrupt or Kant's remarks abridged)."]],"poelitz-s30-006":["All logicians admit that the inference in the fourth figure is very unnatural, because 2 *consequentiae immediatae* must be intermixed. The 3 figures [*sic*] are thus only artifices for complicating something; yet they do infer correctly, and are *ratiocinia hybrida*. —",["'Die 3 Figuren' is given as the transcript has it; the sense is presumably the three figures other than the first. Possibly an error of the transcript."]],"poelitz-s30-007":["To make possible the reduction of the others to the first figure, the logicians have devised barbarous words, in which the vowels indicate quantity and quality, while the consonants serve partly euphony; at the same time, in all the figures except the first, they also indicate by what *metathesis* the reduction is possible. *S. vult simpliciter verti*: the proposition where S occurs must be converted *simpliciter*; *P. vero per accidens*; *M. transponi*, that is, the major must be made into the minor; C must be contraposed. The whole verse runs thus:",["The mnemonic words are the scholastic ones (Barbara, Celarent, and so on); 'metathesis' is Kant's own Greek term for the transposition. Kant's 'contraponirt' for C stands in the transcript, though the verse itself says *per impossibile duci*."]],"poelitz-s30-008":["*S vult simpliciter verti*",[]],"poelitz-s30-009":["*P vero per accid.*",[]],"poelitz-s30-010":["*M vult transponi*",[]],"poelitz-s30-011":["*C per impossibile duci.*",["OCR 'impoßibile' retained as the transcript's spelling."]],"poelitz-s30-012":["The verse in which the vowels indicate quantity and quality therefore runs:",[]],"poelitz-s30-013":["*Aßerit A negat E, sed universaliter ambo*",["OCR 'vniversaliter' read as 'universaliter'."]],"poelitz-s30-014":["*Aßerit I, negat O, sed particulariter ambo.* In the first figure are: bArbArA, cELArEnt, dArIi, fErIO. In the second figure: cEsArE, cAmEstrEs, fEstInO, bArOcO. In the third figure: dArAptI, fElAptOn, dIsAmIs, dAtIsI, bOcArdO, fErIsOn. In the fourth figure: cAlEmEs, bAmAlIp, dImAtIs, fEsApO, frEsIsOm.",["The OCR garbles the mnemonic words (e.g. 'dArll', 'fErlO', 'fEstlnO', 'dlmAtls', '0' for O); I have restored them to the standard forms with the capitalised vowels. 'bArOccO' is read as 'bArOcO'."]],"poelitz-s30-015":["There are 19 of them, and only so many are possible in the 4 figures. In reading one must always draw the consonants to the preceding vowel, e.g. cAm-Estr-Es. — C wants to be contraposed, M transposed; the minor is to be converted *simpliciter*, and the conclusion also. A hypothetical inference is one whose major is a hypothetical proposition. This has 2 members: an *antecedens*, which is the ground, and a *consequens*, which is the consequence. Here one infers *a positione antecedentis ad positionem consequentis*, which is the *modus ponens*, and *a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis*, which is the *modus tollens*. For example: if God is just, he punishes evil; *atqui*, the former is true, *ergo* the latter. Or: the soul is composite, therefore it is corporeal; *atqui falsum est posterius ergo prius*. In disjunctive inferences the major is a disjunctive proposition, and must therefore also have *membra disjunctionis*. By the *modus ponens* I infer: *unum membrum est verum igitur falsa cetera*; and by the *modus tollens*: *omnia membra sunt falsa igitur verum est unum quod manet*. For example: the soul is simple or composite; *atqui* it is simple, therefore not composite; or it is not composite, therefore it is simple. —",["The long sentence on hypothetical inference is divided into several. 'Vnum', 'vnum' read as 'unum'; the OCR 'tolle ntem' as 'tollentem'.","'Die Seele ist zusammengesezt, also ist sie körperlich' is given literally; it stands for the hypothetical major (if the soul is composite, it is corporeal)."]],"poelitz-s30-016":["The dilemma consists of a disjunctive hypothetical inference of reason. In every hypothetical inference of reason the major is a hypothetical proposition, but the *consequens* is a dilemma. It is a disjunctive judgment, for example: if this world is not the best, then God could not, or would not, create a better one. Now I refute the *consequens* through all its members. God could have created a better world, for he is almighty; he also would have, for he is infinitely good; therefore the world is not the best. Here the inference proceeds *a remotione consequentis ad negationem antecedentis*. The ancients made much of this form and entitled it *cornutum*. They knew how to drive their opponent into a corner by reciting every direction in which he could turn and refuting him in each. Whatever opinion he adopted, they showed him many difficulties in it. But it is a sophistical trick not to refute propositions directly but merely to point out difficulties. That can be done with many things, indeed with most. If I were to call false everything in which difficulties are found, it would be an easy game to reject everything. It is indeed good to show the impossibility of the opposite, but there is something deceptive here, because the inconceivability of the opposite is taken for its impossibility; and proving *apagogice* is always the sophist's trick. These dilemmas have much that is captious about them, even though their inference is correct. They can be used to defend true propositions, but also to attack true propositions by means of the difficulties that are raised against them. —",["The sentence 'das consequens aber ein Dilemma' is kept as it stands; the following sentence suggests the consequent is a disjunctive judgment, so the transcript may be loose or corrupt here.","Vernunftschluß = 'inference of reason'; Schluß = 'inference'; Schwürigkeiten (Schwierigkeiten) = 'difficulties'.","I divided the long sentence about the ancients into shorter ones; 'in die Enge treiben' is rendered 'drive into a corner'."]],"poelitz-s30-017":["Inferences of reason are further divided into *cryptica* and *formalia*. A concealed inference is one that contains everything as regards matter, but in which the form, although it is thought, is not properly expressed. This is also entitled inferring *per medios terminos*, for example: all bodies are divisible, because they are composed. No man in fact infers in secret otherwise than formally, for without this we could not infer at all, since otherwise there would be no connection. An enthymeme is such a concealed inference, in which one of the premisses is lacking, which one retains in thought. —",["'Vorsäzen' rendered 'premisses'; 'der Materie nach' as 'as regards matter'.","'ins geheim' rendered 'in secret'; the sense is that the formal structure is always present in thought."]],"poelitz-s30-018":["We have still two kinds of inference which do not properly belong to logic, but which cannot conveniently be placed elsewhere. The first is the inference *per inductionem*, in which we infer: what belongs to many things that belong to a genus belongs to all the remaining things that belong to this genus. This is not, strictly speaking, correctly inferred, for we cannot infer from many to all, though we can infer from all to many. But it must be done in this way, because otherwise we should have no universal judgments from experience. Properly, therefore, we should say: of what belongs to many things belonging to a genus, we can *praesumere* that it belongs to all the other things belonging to the same genus. But it must be carefully noted that this is only an empirical certainty, which is very different from the rational. The second kind of inference is that *per analogiam*: when two or more things of one genus agree in as many marks as I have been able to discover, I infer that they will agree also in all the other marks which I do not know. Without these two kinds of inference we could acquire no extended knowledge from experience, for without them we could have no universal propositions, and so could make no inferences. We might often be deceived here, yet we cannot avoid these inferences. We must, however, use them with great caution. — Inferences from examples are nothing at all, for example: men have sinned, therefore the angels also may well have sinned, because both come under the concept of rational beings. — There can be a connection among judgments which has the appearance (*species*) of an inference of reason, and yet the form can be false; this is then entitled *fallacia*. In so far as one thereby deceives oneself, it is a *paralogismus*; in so far as one deceives others intentionally, it is a *sophisma*. The ancients and the Stoics busied themselves greatly with the art of making such sophisms. Hence various kinds have arisen, for example the *sophisma figurae dictionis*, in which the *terminus medius* is taken in different meanings, for example: a philosopher [Weltweiser] is a kind of scholar; Leibniz is a philosopher; therefore he is a kind of scholar. *Fallacia a dicto secundum quid ad dictum simpliciter*: he who says you are an animal speaks the truth; but he who says you are an ass says you are an animal; therefore he who says you are an ass speaks the truth. In part he does speak the truth, because the ass has this in common with man, that it is an animal. Here one can answer: *concedo totum argumentum*; only one must make some restrictions. *Sophisma heterozeteseos*: when one does not answer the question one ought to answer, but another. *Sophisma ignorationis elenchi*: when one does not refute what one ought to refute, namely what the other means, but what one believes the other means. One thus refutes one's own fancy about the other's propositions. It is properly entitled a missing of the point at issue, for *elenchus* is the distinct representation of the content of a matter. The Megarian sect laid the greatest weight on this. Its founder was Euclid of Megara. One of these [sophisms] they called the Liar, from the middle term: If you say you are lying, you speak the truth in lying; but you say you are lying; therefore you speak the truth in lying.1) Its incorrectness is easily seen, namely something is omitted in the major, etc.: in that (you say) you lie. — The *Obullatus* was so called: Do you know your father? Yes. Now I present him to you veiled; do you still know him? No. Therefore you know your father, and also do not know him. *Acervus*, *Calvus*, *Cornutus*. What you have not lost, you still have; but you have not lost your horns; therefore you still have them. — A sorites is entitled a chain of inferences of reason which are subordinated. A chain of inferences in which the conclusion of one becomes the premiss of another is a *ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*. Here I can in two ways\n\n1) It runs thus: If you say you lie, and in saying this [speak] the truth",["Passage breaks off in mid-sentence ('Hier kann ich doppelt'), continued in the next passage; the footnote 1) likewise continues there.","Stray marks (•, ■) and a stray opening quotation mark were silently dropped. 'Vernnnftschhißes' read as Vernunftschlusses; 'figurac' as *figurae*; 'seeundum' as *secundum*; 'conceclo' as *concedo*; 'Emschrenkungen' as Einschränkungen; 'beantAvortet' as beantwortet.","'Obullatus' kept as printed; the traditional name is *Obvelatus* (the Veiled One), so this may be an OCR or transcript slip. 'Einen nannten sie den Lügner' is taken to mean one of the sophisms.","The sentence 'Wenn du sagst du lügst … die Wahrheit' in the footnote is incomplete in the German and is left so; 'Weltweiser' is given as 'philosopher [Weltweiser]'.","'Elenchus ist die deutliche Vorstellung des Inhalts einer Sache' is translated as it stands, though the definition is odd; I restated the Liar argument and the Obullatus argument as separate short sentences."]],"poelitz-s30-019":["speak the truth, then you speak the truth in lying — it should read: in that you",["Continues the footnote of the preceding passage and breaks off; the next passage completes it ('sagst du lügst').","'redest so redst' is a doubled verb form in the source; rendered as a single 'speak'."]],"poelitz-s30-020":["say you are lying; and then the inference would be correct. [I can infer] either downwards to the consequences, and these are then episyllogisms, or upwards to the grounds, and these are then prosyllogisms, namely when the premiss of the one becomes the conclusion of the other. For example: everything simple is indivisible; the soul is simple; therefore it is indivisible. Everything that thinks is simple; the soul thinks; therefore it is simple. Through episyllogisms I infer: everything simple is indivisible; the soul is simple; therefore, etc. Everything indivisible is unchangeable; but, etc.; therefore, etc. In disputations the opponent must infer through prosyllogisms. — If, from a series of episyllogisms, the major is omitted, it is a sorites. One here leaves out one of the premisses for the sake of brevity. If it consists of categorical propositions, it is a *sorites categoricus*. In the same way there are also hypothetical",["Opens by completing the footnote of the previous two passages ('indem du sagst du lügst'); the main sentence from 018 ('Hier kann ich doppelt … schließen') resumes after it, so I supplied '[I can infer]' to make the sentence run in English.","'denn' in the sense of 'dann' is rendered 'then'.","Breaks off in mid-sentence ('Es giebt … auch hypothetische'); not completed.","'Opponent' is kept as the disputation term; 'major' and 'premiss' follow Kant's usage (the omitted major is the 'Obersatz')."]],"poelitz-s30-021":["A *saltus in probando* [leap in proving] is the connection of one premiss with the conclusion where the other premiss has been omitted. It is *legitimus* [legitimate] if anyone can at once supply the missing premiss, *illegitimus* [illegitimate] if the subsumption, for example, is not clear. In the latter case I connect a *nota remota* [remote mark] with a thing without any *intermedia* [intermediate marks]. — A *petitio principii* is the assumption, as a ground of proof, of a proposition taken to be immediately certain, which nevertheless still requires proof. —",["Restated the first sentence as two, and supplied 'In the latter case' for 'hier' to make plain that the remark concerns the illegitimate leap.","Latin terms kept in italics with English in square brackets; 'Praemiße' rendered 'premiss' (British spelling).","'notam remotam' and 'intermedia' restored to nominative forms in the English gloss; the German construction (accusative after 'verknüpfe') is retained in the sense only."]],"poelitz-s30-022":["A *circulus in probando* [circle in proving] occurs when I make the proposition which I wished to prove the ground of its own proof. The circle is often hard to detect, and it is most frequent just where the proofs are difficult. A proof can prove too much, and also too little. It proves too much if it extends also to what is false. If it proves too little, it proves [only] a part of the truth, or a part of what is to be proved; it is therefore not to be rejected.",["Supplied 'only' in square brackets to bring out the sense of 'einen Teil der Wahrheit'.","Restated 'Ein Beweis kann zu viel auch zu wenig beweisen' as 'too much, and also too little' (the German is elliptical; 'auch' read as 'and also').","Stray space before the semicolon in the OCR silently removed."]],"poelitz-s30-023":["We shall now say something further about the *Topik* [topics], which Aristotle set in motion. If anyone wishes to reflect upon a subject, he must know to what science it belongs. In every science there are main divisions, in which this or that finds its proper place. These main divisions are entitled *loci* [places]. The regular division of all the themes of a science, under which each mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] receives its position, is entitled *Topik*. Such a true *Topik*, which however does not consist in the mere enumeration of themes, would be an incomparable guide to reflection in the sciences. For example, I can assign to virtue a political place, as a means of attaining happiness, but also a moral place. Place may also be defined as every determination [*Bestimmung*] of a mode of knowledge in a possible system; it is then entitled *locus scientificus*, and the art of indicating the place of the manifold modes of knowledge in a possible system is the *topica*. For example, if I wish to speak of philosophy, I think first of the etymology of the word, which is the *locus grammaticus*; then of the definition, which is the *locus logicus*. A *locus metaphysicus* is, for example, the heading of substance, of *nexus effectivus* [effective connection]. A *locus topicus* is therefore a tautology. —",["'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge' / 'mode of knowledge' (plural 'Erkenntnißen'), per the method.","Restated the long sentence on the true *Topik* with its parenthetical clause as two clauses for clarity; 'denn' ('then') read as 'dann', hence 'it is then entitled'.","'heißen/heist' rendered 'is entitled'. 'Tavtologie' normalised to 'tautology'.","'nexu effectivo' is an ablative in the original (probably 'under the title of ... the effective nexus'); the Latin case is a transcript peculiarity, and the sense is given in the gloss."]],"poelitz-s30-024":["We now come to the practical part of logic. It is to contain the rules for giving to every mode of knowledge the form of a science. It cannot specify the sources of knowledge, for it abstracts from all content; it has to do solely with the scholastic form of science. Science is a system of modes of knowledge in which the idea of the whole always lies at the basis and determines the division. We shall speak of the scholastic and the popular form of science, since there can be science without the scholastic form of system. The manner of connecting thoughts is either *Manier* [manner], that is, *modus aestheticus*, or method, that is, *modus logicus*. The former is also called popularity, and is a great art. But one will never attain to it unless one has command of method; without method it is mere rhapsody. Method concerns what is scientific in the thinking-through; manner concerns the presentation. To think without any method is entitled tumultuary. If one has thought according to a method but does not present it so, the result is rhapsodic. The former betrays little order in the head of the thinker. If the propositions are connected in order, yet in such a way that the transition from one proposition to another is not easily understood, the result is aphoristic. But if one has thought according to a method, has expressed it in the presentation, and the transition from one proposition to another is distinct, then the knowledge is systematic. —\n\nPopularity requires everything methodical, like scholastic perfection; only it requires that the scholastic arrangements and means to perfection be concealed, and this is what is called natural. The author [Meier] speaks also of the mathematical and the philosophical manner of teaching, because it was formerly the fashion to carry the mathematical manner of teaching into philosophy and vice versa. — To observe a method is something other than to present methodically. The *methodus syllogistica* is a presentation wholly by syllogisms, that is, *ratio formalis* [formal reasoning]. — Mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß Art*] is the manner in which one arrives at the connection of the manifold. — Style is the manner of communicating to others a manifold of modes of knowledge and of making it comprehensible. We need method for thinking, and style for presentation. The manner of presentation is important, but the manner of thinking more important still. Method belongs to logical, style to aesthetic perfection. *Wolredenheit* [correct speech] concerns language alone, with regard to grammatical rules; *Beredsamkeit* [eloquence] is the art of producing persuasion instead of conviction. Style in general is the manner of expressing something in conformity with the method of thinking, and to that extent logical perfection is promoted. All method could be divided into the popular and the scientific. If something is composed according to the latter, it is entitled methodical *κατ' ἐξοχήν* [pre-eminently], and this is of great advantage to the sciences. The popular method has the aim of furthering, not the sciences, but interest. The popular presentation is quite different from the popular method: the presentation can be popular while the method is scholastic. One need only begin, for example, with principles, definitions, and so forth, as Gottsched does, since he is so easy that even women can read and understand him. The French, who have advanced furthest in the popular method, begin at once with what is interesting. In the scholastic method I must begin from the first elements. The popular and scholastic methods therefore differ in kind, not in presentation; that [the latter] is a natural gift.",["The Greek in the OCR ('Korr 'e^oyjiv') is garbled; read as *κατ' ἐξοχήν* ('kat' exochen'). Doubtful but the sense is clear.","Restated several long sentences as shorter ones; the paragraph break after 'systematisch' is mine, marking the shift in the transcript.","'Das erstere verräth wenig Ordnung' follows the rhapsodic/tumultuary pair in the German; the antecedent is ambiguous and I have left it as 'the former', i.e. thinking without method (tumultuary), by the sense.","'Erkenntniß Art' is obscure (perhaps 'Erkenntnisart', mode of knowledge, or a slip for 'Methode'); translated as it stands, flagged as doubtful.","'Der Autor' taken to be Meier, the author of the textbook; '[Meier]' is supplied. 'Manier'/'Methode' kept distinct; 'Wolredenheit' kept in German with gloss.","The last sentence is elliptical: 'das' (that) is read as referring to the presentation (Vortrag), which is a natural gift; '[the latter]' is supplied by judgment.","'Gottsched ... da er doch so leicht ist': 'er' taken to refer to Gottsched (his book) as easy to read."]],"poelitz-s30-025":["Of the mode of teaching [*Lehrart*] in rational and in historical knowledge. The former rests on concepts, the latter on facts, so the method too must be different in each. Nevertheless history, if it is to be a science, must be a system, and as a system it requires a method resting on rules of reason. Every method must indeed be in accordance with reason, but *ratione* of content they are different. The method of rational knowledge is either scientific or scholastic, in which the form of science shines clearly forth, or popular, in which it does not shine forth but is covered, as it were, with a garment. — The scientific form is no longer to be found in philosophy, because it is too contrary to present taste. But the metaphysical part of science must be expounded scientifically. Method is further divided into the analytic and the synthetic. The first begins with the *principiata* and proceeds to the *principia*. The synthetic proceeds from the *principia* to the consequences, or from the simple to the composite. The analytic method might also be entitled the regressive, and the synthetic the progressive. In the popular method the analytic procedure is better; but the synthetic is the most perfect, and a system can be possible only through it. In the analytic method, it is said, we ascend. It is also otherwise entitled the method of invention. — The author distinguishes art and science. Art [*Kunst*] comes from *können* (to be able), science [*Wissenschaft*] from *wissen* (to know). I can know how to make something [without being able to do it], and conversely. There are many arts that are far removed from science, and it is not feasible to make them into sciences, for example painting and music. Art can again be divided into bread-art and free art. It is bread-art in so far as it is valued not on account of its inner worth but as a means of earning bread. Free arts are those that are valued on account of their inner worth; and if they are used as bread-arts they are degraded (*pretium internum est dignitas*), for example poetry and eloquence. The use of an art that is in itself free as a bread-art is, however, quite permissible. —",["Restated nothing at length, but 'Ich kann etwas wißen zu machen ohne es zu können' is awkward in the German; I read it as 'know how to make' and put 'without being able to do it' in brackets as the evident sense.","OCR slips corrected silently: 'Wißenschaf t', 'seyjj' (= seyn), doubled dash before 'Der Autor'.","Lehrart rendered 'mode of teaching', Methode 'method'; heißen/nennen rendered 'is entitled'.","The 'author' is Meier, whose Auszug the lectures follow."]],"poelitz-s30-026":["Art and Science",["Marginal heading as printed."]],"poelitz-s30-027":["Knowing and being able are different perfections. Practice belongs to art. A science is a system of modes of knowledge. Science is either rational knowledge or erudition [*Gelehrsamkeit*]. Erudition is itself a science of rational modes of knowledge, though it is not itself rational knowledge. Both, however, are distinguished from art. The man of fine wit [*schöner Geist*], the orator and the poet are not philosophers, not scholars, not mathematicians, but artists. See p. 28.",["OCR 'S}rstem' read as 'System'.","'vid. pag. 28' refers to the transcript's own pagination; kept as 'See p. 28'.","Erkenntnisse in the plural rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"poelitz-s31-001":["Of the Mode of Teaching",["Heading; *Lehrart* kept consistently as 'mode of teaching'."]],"poelitz-s31-002":["There is, further, a division of the mode of teaching into the artificial and the natural. But every mode of teaching is artificial, if 'artificial' is understood to mean what takes place according to principles previously considered. An 'artificial mode of teaching' is therefore a tautology. The natural can, however, be distinguished from the contrived [*gekünstelt*]. That is contrived in which an art is displayed that is contrary to the nature and the end of the knowledge; that is natural which is suited to the end. — The syllogistic method is one in which science is expounded in a chain of inferences. — The tabular method is that in which a system already completed is shown in its whole connection. The mode of teaching a mode of knowledge within a system is either acroamatic, when I alone teach, or erotematic, when I ask questions. The latter can again be divided into the dialogical or Socratic and the catechetical. In the Socratic or dialogical, teacher and learner both ask questions and answer each other in turn. It is presupposed of the one who asks that he does not know the matter but wishes to learn it from the one he asks. In the catechetical method the one who answers is the learner, so that it is just the reverse. If only the historical is in view, this mode of teaching is quite good; for then it is called *examen*, and is merely an inquiry whether the pupil has grasped well what is a matter of memory. The *modus catecheticus* is more a manner than a method, for it is only the way in which matters of memory can be questioned out, not the way in which rational modes of knowledge can be imparted; for that the dialogical method is the best. — In the latter too the instructor is a teacher, as in the catechetical, but it also appears that the pupil himself is a teacher. [Otherwise] the end is not attained, that the pupil's thoughts are developed; rather the teacher has put them in. Even in religious instruction, where rational modes of knowledge are the most important, the catechetical method is wholly unserviceable. For by repeating the questions and answers after the teacher the pupil gains no more light in religion; it becomes a work of memory, which he forgets in a short time. Here the proper dialogical or Socratic method must be observed. Socrates questions his pupils, and it appears as if he knew nothing at all. He calls himself the midwife of his pupils' thoughts, and says that he learns much from the fact that he questions others. And this is indeed so: by questioning one is led to much that one knows, and yet did not previously believe oneself to have in one's knowledge. This questioning is very artful [*künstlich*]. The pupil learns from the source of his own reason, and such modes of knowledge are indelible. —",["Divided the very long sentences; 'künstlich' rendered 'artificial' in the definition and 'artful' for Socratic questioning, 'gekünstelt' as 'contrived'.","'denn heist es examen' read as 'dann' (then); 'denn' is the transcript's spelling.","'Der Lehrende ist da auch Lehrer ... aber es scheint auch daß der Schüler selbst Lehrer ist' and the next sentence are obscure and probably garbled in the transcript; I supplied '[Otherwise]' for 'sonst' placement and translated literally.","'Tavtologie' read as tautology; acroamatic and erotematic kept as technical terms.","'sokratisch/dialogisch' kept both adjectives as Kant's synonyms."]],"poelitz-s31-003":["The author also speaks of the geographical and the chronological mode of teaching. The former sets forth the things of the world according to their connection in space, the latter according to their connection in time. The former must lie at the basis of the latter. One must distinguish *doctrina* and *disciplina* [doctrine and discipline]. Every instruction can be negative, that is, guard against errors, and then it is discipline; or positive, that is, extend knowledge, and then it is doctrine. — Criticism [*Kritik*] is what precedes before I expound modes of knowledge as doctrine or as discipline. It is the investigation of the sources from which knowledge springs; but that is for the teacher. The author says that science is demonstrated discipline. That is false: it can be doctrine, and it can be science without demonstration, as for example history. —",["OCR 'hegen' read as 'liegen'; 'seynd.h.' read as 'seyn, d. h.'.","'doctrin' and 'disciplin' left in the transcript's forms; Latin forms added in brackets as a supplied gloss.","'denn' = 'dann' (then). *Kritik* rendered 'criticism' here, as the context is methodological."]],"poelitz-s31-004":["To meditate [*meditiren*] does not mean to think something over [*überdenken*], for that is done tumultuously, but to reflect [*nachdenken*], and that is methodical. In meditating one must make preliminary investigations, write them down, and then bring them into order; this constitutes meditation. It must accompany all reading and learning. —",["'denn' = 'dann' (then) in 'und denn das in Ordnung bringen'."]],"poelitz-s31-005":["*Termini familiares*, or current expressions, which one believes one understands because of their frequent use, but about which it is nevertheless hard to give an explanation, for example fate, fortune, etc. A *Logodaedalus* is one who contrives new words in order to dispose of others with them instead of with things. He has his name from Daedalus, a celebrated builder in Crete. Artificial works are called *opera daedala*. — A *terminus vagus* is one that has no determinate meaning; [a *terminus*] *inanis* is one that has no meaning. —",["'Terminus ... inanis': the German reads 'inanis der keine Bedeutung hat'; I supplied '[a *terminus*]'.","Schiksal and Glük rendered 'fate' and 'fortune'."]],"poelitz-s31-006":["Of words. That does not belong in logic at all. If the question is how words are to be used in conformity with thoughts, a universal characteristic of languages would have to be made. It can only show the difference between *termini technici* and *termini vulgares*. Synonyms are two words that have one meaning. In a language, however, no completely synonymous words are to be found. Homonyms: when one word has different meanings. — We must not venture to use obsolete words again, or to introduce new ones, unless it were a proposal for a new idea for which there is as yet no word. A tropical [figurative] expression is a simile that one employs to express the thing, for example 'enlightenment'. — A *propositio indefinita* is one in which the sign of quantity is not added, and it is always regarded as universal. —",["'terminorum technicorum et vulgarium' given in the nominative as *termini technici* and *termini vulgares*, the genitive plural being governed by the German 'Unterschied'; this is a small normalisation.","'tropischer Ausdruk' rendered 'tropical [figurative] expression', bracket supplied; Erleuchtung can also be 'illumination'.","'Charakteristik' kept as 'characteristic' in the Leibnizian sense."]],"poelitz-s31-007":["As regards manner of writing [*Schreibart*], this also does not belong to logic. In philosophy one must not hunt after *lepores* [witticisms, graces]; the essential property is simplicity and precise appropriateness of expression. Barbarisms, and everything that offends against correctness of language, can be avoided. — Rational knowledge [*Vernunft Erkenntniß*] has nothing to do with style, except as regards purity. — Oratory [*Rede*] is aesthetic art applied to modes of knowledge which are at the same time to possess logical perfection. Here it is to be noted that fluency [*Beredheit*], that is, abundance of expressions, is one thing; eloquence [*Wolredenheit*] concerns style, speaking in a manner appropriate to the object; and rhetoric [*Beredsamkeit*] is the art of persuading someone, which properly ought not to be. We ought to convince, but not to persuade. In the didactic method *condescendenz* and *coascendenz* are to be observed. The teacher must regard himself as standing in the middle: he must rise to those who have greater capacities and higher ends, and stoop to others who lack these. It is a great art to keep to the middle course, and very few possess it. Those who cannot do so must apply themselves more to *coascendenz* than to *condescendenz*. In disputes there is always a *status controversiae*, and in it no *ignoratio elenchi* must occur. The best course would be to abolish all disputes, and, when two persons are at variance, for them to endeavour jointly to remove every misunderstanding and to investigate the truth. —",["The transcript reads 'alles Mißverständnis zu lieben'. I take this to be an error for 'heben' (to remove), and translate accordingly. The reading is conjectural.","The clause 'Beredheit, d. h. Menge der Ausdrücke, Wolredenheit betrift den Stil…' is loosely punctuated. I divided it into three members, *Beredheit*, *Wolredenheit* and *Beredsamkeit*. The English renderings are choices: fluency, eloquence, rhetoric.","*lepores* is left in Latin; the bracketed gloss is mine. *condescendenz* and *coascendenz* are kept as Kant's own forms; the second is an unusual coinage. *Erkenntniß* is rendered 'knowledge' and the plural 'modes of knowledge'.","I supplied 'is one thing', 'he must rise' and 'who lack these', and restated the long sentence on the teacher as several shorter ones. The German has only 'die dieses nicht haben'."]],"poelitz-s31-008":["Logomachy [*Logomachie*] is a dispute about words, in which people quarrel and in the end agree in the matter, and have differed only with regard to the expressions. Professor Kant admits no logomachies; there is always a difference in the things, only an imperceptible one. One can argue against persons and against things [*Sachen*]. The former belong only to personal dispute and do not hold for all men. — *Argumenta kat' anthropon* are those in which I adopt the opinion of my opponent, though not as true, in order to prove my own opinion from it. Properly all arguments should be *argumenta kat' alētheian*, in which I lay at the foundation something that I actually hold to be true. *Ad verecundiam*: when the opponent says, 'You surely will not be wiser than Leibniz, Wolff, etc., etc.' — One can retort another's attacks by the evil consequences which one draws from his opinions; this is entitled *argumentum ab odio*. —",["The OCR of the Greek phrases is badly garbled ('koct 'dvOpcoiTOV', 'Kcrr'aAr)6sioa;'). I restored them as κατ' ἄνθρωπον and κατ' ἀλήθειαν and give them in transliteration. This is conjectural from the sense. 'kerne' is read as 'keine'.","'wieder Personen und Sachen' is read as 'wider' (against), that is, arguing against persons or against things. The 'former' (*ersten*) are the arguments directed at persons.","I put the quoted words of the opponent in single quotation marks. The German has no marks. 'heist' is rendered 'is entitled'.","'Wolf' is rendered 'Wolff', the usual English spelling. The 'etc., etc.' is as in the source."]],"poelitz-s31-009":["On the composition of writings: this does not belong to logic. — One who makes a single book out of many is entitled a *compilator*, and he is useful, because there is often little knowledge [*Kenntniß*] in many books. One who makes a small book out of one is entitled an *epitomator*. It is best when the author himself epitomizes.",["'nüzlieh' is an OCR slip for 'nützlich', corrected silently. I treat 'Verfaßen' as the composition or writing of books.","*Kenntniß* is rendered 'knowledge' with the German in brackets, to keep it distinct from *Erkenntniß*, which is 'knowledge' in the fixed vocabulary. 'Information' would also be possible."]]};
